,"FlowID":"FL_135"}]},{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_153","SubSet":5,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_143","Description":"Item 1 (id 921)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_137","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_139","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Not only are you setting your daughter up for all sorts of problems, you're disrespecting the 12 years you had with the original dog. Was the dog not a valued member of your family that you can just replace her and pretend it's the same dog? And all of this because you don't know how to talk to your daughter about a tough subject? What's going to happen when you need to discuss sex or drugs? Or when a grandparent dies and she wonders why they didn't just \"de-age\" like the dog? And when she inevitably does find out the truth she won't trust you or believe what you say anymore. Grow up and be a d*** parent."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were trying to spare your daughter the pain of losing a dog she loved. But replacing the dog and telling her it \"de-aged\" is likely to create more confusion and hurt later. It also overlooks the 12 years you had with the original dog, who was a valued family member in her own right.\n\nChildren need age-appropriate, honest explanations of death, even when those conversations are difficult. Otherwise, when a grandparent dies or another hard subject comes up, she may reasonably wonder why they cannot \"de-age\" too. When she learns what happened, her trust in what you tell her may be damaged. Talk to her honestly now, apologize for the story, and help her grieve the dog she lost."},{"Description":"ord_921","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_921","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_140","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were trying to spare your daughter the pain of losing a dog she loved. But replacing the dog and telling her it \"de-aged\" is likely to create more confusion and hurt later. It also overlooks the 12 years you had with the original dog, who was a valued family member in her own right.\n\nChildren need age-appropriate, honest explanations of death, even when those conversations are difficult. Otherwise, when a grandparent dies or another hard subject comes up, she may reasonably wonder why they cannot \"de-age\" too. When she learns what happened, her trust in what you tell her may be damaged. Talk to her honestly now, apologize for the story, and help her grieve the dog she lost."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Not only are you setting your daughter up for all sorts of problems, you're disrespecting the 12 years you had with the original dog. Was the dog not a valued member of your family that you can just replace her and pretend it's the same dog? And all of this because you don't know how to talk to your daughter about a tough subject? What's going to happen when you need to discuss sex or drugs? Or when a grandparent dies and she wonders why they didn't just \"de-age\" like the dog? And when she inevitably does find out the truth she won't trust you or believe what you say anymore. Grow up and be a d*** parent."},{"Description":"ord_921","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_921","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Block","ID":"BL_6yE3F8l9hUikad7","FlowID":"FL_141","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_154","Description":"Item 2 (id 1084)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_155","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_156","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Jesus. You don't know why he took the pictures. Maybe he grew up there as a kid. Maybe he's an artist. Maybe he liked the flowers. Does it even matter? People walk past that house and look at it every f day. The house is on Google Street view. Or still on some real estate website. There's nothing private about the outside of a f house.\nAnd 'creepy'? Jesus. You think this guy's going to jerk off at home to some pictures of a house? You're the creep here."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that seeing a stranger photograph a nearby house made you concerned for the family, but you did not know why he was taking the pictures. He may have grown up there, been interested in the garden, or been taking reference photos as an artist. The exterior of a house is visible to everyone walking by, and it may already appear on Street View or real-estate listings. Publicly shaming him based on an assumption was an overreaction."},{"Description":"ord_1084","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1084","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_157","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that seeing a stranger photograph a nearby house made you concerned for the family, but you did not know why he was taking the pictures. He may have grown up there, been interested in the garden, or been taking reference photos as an artist. The exterior of a house is visible to everyone walking by, and it may already appear on Street View or real-estate listings. Publicly shaming him based on an assumption was an overreaction."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Jesus. You don't know why he took the pictures. Maybe he grew up there as a kid. Maybe he's an artist. Maybe he liked the flowers. Does it even matter? People walk past that house and look at it every f day. The house is on Google Street view. Or still on some real estate website. There's nothing private about the outside of a f house.\nAnd 'creepy'? Jesus. You think this guy's going to jerk off at home to some pictures of a house? You're the creep here."},{"Description":"ord_1084","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1084","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_bdCVDLSCxhWO8OF","FlowID":"FL_3","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_158","Description":"Item 3 (id 939)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_159","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_160","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're clearly in the wrong. If you and your wife had talked about telling your son of Santa's non-existence beforehand, that would be one thing. But you were a d and decided to go behind your wife's back and essentially ruin your child's innocence like a week before Christmas. What a horribly s** thing to do."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you wanted to be honest with your son and avoid deceiving him. But telling him Santa is not real was a parenting decision you and your wife needed to make together, especially when she had been participating in the tradition with him. At four, he was clearly enjoying the excitement of Christmas, and telling him alone shortly before the holiday took that experience away from him without your wife's input."},{"Description":"ord_939","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_939","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_161","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you wanted to be honest with your son and avoid deceiving him. But telling him Santa is not real was a parenting decision you and your wife needed to make together, especially when she had been participating in the tradition with him. At four, he was clearly enjoying the excitement of Christmas, and telling him alone shortly before the holiday took that experience away from him without your wife's input."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're clearly in the wrong. If you and your wife had talked about telling your son of Santa's non-existence beforehand, that would be one thing. But you were a d and decided to go behind your wife's back and essentially ruin your child's innocence like a week before Christmas. What a horribly s** thing to do."},{"Description":"ord_939","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_939","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_0PS224iJB49NtVb","FlowID":"FL_4","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_162","Description":"Item 4 (id 607)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_163","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_164","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Has he done anything wrong besides be in college at 27? You don't know what circumstances may have lead to him still being there. He could have not had the money to do it at a younger age.\n\nWhy is he a \"perverted loser\"? Has he even done anything perverted to deserve that?\n\nYou're in the wrong. Because you sound judgmental as f***. Tell us more about how successful you are, you amazing 22 year old."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you want better for your sister and are worried about the age gap, but you have not described anything he has done wrong beyond being 27 and still in college. There may be circumstances that led him to attend school later, including not having the money earlier.\n\nYou also have not said what makes him a \"perverted loser\" or whether he has actually done anything perverted. Calling him names and treating him as beneath your sister comes across as judgmental, not protective."},{"Description":"ord_607","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_607","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_165","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you want better for your sister and are worried about the age gap, but you have not described anything he has done wrong beyond being 27 and still in college. There may be circumstances that led him to attend school later, including not having the money earlier.\n\nYou also have not said what makes him a \"perverted loser\" or whether he has actually done anything perverted. Calling him names and treating him as beneath your sister comes across as judgmental, not protective."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Has he done anything wrong besides be in college at 27? You don't know what circumstances may have lead to him still being there. He could have not had the money to do it at a younger age.\n\nWhy is he a \"perverted loser\"? Has he even done anything perverted to deserve that?\n\nYou're in the wrong. Because you sound judgmental as f***. Tell us more about how successful you are, you amazing 22 year old."},{"Description":"ord_607","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_607","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_9vuvYfD4zFpvUHj","FlowID":"FL_5","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_166","Description":"Item 5 (id 3488)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_167","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_168","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nShop from home: they're just picking it up, quick in out. Reserved spot is like for curb side pick up.\n\nWith young children: It's a safety concern to have young kids running around a parking lot as their parents have lots of groceries and can't always hold onto them.\n\nPregnant woman: really dude? This is self-explanatory. You carry a fetus and see how much you like walking around.\n\nStop trying to be an edgy d***."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why reserved spots that often sit empty can feel like a cheap PR stunt. But they serve practical purposes: shop-from-home spaces support quick curbside pickup, and spaces for parents with young children can make it safer to manage groceries and children in a parking lot. Pregnancy can also make walking longer distances difficult. Using those spaces when you do not need them makes pickup and shopping harder for the people they are intended to help."},{"Description":"ord_3488","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3488","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_169","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why reserved spots that often sit empty can feel like a cheap PR stunt. But they serve practical purposes: shop-from-home spaces support quick curbside pickup, and spaces for parents with young children can make it safer to manage groceries and children in a parking lot. Pregnancy can also make walking longer distances difficult. Using those spaces when you do not need them makes pickup and shopping harder for the people they are intended to help."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nShop from home: they're just picking it up, quick in out. Reserved spot is like for curb side pick up.\n\nWith young children: It's a safety concern to have young kids running around a parking lot as their parents have lots of groceries and can't always hold onto them.\n\nPregnant woman: really dude? This is self-explanatory. You carry a fetus and see how much you like walking around.\n\nStop trying to be an edgy d***."},{"Description":"ord_3488","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3488","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_0MKJcn06rBoq6iN","FlowID":"FL_12","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_170","Description":"Item 6 (id 687)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_171","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_172","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nTaking about suspension of disbelief when you're being Space Marines?\n\nShe's researched her character and you dismissed it out of hand.\n\nSounds to me that you just don't want her to play. Do you think she'll spoil your fun? Is it just a testosterone fest when you play?\n\nAnd pink armour would be the same as playing a female character? Wtf?\n\nMaybe having a new character who is different could really make the whole thing more interesting and give it more levels.\n\nAt the end you said everyone is a sexless angel of death anyway, so are they all male or not? Make up your mind."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you want to keep the campaign faithful to the lore, but your cousin researched a way to adapt a Sister of Battle character and you dismissed it out of hand.\n\nPink armor is not the same as letting her play a female character. A different kind of character could also make the campaign more interesting and add new dimensions to the group.\n\nYou also describe Space Marines as sexless Angels of Death, so treating a female character as uniquely immersion-breaking seems inconsistent."},{"Description":"ord_687","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_687","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_173","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you want to keep the campaign faithful to the lore, but your cousin researched a way to adapt a Sister of Battle character and you dismissed it out of hand.\n\nPink armor is not the same as letting her play a female character. A different kind of character could also make the campaign more interesting and add new dimensions to the group.\n\nYou also describe Space Marines as sexless Angels of Death, so treating a female character as uniquely immersion-breaking seems inconsistent."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nTaking about suspension of disbelief when you're being Space Marines?\n\nShe's researched her character and you dismissed it out of hand.\n\nSounds to me that you just don't want her to play. Do you think she'll spoil your fun? Is it just a testosterone fest when you play?\n\nAnd pink armour would be the same as playing a female character? Wtf?\n\nMaybe having a new character who is different could really make the whole thing more interesting and give it more levels.\n\nAt the end you said everyone is a sexless angel of death anyway, so are they all male or not? Make up your mind."},{"Description":"ord_687","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_687","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_FGyfKApnfHNzTrH","FlowID":"FL_20","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_174","Description":"Item 7 (id 2276)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_175","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_176","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. For asking us this instead of her.\n\nMixing families is a delicate thing with a lot of potential resentment. From the (limited) information you give here it seems like you think she should be grateful to have a new family and stop being such a pain over it. Yah, it doesn't work that way.\n\nYou should be talking to her about this. Figuring out what she likes about the new family and what she doesn't. Maybe to her spending the holidays with just you was an amazingly special experience which you are now s******* all over. Maybe she feels like an outsider with this new family. Maybe it's just too big of a change to happen so quickly.\n\nListen to what you said here. You're saying spending time with your new family is nicer than spending time with just her. How do you think a 13 year old feels when you tell them that?"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why you view time with your new wife's family as a positive change after years of Christmas being just the two of you. But blending families can be delicate, and your daughter may be feeling resentment, like an outsider, or overwhelmed by how quickly things are changing.\n\nYou should talk with her rather than asking strangers to interpret it. Find out what she likes about the new family, what she dislikes, and what made the old Christmas tradition meaningful to her. At 13, she may hear \"this is nicer than spending Christmas with just you\" as you valuing the new family over the time you had together."},{"Description":"ord_2276","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2276","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_177","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why you view time with your new wife's family as a positive change after years of Christmas being just the two of you. But blending families can be delicate, and your daughter may be feeling resentment, like an outsider, or overwhelmed by how quickly things are changing.\n\nYou should talk with her rather than asking strangers to interpret it. Find out what she likes about the new family, what she dislikes, and what made the old Christmas tradition meaningful to her. At 13, she may hear \"this is nicer than spending Christmas with just you\" as you valuing the new family over the time you had together."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. For asking us this instead of her.\n\nMixing families is a delicate thing with a lot of potential resentment. From the (limited) information you give here it seems like you think she should be grateful to have a new family and stop being such a pain over it. Yah, it doesn't work that way.\n\nYou should be talking to her about this. Figuring out what she likes about the new family and what she doesn't. Maybe to her spending the holidays with just you was an amazingly special experience which you are now s******* all over. Maybe she feels like an outsider with this new family. Maybe it's just too big of a change to happen so quickly.\n\nListen to what you said here. You're saying spending time with your new family is nicer than spending time with just her. How do you think a 13 year old feels when you tell them that?"},{"Description":"ord_2276","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2276","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_wmHR5Cz3fNnDXM9","FlowID":"FL_21","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_178","Description":"Item 8 (id 1073)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_179","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_180","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. There are huge risks with surgery, like death. You are pressuring your wife to literally risk her life. Then of course there are the high risks - like, again, your wife dying, or your baby dying. She doesn't owe you her life, and she also doesn't owe you a high risk pregnancy that could result in a late-term miscarriage.\n\nThis is hugely f***** up bro, surely you recognize that? You can adopt, or have a surrogate if it must be biological, but please stop pressuring your wife to do this."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you both entered the marriage wanting biological children, and you feel hurt that the plan has changed. But this surgery and a subsequent high-risk pregnancy could put your wife's life at risk, as well as risking the baby and a late-term miscarriage. She does not owe you her life or a high-risk pregnancy.\n\nAdoption or surrogacy are options if having a child remains the goal, but please stop pressuring her to undergo surgery."},{"Description":"ord_1073","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1073","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_181","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you both entered the marriage wanting biological children, and you feel hurt that the plan has changed. But this surgery and a subsequent high-risk pregnancy could put your wife's life at risk, as well as risking the baby and a late-term miscarriage. She does not owe you her life or a high-risk pregnancy.\n\nAdoption or surrogacy are options if having a child remains the goal, but please stop pressuring her to undergo surgery."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. There are huge risks with surgery, like death. You are pressuring your wife to literally risk her life. Then of course there are the high risks - like, again, your wife dying, or your baby dying. She doesn't owe you her life, and she also doesn't owe you a high risk pregnancy that could result in a late-term miscarriage.\n\nThis is hugely f***** up bro, surely you recognize that? You can adopt, or have a surrogate if it must be biological, but please stop pressuring your wife to do this."},{"Description":"ord_1073","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1073","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_Tdv9K8yaj4cy0qm","FlowID":"FL_30","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_182","Description":"Item 9 (id 3804)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_183","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_184","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"God, you're in the wrong, don't do this. What if sister-in-law A goes on a tirade of how the gift wasn't good enough or it was the wrong color or the wrong size and now, suddenly, you've got the mystery of who put that gift there and SURPRISE you're dragged into the battle.\n\nGet sister-in-law A's kids some extra gifts, and label them from either yourself or from Santa. Balance out the presents given, or, take sister-in-law B's gifts for your children home for them to unwrap in private."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you're hoping a small anonymous gift might spare A's kids from being caught in the adults' conflict. But labeling it as from B could pull you directly into that conflict if A reacts badly to the gift or starts asking who left it.\n\nIf you want to make things feel more balanced, get A's kids a small extra gift from you or from Santa. You could also have your own kids open B's gifts privately at home."},{"Description":"ord_3804","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3804","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_185","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you're hoping a small anonymous gift might spare A's kids from being caught in the adults' conflict. But labeling it as from B could pull you directly into that conflict if A reacts badly to the gift or starts asking who left it.\n\nIf you want to make things feel more balanced, get A's kids a small extra gift from you or from Santa. You could also have your own kids open B's gifts privately at home."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"God, you're in the wrong, don't do this. What if sister-in-law A goes on a tirade of how the gift wasn't good enough or it was the wrong color or the wrong size and now, suddenly, you've got the mystery of who put that gift there and SURPRISE you're dragged into the battle.\n\nGet sister-in-law A's kids some extra gifts, and label them from either yourself or from Santa. Balance out the presents given, or, take sister-in-law B's gifts for your children home for them to unwrap in private."},{"Description":"ord_3804","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3804","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_aNUv8MtPBFto2XA","FlowID":"FL_31","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_186","Description":"Item 10 (id 2710)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_187","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_188","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\n\"My story doesn't begin here\". Ego much? Sorry to break it to you mate, but that is where your story begins.\n\nIf you don't want to date those girls, fine. But to do it cuz of where they live (or most precisely, where YOU live) is really the wrong thing to do."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you are eager to leave your hometown and see university as the start of a new chapter. But your story does begin where you grew up, and dismissing every girl from there solely for living in the same place as you is unfair. You never have to date anyone you do not want to date, but it would be better to judge people as individuals rather than treating your hometown as something they represent."},{"Description":"ord_2710","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2710","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_189","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you are eager to leave your hometown and see university as the start of a new chapter. But your story does begin where you grew up, and dismissing every girl from there solely for living in the same place as you is unfair. You never have to date anyone you do not want to date, but it would be better to judge people as individuals rather than treating your hometown as something they represent."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\n\"My story doesn't begin here\". Ego much? Sorry to break it to you mate, but that is where your story begins.\n\nIf you don't want to date those girls, fine. But to do it cuz of where they live (or most precisely, where YOU live) is really the wrong thing to do."},{"Description":"ord_2710","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2710","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_bkYrR1j7cOV423E","FlowID":"FL_32","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_190","Description":"Item 11 (id 1672)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_191","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_192","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. The fact that you're holding your own biological child to a different standard than your stepdaughter is unfair. Even if she hasn't warmed up to you as a father figure, that's a role you decided to put yourself in by marrying her mother. You should want to help her because she's your stepdaughter, and will be your stepdaughter for the rest of your life.\n\nAlso, the fact that she doesn't make her own income and you're refusing to let her use your mutual funds to assist her daughter just because her daughter doesn't see you as her father is another wrong move. At the very least you should be willing to do this for your wife's sake."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you see the $800 as a significant expense and believe your wife and her ex should handle it. But treating your biological child differently from your stepdaughter is unfair. By marrying her mother, you took on a stepfather role, even if she does not see you as a father figure.\n\nYour wife also does not have her own income, so refusing to let her use shared funds to help her daughter leaves her without meaningful discretion over the household money. At minimum, helping with the deposit would be a way to support your wife."},{"Description":"ord_1672","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1672","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_193","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you see the $800 as a significant expense and believe your wife and her ex should handle it. But treating your biological child differently from your stepdaughter is unfair. By marrying her mother, you took on a stepfather role, even if she does not see you as a father figure.\n\nYour wife also does not have her own income, so refusing to let her use shared funds to help her daughter leaves her without meaningful discretion over the household money. At minimum, helping with the deposit would be a way to support your wife."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. The fact that you're holding your own biological child to a different standard than your stepdaughter is unfair. Even if she hasn't warmed up to you as a father figure, that's a role you decided to put yourself in by marrying her mother. You should want to help her because she's your stepdaughter, and will be your stepdaughter for the rest of your life.\n\nAlso, the fact that she doesn't make her own income and you're refusing to let her use your mutual funds to assist her daughter just because her daughter doesn't see you as her father is another wrong move. At the very least you should be willing to do this for your wife's sake."},{"Description":"ord_1672","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1672","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_AmKYW23QoXywdgK","FlowID":"FL_33","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_194","Description":"Item 12 (id 2937)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_195","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_196","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. And it's lame to imply your wife is somehow neglecting her 6 year old son. Surely the 6 year old is sleeping. Along those lines, your wife and her friend are trying to least impact their kids by meeting when the kids are sleeping and don't need anything. You're simply jealous your wife is going out and instead of discussing that, you're throwing shade about something that's not relevant."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were concerned about the late hour and your wife's past injury, but meeting after the kids are asleep may be the way she and her friend can spend time together with the least impact on their children. A six-year-old is likely sleeping, not being neglected. This reads more like jealousy or discomfort with her going out, and that would be better discussed directly than framing her plans as failing to prioritize her child."},{"Description":"ord_2937","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2937","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_197","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were concerned about the late hour and your wife's past injury, but meeting after the kids are asleep may be the way she and her friend can spend time together with the least impact on their children. A six-year-old is likely sleeping, not being neglected. This reads more like jealousy or discomfort with her going out, and that would be better discussed directly than framing her plans as failing to prioritize her child."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. And it's lame to imply your wife is somehow neglecting her 6 year old son. Surely the 6 year old is sleeping. Along those lines, your wife and her friend are trying to least impact their kids by meeting when the kids are sleeping and don't need anything. You're simply jealous your wife is going out and instead of discussing that, you're throwing shade about something that's not relevant."},{"Description":"ord_2937","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2937","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_Ao8qsqAwovGXTFi","FlowID":"FL_34","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_198","Description":"Item 13 (id 787)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_199","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_200","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nIf you're working equal hours, you share equal responsibility in the home. Because it's your home, and your life. Your income does not enable you to treat your wife as an employee because you \"fund\" most of your life together. INCLUDING the care of your child. Talking about your child like your wife should be grateful your money allows her to provide for him. If you weren't married to your wife, that child would still be YOUR responsibility to clothe, feed and protect.\n\nIf you're really making sooooo much more money, get a maid. Jiminy cricket."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you see your higher income as a larger financial contribution and feel it should be balanced elsewhere. But if you are working equal hours, you should share equal responsibility at home. It is your home and your child too; your income does not make your wife an employee or make childcare less your responsibility.\n\nYour child would still be your responsibility to feed, clothe, and protect if you were not married. If your income leaves room in the budget, hiring a cleaner could help reduce the workload for both of you."},{"Description":"ord_787","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_787","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_201","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you see your higher income as a larger financial contribution and feel it should be balanced elsewhere. But if you are working equal hours, you should share equal responsibility at home. It is your home and your child too; your income does not make your wife an employee or make childcare less your responsibility.\n\nYour child would still be your responsibility to feed, clothe, and protect if you were not married. If your income leaves room in the budget, hiring a cleaner could help reduce the workload for both of you."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nIf you're working equal hours, you share equal responsibility in the home. Because it's your home, and your life. Your income does not enable you to treat your wife as an employee because you \"fund\" most of your life together. INCLUDING the care of your child. Talking about your child like your wife should be grateful your money allows her to provide for him. If you weren't married to your wife, that child would still be YOUR responsibility to clothe, feed and protect.\n\nIf you're really making sooooo much more money, get a maid. Jiminy cricket."},{"Description":"ord_787","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_787","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_e4DJ1weVXMUfEKY","FlowID":"FL_35","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_202","Description":"Item 14 (id 3920)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_203","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_204","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Kids look forward to summer because they bust their a** all year to get good grades and please their parents. They get 2 months to relax and be a kid. You're robbing your kid of their childhood, if his dream pans out great if not then he'll have other opportunities but would you rather him look back in 10 years and say he remembered the summer he played games with his friends and had fun or slaved over more schoolwork and stressed."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why you want to protect his future and help him maintain strong grades. But he already has straight Bs and spends the school year meeting expectations. Summer gives kids a chance to rest, have fun with friends, and explore interests like gaming. His dream may or may not pan out, but he will still have other opportunities. In ten years, he is more likely to value remembering a summer spent enjoying himself than one spent overwhelmed by extra schoolwork and stress."},{"Description":"ord_3920","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3920","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_205","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why you want to protect his future and help him maintain strong grades. But he already has straight Bs and spends the school year meeting expectations. Summer gives kids a chance to rest, have fun with friends, and explore interests like gaming. His dream may or may not pan out, but he will still have other opportunities. In ten years, he is more likely to value remembering a summer spent enjoying himself than one spent overwhelmed by extra schoolwork and stress."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Kids look forward to summer because they bust their a** all year to get good grades and please their parents. They get 2 months to relax and be a kid. You're robbing your kid of their childhood, if his dream pans out great if not then he'll have other opportunities but would you rather him look back in 10 years and say he remembered the summer he played games with his friends and had fun or slaved over more schoolwork and stressed."},{"Description":"ord_3920","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3920","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_QCQUB3JhIGz1rVA","FlowID":"FL_36","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_206","Description":"Item 15 (id 2291)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_207","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_208","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think this is different for everyone. Personally as a dog owner, I don't mind if someone throws their dog's poop in my trash can IF it's full. But if it's already been emptied then h no.\n\nBut if I wasn't a dog owner and someone put their dog's poop in my bin? I'd be pretty p**. I wouldn't want my plastic bin to absorb any of that odor. Maybe that's paranoid... but it's my bin. Keep your poop out of it.\n\nYou're in the wrong."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you bag the poop and are trying not to leave it in anyone's yard. Still, people have different boundaries about their bins. A full bin may feel less intrusive to some dog owners, but putting a bag in an emptied bin can leave odor behind. For people who do not own dogs, that can be especially frustrating. It is their bin, so keep the bag with you until you reach a public trash can or your own."},{"Description":"ord_2291","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2291","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_209","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you bag the poop and are trying not to leave it in anyone's yard. Still, people have different boundaries about their bins. A full bin may feel less intrusive to some dog owners, but putting a bag in an emptied bin can leave odor behind. For people who do not own dogs, that can be especially frustrating. It is their bin, so keep the bag with you until you reach a public trash can or your own."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think this is different for everyone. Personally as a dog owner, I don't mind if someone throws their dog's poop in my trash can IF it's full. But if it's already been emptied then h no.\n\nBut if I wasn't a dog owner and someone put their dog's poop in my bin? I'd be pretty p**. I wouldn't want my plastic bin to absorb any of that odor. Maybe that's paranoid... but it's my bin. Keep your poop out of it.\n\nYou're in the wrong."},{"Description":"ord_2291","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2291","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_oB4s9Knh9mKumeA","FlowID":"FL_37","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_210","Description":"Item 16 (id 1030)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_211","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_212","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Since this is a common occurrence (don't know what job you do), just COMPLETELY shut off your phone. Your company isn't going to die if you ignore stuff on your given days off.\n\nI'm sorry, but this would definitely be the wrong thing to do. I understand all the conditions of making sure no one is around and whatever, but it's still a jerk thing. I wouldn't want to hear constant buzzing from a phone since the volume is turned low\/off. Also, stop catering to your impatient coworkers."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that unanswered work messages make it hard for you to relax during a movie, and you're trying to minimize the impact by sitting in the back with your phone dimmed and silent. But a lit screen can still distract people in a dark theater, and any buzzing may be audible too. If this is a common issue, fully shut your phone off for the movie and stop catering to coworkers who expect immediate responses on your days off."},{"Description":"ord_1030","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1030","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_213","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that unanswered work messages make it hard for you to relax during a movie, and you're trying to minimize the impact by sitting in the back with your phone dimmed and silent. But a lit screen can still distract people in a dark theater, and any buzzing may be audible too. If this is a common issue, fully shut your phone off for the movie and stop catering to coworkers who expect immediate responses on your days off."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Since this is a common occurrence (don't know what job you do), just COMPLETELY shut off your phone. Your company isn't going to die if you ignore stuff on your given days off.\n\nI'm sorry, but this would definitely be the wrong thing to do. I understand all the conditions of making sure no one is around and whatever, but it's still a jerk thing. I wouldn't want to hear constant buzzing from a phone since the volume is turned low\/off. Also, stop catering to your impatient coworkers."},{"Description":"ord_1030","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1030","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_dXavEPquqU0vBHV","FlowID":"FL_38","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_214","Description":"Item 17 (id 246)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_215","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_216","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. 100% wrong. I didn't even have to read the post to already know you calling someone the r-word makes you in the wrong. But reading the post solidified it. Have you ever tried to do things with your non-dominate hand? It's hard, but he's practicing, and you shouldn't knock him for that. Your comment was completely rude and unnecessary, especially because he apologized.\n\nSide note: the fact you take pride in being left handed because it's \"unique\" is also a little sad. Makes it seem your little outburst is because you want it to stay that way."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you were frustrated after hot soup splashed on you, especially when he already has good chopstick skills with his right hand. But he was practicing with his non-dominant hand, and that takes time. He apologized, and calling him the r-word was rude and unnecessary.\n\nBeing left-handed can be something you enjoy about yourself, but your cousin learning to use his left hand does not take that away from you."},{"Description":"ord_246","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_246","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_217","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you were frustrated after hot soup splashed on you, especially when he already has good chopstick skills with his right hand. But he was practicing with his non-dominant hand, and that takes time. He apologized, and calling him the r-word was rude and unnecessary.\n\nBeing left-handed can be something you enjoy about yourself, but your cousin learning to use his left hand does not take that away from you."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. 100% wrong. I didn't even have to read the post to already know you calling someone the r-word makes you in the wrong. But reading the post solidified it. Have you ever tried to do things with your non-dominate hand? It's hard, but he's practicing, and you shouldn't knock him for that. Your comment was completely rude and unnecessary, especially because he apologized.\n\nSide note: the fact you take pride in being left handed because it's \"unique\" is also a little sad. Makes it seem your little outburst is because you want it to stay that way."},{"Description":"ord_246","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_246","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_G2wdqg1DPwDdFi1","FlowID":"FL_39","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_218","Description":"Item 18 (id 1317)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_219","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_220","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nFuel is only a very small portion of the cost of operating a vehicle. Even if he started with an empty tank and ended with a full one, your buddy was absorbing most of the travel cost himself by supplying the vehicle that was used.\n\nJust picking some numbers out of the air... If gas cost $2 a gallon at the time, and the vehicle got 20 miles to the gallon, that's only $0.10 a mile in fuel. Operating cost for a vehicle can easily be 50 or 60 cents a mile."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you felt the driver should cover the first tank since it was empty before the trip began. But fuel is often only a small part of the cost of using a vehicle. By providing the 8-seater, your friend was already taking on wear, maintenance, depreciation, and other operating costs for the group.\n\nFor example, at $2 per gallon and 20 mpg, gas is about 10 cents per mile, while total vehicle operating costs can easily be 50-60 cents per mile. Splitting the fuel from the start and again at the end was a fair way to share some of the cost of the trip."},{"Description":"ord_1317","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1317","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_221","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you felt the driver should cover the first tank since it was empty before the trip began. But fuel is often only a small part of the cost of using a vehicle. By providing the 8-seater, your friend was already taking on wear, maintenance, depreciation, and other operating costs for the group.\n\nFor example, at $2 per gallon and 20 mpg, gas is about 10 cents per mile, while total vehicle operating costs can easily be 50-60 cents per mile. Splitting the fuel from the start and again at the end was a fair way to share some of the cost of the trip."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nFuel is only a very small portion of the cost of operating a vehicle. Even if he started with an empty tank and ended with a full one, your buddy was absorbing most of the travel cost himself by supplying the vehicle that was used.\n\nJust picking some numbers out of the air... If gas cost $2 a gallon at the time, and the vehicle got 20 miles to the gallon, that's only $0.10 a mile in fuel. Operating cost for a vehicle can easily be 50 or 60 cents a mile."},{"Description":"ord_1317","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1317","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_txJnyhcu1fSQb4B","FlowID":"FL_40","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_222","Description":"Item 19 (id 24)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_223","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_224","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Unless he's dropping out of school or doing something else that will hurt him in the long term to pursue it. He's got a dream. It'll probably come to nothing except a couple of embarrassing videos that you'll be giving him s about for the next 60 years, but s**** on people's dreams is the wrong thing to do."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you want him to have a stable future and not struggle financially. But he is 13 and has a dream; unless pursuing YouTube means dropping out of school or otherwise harming his long-term future, he should have room to try it. It may amount to a few embarrassing videos, but discouraging him from the outset is not helpful. Encourage him to keep up with school and develop practical backup plans while he explores it."},{"Description":"ord_24","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_24","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_225","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you want him to have a stable future and not struggle financially. But he is 13 and has a dream; unless pursuing YouTube means dropping out of school or otherwise harming his long-term future, he should have room to try it. It may amount to a few embarrassing videos, but discouraging him from the outset is not helpful. Encourage him to keep up with school and develop practical backup plans while he explores it."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Unless he's dropping out of school or doing something else that will hurt him in the long term to pursue it. He's got a dream. It'll probably come to nothing except a couple of embarrassing videos that you'll be giving him s about for the next 60 years, but s**** on people's dreams is the wrong thing to do."},{"Description":"ord_24","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_24","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_KusI7s3IxAfRR9h","FlowID":"FL_41","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_226","Description":"Item 20 (id 2872)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_227","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_228","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Tbh. You wanted a rational conversation, but all you did was just tell her no, it's b*******, I do not spend, I do not, you're tearing me apart Lisa. There are ways to have a lovely wedding for a decent price, you can budget where you can, there are tips and tricks you can research. As for the ring, yeah, it's important. You need to talk to her instead of just telling her off and then basically telling her you do not care about your wedding day. Also, if you wanted a rational conversation why did ypu just say 'yeah' when she told youhow important it is to her?"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you see wedding culture as a money pit and want to avoid draining your savings, which is a reasonable concern. But you mostly responded by telling her what she would not get instead of discussing a budget and what matters to each of you.\n\nThere are ways to have a lovely wedding at a decent price, with budgeting and research into cost-saving options. The ring matters to her, too. Talk with her about your shared priorities rather than dismissing her hopes or giving a flat \"yeah\" when she tells you how important this is to her."},{"Description":"ord_2872","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2872","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_229","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you see wedding culture as a money pit and want to avoid draining your savings, which is a reasonable concern. But you mostly responded by telling her what she would not get instead of discussing a budget and what matters to each of you.\n\nThere are ways to have a lovely wedding at a decent price, with budgeting and research into cost-saving options. The ring matters to her, too. Talk with her about your shared priorities rather than dismissing her hopes or giving a flat \"yeah\" when she tells you how important this is to her."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Tbh. You wanted a rational conversation, but all you did was just tell her no, it's b*******, I do not spend, I do not, you're tearing me apart Lisa. There are ways to have a lovely wedding for a decent price, you can budget where you can, there are tips and tricks you can research. As for the ring, yeah, it's important. You need to talk to her instead of just telling her off and then basically telling her you do not care about your wedding day. Also, if you wanted a rational conversation why did ypu just say 'yeah' when she told youhow important it is to her?"},{"Description":"ord_2872","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2872","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_k0va7GAiv4JBbBb","FlowID":"FL_42","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_230","Description":"Item 21 (id 3532)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_231","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_232","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"\"Politics and religion have nothing to do at the dinner table\"\n\nYour mother is the one who brought up religion at the dinner table. Your father is the one who brought up politics at the dinner table. Why are you telling gf not to share? Why not your parents? Just kidding, the answer is obvious: You are afraid of your parents' disapproval, and you don't really care about her disapproval."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you wanted the dinner to stay calm and avoid conflict with your parents. But your mother brought up religion, and your father brought up politics. Your girlfriend responded to topics they introduced.\n\nIt seems like you expect her to stay quiet to protect your parents from disagreement, while not asking them to extend her the same courtesy. A respectful conversation can include differing views. You should address this with your parents too, rather than placing the responsibility entirely on her."},{"Description":"ord_3532","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3532","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_233","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you wanted the dinner to stay calm and avoid conflict with your parents. But your mother brought up religion, and your father brought up politics. Your girlfriend responded to topics they introduced.\n\nIt seems like you expect her to stay quiet to protect your parents from disagreement, while not asking them to extend her the same courtesy. A respectful conversation can include differing views. You should address this with your parents too, rather than placing the responsibility entirely on her."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"\"Politics and religion have nothing to do at the dinner table\"\n\nYour mother is the one who brought up religion at the dinner table. Your father is the one who brought up politics at the dinner table. Why are you telling gf not to share? Why not your parents? Just kidding, the answer is obvious: You are afraid of your parents' disapproval, and you don't really care about her disapproval."},{"Description":"ord_3532","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3532","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_GpXNuZEF1557ufI","FlowID":"FL_52","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_234","Description":"Item 22 (id 3231)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_235","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_236","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong for running a red light. You're in the wrong for doubling down on justifying it. You're in the wrong for mocking the guy who rightfully called you out for it.\n\nI spent years cycling as my main transpo form and it's really not that f****** hard to wait at a red light."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you saw an empty intersection and felt waiting at the red light was pointless. Still, you ran a red light, doubled down on defending it, and mocked someone who called you out. Having cycled as my main transportation for years, I think waiting for the light is a basic part of riding responsibly."},{"Description":"ord_3231","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3231","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_237","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you saw an empty intersection and felt waiting at the red light was pointless. Still, you ran a red light, doubled down on defending it, and mocked someone who called you out. Having cycled as my main transportation for years, I think waiting for the light is a basic part of riding responsibly."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong for running a red light. You're in the wrong for doubling down on justifying it. You're in the wrong for mocking the guy who rightfully called you out for it.\n\nI spent years cycling as my main transpo form and it's really not that f****** hard to wait at a red light."},{"Description":"ord_3231","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3231","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_pHa7rpnNC7mhF5K","FlowID":"FL_53","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_238","Description":"Item 23 (id 699)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_239","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_240","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. So for Halloween you faked a disability and took advantage of the kindness of strangers? Yeah, you're in the wrong.\n\nIf I helped someone out because I thought they had a disability, and then found out they were faking it for whatever wrong reason...I'd be creeped out."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were trying to approach the costume thoughtfully and gain some understanding of navigating without vision. But you still faked a disability and accepted help from strangers who believed you genuinely needed it. Many people would feel creeped out and used if they learned their kindness had been part of someone's Halloween role."},{"Description":"ord_699","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_699","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_241","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were trying to approach the costume thoughtfully and gain some understanding of navigating without vision. But you still faked a disability and accepted help from strangers who believed you genuinely needed it. Many people would feel creeped out and used if they learned their kindness had been part of someone's Halloween role."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. So for Halloween you faked a disability and took advantage of the kindness of strangers? Yeah, you're in the wrong.\n\nIf I helped someone out because I thought they had a disability, and then found out they were faking it for whatever wrong reason...I'd be creeped out."},{"Description":"ord_699","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_699","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_aFLDJztmRpVmRRC","FlowID":"FL_54","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_242","Description":"Item 24 (id 1559)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_243","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_244","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Or your sister's in the wrong if this really is her story. Sweet zombie baby Jesus, punishing the aunt for the dad's behaviour is not okay. Your aunt and cousin would be within their rights to stop speaking to your side of the family entirely if you're going to side with your wrong dad."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: this sounds like miscommunication and your sister may have wanted to avoid more conflict. Still, excluding your aunt over your father's unfounded accusation punishes her for his behavior. Your aunt and cousin may reasonably decide to step back from your side of the family if they feel you are siding with him."},{"Description":"ord_1559","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1559","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_245","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: this sounds like miscommunication and your sister may have wanted to avoid more conflict. Still, excluding your aunt over your father's unfounded accusation punishes her for his behavior. Your aunt and cousin may reasonably decide to step back from your side of the family if they feel you are siding with him."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Or your sister's in the wrong if this really is her story. Sweet zombie baby Jesus, punishing the aunt for the dad's behaviour is not okay. Your aunt and cousin would be within their rights to stop speaking to your side of the family entirely if you're going to side with your wrong dad."},{"Description":"ord_1559","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1559","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_UrT5fadix8B8ReH","FlowID":"FL_55","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_246","Description":"Item 25 (id 1902)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_247","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_248","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nLike a really huge wrong. A little Nutella isn't going to hurt your baby. And your wife is doing all the heavy lifting here. Stop being a controlling jerk and start being more supportive. Go buy your wife and jar and tell her you're sorry ffs."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you see pregnancy as a team effort and want to be involved in choices that affect the baby. But a small spoonful of Nutella every few days is unlikely to harm the baby, and your wife is the one carrying the pregnancy and doing the physical work. She needs support rather than having her food policed or being yelled at. Buy her a jar of Nutella and apologize."},{"Description":"ord_1902","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1902","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_249","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you see pregnancy as a team effort and want to be involved in choices that affect the baby. But a small spoonful of Nutella every few days is unlikely to harm the baby, and your wife is the one carrying the pregnancy and doing the physical work. She needs support rather than having her food policed or being yelled at. Buy her a jar of Nutella and apologize."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nLike a really huge wrong. A little Nutella isn't going to hurt your baby. And your wife is doing all the heavy lifting here. Stop being a controlling jerk and start being more supportive. Go buy your wife and jar and tell her you're sorry ffs."},{"Description":"ord_1902","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1902","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_6vLzfHnBUl4sNXa","FlowID":"FL_56","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_250","Description":"Item 26 (id 2769)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_251","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_252","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYou suck for:\n\n\"Also, I told her that even If I decide to donate half of my salary, she should support my decision as long as I'm covering both of us.\"\n\n\"You get no say in the budget because I make all the money\" is a pretty s***** thing to say to your partner who is supposed to be your equal.\n\nOne suggestion I've seen when there is a large income gap is that you each get the same amount of money to do whatever you want with. Save, buy a car, home theater, whatever. Might be something to think about."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nIt sounds like you see your income as yours to allocate, especially since you cover most shared expenses and are still saving. But telling your wife that she has no say if you choose to give away half your salary treats her as less of an equal partner in plans that affect both of you, including buying a flat.\n\nA possible approach with a large income gap is for each of you to have the same amount of personal spending money, while the rest goes toward shared expenses, savings goals, and family support. You could each use that personal amount however you want-saving, buying a car, or helping relatives-while still making the larger financial decisions together."},{"Description":"ord_2769","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2769","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_253","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nIt sounds like you see your income as yours to allocate, especially since you cover most shared expenses and are still saving. But telling your wife that she has no say if you choose to give away half your salary treats her as less of an equal partner in plans that affect both of you, including buying a flat.\n\nA possible approach with a large income gap is for each of you to have the same amount of personal spending money, while the rest goes toward shared expenses, savings goals, and family support. You could each use that personal amount however you want-saving, buying a car, or helping relatives-while still making the larger financial decisions together."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYou suck for:\n\n\"Also, I told her that even If I decide to donate half of my salary, she should support my decision as long as I'm covering both of us.\"\n\n\"You get no say in the budget because I make all the money\" is a pretty s***** thing to say to your partner who is supposed to be your equal.\n\nOne suggestion I've seen when there is a large income gap is that you each get the same amount of money to do whatever you want with. Save, buy a car, home theater, whatever. Might be something to think about."},{"Description":"ord_2769","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2769","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_C3ZcwOj8wUr5nve","FlowID":"FL_57","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_254","Description":"Item 27 (id 2630)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_255","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_256","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong here's an idea, why don't you be a paralegal instead of a lawyer? That would be much less demanding on your time and you could be a better dad to your two your old while she pursues med school.\n\nHuh, that doesn't sound appealing? You feel like you don't want to live smaller dreams in order to make sure your partner can live out hers? Maybe that's exactly how she feels, dummy.\n\nIt does change things that this is a new dream of hers. I still don't think OPs position should just be 'no', and there's no harm in her starting undergrad while she figures out how feasible this path is going to be. If she really wanted to make this happen, it would not be impossible, and OP should not immediately poo-poo it the way he's doing."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why the length of this path feels daunting when you will both be raising a young child and starting demanding careers. But suggesting she become a nurse practitioner or occupational therapist instead of pursuing the career she wants is a lot like asking you to become a paralegal rather than a lawyer so you can be more available at home.\n\nHer interest in medicine may be new, and it makes sense to talk honestly about finances, childcare, and the practical demands involved. Still, there is no harm in her starting undergrad while the two of you work out whether medical school is feasible. If she decides she truly wants this, it may be difficult, but it is not impossible. Don't shut the goal down before she has the chance to explore it."},{"Description":"ord_2630","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2630","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_257","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why the length of this path feels daunting when you will both be raising a young child and starting demanding careers. But suggesting she become a nurse practitioner or occupational therapist instead of pursuing the career she wants is a lot like asking you to become a paralegal rather than a lawyer so you can be more available at home.\n\nHer interest in medicine may be new, and it makes sense to talk honestly about finances, childcare, and the practical demands involved. Still, there is no harm in her starting undergrad while the two of you work out whether medical school is feasible. If she decides she truly wants this, it may be difficult, but it is not impossible. Don't shut the goal down before she has the chance to explore it."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong here's an idea, why don't you be a paralegal instead of a lawyer? That would be much less demanding on your time and you could be a better dad to your two your old while she pursues med school.\n\nHuh, that doesn't sound appealing? You feel like you don't want to live smaller dreams in order to make sure your partner can live out hers? Maybe that's exactly how she feels, dummy.\n\nIt does change things that this is a new dream of hers. I still don't think OPs position should just be 'no', and there's no harm in her starting undergrad while she figures out how feasible this path is going to be. If she really wanted to make this happen, it would not be impossible, and OP should not immediately poo-poo it the way he's doing."},{"Description":"ord_2630","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2630","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_o7ykYChNoeBj6o8","FlowID":"FL_58","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_258","Description":"Item 28 (id 1224)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_259","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_260","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"\"Now nobody wants to go.\"\n\nSurely you jest! A bunch of college kids don't want to take a test for the right to be in a dorm room and watch a tv?? Are dorm rooms generally disgusting places no one wants to be? Are tv's wildely available? What could possibly get college kids not to submit to a quiz for the right to watch TV in a dorm room?\n\nSuch a puzzle.\n\nYou're in the wrong."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that it seemed fairer than choosing only your best friends, and that it is your TV and room. But asking people to take different quizzes based on gender and how attractive you find them makes the invitation process demeaning. A free screening is not enough to make people want to submit to that, so it is understandable that they no longer want to attend."},{"Description":"ord_1224","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1224","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_261","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that it seemed fairer than choosing only your best friends, and that it is your TV and room. But asking people to take different quizzes based on gender and how attractive you find them makes the invitation process demeaning. A free screening is not enough to make people want to submit to that, so it is understandable that they no longer want to attend."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"\"Now nobody wants to go.\"\n\nSurely you jest! A bunch of college kids don't want to take a test for the right to be in a dorm room and watch a tv?? Are dorm rooms generally disgusting places no one wants to be? Are tv's wildely available? What could possibly get college kids not to submit to a quiz for the right to watch TV in a dorm room?\n\nSuch a puzzle.\n\nYou're in the wrong."},{"Description":"ord_1224","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1224","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_4B0A7G89SNQgAqu","FlowID":"FL_59","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_262","Description":"Item 29 (id 3911)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_263","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_264","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYou're not the boss, you're shift leader while she is out and the power is going to your head.\n\nYou have been there 6 months and he's been there for years. Your other coworkers seem to Express no issue.\n\nAlso how is bringing someone directly to an item instead of directing them, a problem? That's good customer service!"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you are concerned that Adam's muteness is affecting phone coverage and the team's workload. But you are filling in temporarily, while he has worked there for years and your other coworkers do not seem to see this as a problem. Bringing a customer directly to an item also sounds like good customer service. Raise any concrete coverage issue with your boss, but do not push for him to be transferred or lose his job."},{"Description":"ord_3911","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3911","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_265","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you are concerned that Adam's muteness is affecting phone coverage and the team's workload. But you are filling in temporarily, while he has worked there for years and your other coworkers do not seem to see this as a problem. Bringing a customer directly to an item also sounds like good customer service. Raise any concrete coverage issue with your boss, but do not push for him to be transferred or lose his job."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYou're not the boss, you're shift leader while she is out and the power is going to your head.\n\nYou have been there 6 months and he's been there for years. Your other coworkers seem to Express no issue.\n\nAlso how is bringing someone directly to an item instead of directing them, a problem? That's good customer service!"},{"Description":"ord_3911","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3911","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_6nqGSMaz7tyeKqW","FlowID":"FL_60","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_266","Description":"Item 30 (id 1414)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_267","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_268","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. If you \"crave physical affection\" so much that you're willing to A) cuddle with a dude and not tell your gf about it and B) use this dude as your weird platonic cuddle doll knowing he has feelings for you, an LDR is not for you."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you miss physical affection in a long-distance relationship and do not have romantic feelings for him. But hiding the cuddling from your girlfriend and continuing to use him as a cuddle partner when you know he has a crush on you is unfair to both of them. If physical closeness is this important to you, I think a long-distance relationship may not be a good fit right now."},{"Description":"ord_1414","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1414","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_269","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you miss physical affection in a long-distance relationship and do not have romantic feelings for him. But hiding the cuddling from your girlfriend and continuing to use him as a cuddle partner when you know he has a crush on you is unfair to both of them. If physical closeness is this important to you, I think a long-distance relationship may not be a good fit right now."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. If you \"crave physical affection\" so much that you're willing to A) cuddle with a dude and not tell your gf about it and B) use this dude as your weird platonic cuddle doll knowing he has feelings for you, an LDR is not for you."},{"Description":"ord_1414","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1414","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_nIkc6CpeLZD4k8c","FlowID":"FL_61","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_270","Description":"Item 31 (id 3064)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_271","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_272","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Yes 100% you were in the wrong, it's not even in question.\n\nYou don't get to tell your partner who they can be friends with. If you don't want to hang around with the crazy chick then don't, but you don't get to control your GF. If they enjoy each other's company why do you even care?"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you wanted your girlfriend to understand why you found this girl so difficult to be around, especially after the things you say she had said to you. But you do not get to decide who your partner can be friends with. You could choose not to spend time with that person yourself; trying to stop your girlfriend from seeing her was controlling. If they enjoyed each other's company, that was her choice."},{"Description":"ord_3064","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3064","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_273","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you wanted your girlfriend to understand why you found this girl so difficult to be around, especially after the things you say she had said to you. But you do not get to decide who your partner can be friends with. You could choose not to spend time with that person yourself; trying to stop your girlfriend from seeing her was controlling. If they enjoyed each other's company, that was her choice."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Yes 100% you were in the wrong, it's not even in question.\n\nYou don't get to tell your partner who they can be friends with. If you don't want to hang around with the crazy chick then don't, but you don't get to control your GF. If they enjoy each other's company why do you even care?"},{"Description":"ord_3064","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3064","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_7bVHZUfYFjbldfy","FlowID":"FL_62","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_274","Description":"Item 32 (id 453)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_275","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_276","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\n\"\"I asked him if he'd still care about me....if he missed me\"\"\n\nWhy? Y'all broke up for a reason.\n\n\"\"I miss you....but why would you care\" He then got p***** off and told me that was incredibly manipulative.\"\"\n\nHe's right - it's the little \"but why would you care?\" line at the end that does it. You're trying to guilt trip the dude and that's not okay.\n\n\"\"I told my boyfriend I missed him\"\"\n\nNo, you told your EX-boyfriend that you missed him and then threw in a passive-aggressive comment that you're not trying to play off."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were trying to express that you recognized he had moved on, not blame him for it. But asking whether he still cared or missed you after the breakup, then adding \"but why would you care?\" can easily put him in the position of feeling guilty for not returning those feelings.\n\nHe is your ex, and the breakup happened for a reason. Telling him you miss him is one thing; adding that line makes it come across as a passive-aggressive guilt trip, even if that was not your intent."},{"Description":"ord_453","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_453","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_277","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you were trying to express that you recognized he had moved on, not blame him for it. But asking whether he still cared or missed you after the breakup, then adding \"but why would you care?\" can easily put him in the position of feeling guilty for not returning those feelings.\n\nHe is your ex, and the breakup happened for a reason. Telling him you miss him is one thing; adding that line makes it come across as a passive-aggressive guilt trip, even if that was not your intent."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\n\"\"I asked him if he'd still care about me....if he missed me\"\"\n\nWhy? Y'all broke up for a reason.\n\n\"\"I miss you....but why would you care\" He then got p***** off and told me that was incredibly manipulative.\"\"\n\nHe's right - it's the little \"but why would you care?\" line at the end that does it. You're trying to guilt trip the dude and that's not okay.\n\n\"\"I told my boyfriend I missed him\"\"\n\nNo, you told your EX-boyfriend that you missed him and then threw in a passive-aggressive comment that you're not trying to play off."},{"Description":"ord_453","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_453","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_KPJvsRvx4dXRkub","FlowID":"FL_63","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_278","Description":"Item 33 (id 461)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_279","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_280","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nWhat the heck does him being gay or him being lonely have to do with any of this? You have a worker who likes to work. Stop in the wrong about it, explain to your boss that you've made it clear to Eric that he can leave any time and stop being a terrible manager."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you were worried that Eric staying late would create concerns for your boss and could shift work onto you. But his being gay or lonely is irrelevant here. You have an employee who wants to work. Make it clear to Eric that he can leave at the end of his scheduled hours, explain that to your boss, and stop treating him poorly for how he spends his time."},{"Description":"ord_461","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_461","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_281","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand where you are coming from: you were worried that Eric staying late would create concerns for your boss and could shift work onto you. But his being gay or lonely is irrelevant here. You have an employee who wants to work. Make it clear to Eric that he can leave at the end of his scheduled hours, explain that to your boss, and stop treating him poorly for how he spends his time."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nWhat the heck does him being gay or him being lonely have to do with any of this? You have a worker who likes to work. Stop in the wrong about it, explain to your boss that you've made it clear to Eric that he can leave any time and stop being a terrible manager."},{"Description":"ord_461","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_461","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_GEdYOdgaDOAWsyQ","FlowID":"FL_64","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_282","Description":"Item 34 (id 2532)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_283","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_284","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You made s*** awkward for no reason. Were you REALLY worried about the nurse being objectified (by a married woman giving an off the cuff complement and trying to make small talk) or did you just have a pang of jealousy against your much younger good looking coworker?"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you want to protect a new nurse from comments that make her feel reduced to her appearance. In this case, though, the wife gave her a brief compliment and made small talk, and the nurse responded warmly. Calling it irrelevant and inappropriate made the room awkward without any clear need."},{"Description":"ord_2532","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2532","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_285","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you want to protect a new nurse from comments that make her feel reduced to her appearance. In this case, though, the wife gave her a brief compliment and made small talk, and the nurse responded warmly. Calling it irrelevant and inappropriate made the room awkward without any clear need."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You made s*** awkward for no reason. Were you REALLY worried about the nurse being objectified (by a married woman giving an off the cuff complement and trying to make small talk) or did you just have a pang of jealousy against your much younger good looking coworker?"},{"Description":"ord_2532","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2532","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_5mpSeRDBGSYXwAx","FlowID":"FL_65","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_286","Description":"Item 35 (id 1588)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_287","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_288","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Because sometimes plans change and adulting involves understanding that work obligations sometimes take precedence in order to continue providing.\n\nI can't honestly connect your comments about his haircut to your comments about the cabin. You sound really passive aggressive and petulant. Go to the work event with him to show your support and be proud of his accomplishments. Then take him to the cabin to celebrate privately.\n\nAs for the haircut, it's just hair. Bad cuts happen. Guess what? It won't last forever, but the way you treat him over all this will certainly leave a lasting impression."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you were really looking forward to this cabin day, especially with your conflicting schedules and no usual Christmas celebration nearby. Still, work obligations sometimes take priority, particularly when he is being recognized for doing well.\n\nHis haircut and the cabin plans seem like separate issues. Suggesting he skip the award over a haircut may come across as passive-aggressive. Go to the work event with him, support his accomplishment, and then celebrate together at the cabin afterward.\n\nBad haircuts happen and will grow out. How you handle his disappointment and success may leave a much longer impression."},{"Description":"ord_1588","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1588","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_289","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that you were really looking forward to this cabin day, especially with your conflicting schedules and no usual Christmas celebration nearby. Still, work obligations sometimes take priority, particularly when he is being recognized for doing well.\n\nHis haircut and the cabin plans seem like separate issues. Suggesting he skip the award over a haircut may come across as passive-aggressive. Go to the work event with him, support his accomplishment, and then celebrate together at the cabin afterward.\n\nBad haircuts happen and will grow out. How you handle his disappointment and success may leave a much longer impression."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Because sometimes plans change and adulting involves understanding that work obligations sometimes take precedence in order to continue providing.\n\nI can't honestly connect your comments about his haircut to your comments about the cabin. You sound really passive aggressive and petulant. Go to the work event with him to show your support and be proud of his accomplishments. Then take him to the cabin to celebrate privately.\n\nAs for the haircut, it's just hair. Bad cuts happen. Guess what? It won't last forever, but the way you treat him over all this will certainly leave a lasting impression."},{"Description":"ord_1588","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1588","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_kxz9QTJXUChount","FlowID":"FL_66","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_290","Description":"Item 36 (id 2760)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_291","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_292","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYou are getting pissy because she listed what she got for her birthday, including the price. H***, here in the midwest, it's almost customary for someone to say something like:\n\nPerson 1: Oh, that's a cute jacket\n\nPerson 2: Thanks - $20 at Ross, it was half off."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that listing the prices made the conversation feel rude or showy to you. But people often mention prices as part of describing a purchase or gift, especially when they are excited about a good deal or want to give context. In some places, it is even pretty customary: \"Cute jacket!\" \"Thanks, $20 at Ross-it was half off.\" Her including the prices does not necessarily say anything negative about her personality."},{"Description":"ord_2760","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2760","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_293","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that listing the prices made the conversation feel rude or showy to you. But people often mention prices as part of describing a purchase or gift, especially when they are excited about a good deal or want to give context. In some places, it is even pretty customary: \"Cute jacket!\" \"Thanks, $20 at Ross-it was half off.\" Her including the prices does not necessarily say anything negative about her personality."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYou are getting pissy because she listed what she got for her birthday, including the price. H***, here in the midwest, it's almost customary for someone to say something like:\n\nPerson 1: Oh, that's a cute jacket\n\nPerson 2: Thanks - $20 at Ross, it was half off."},{"Description":"ord_2760","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2760","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_ZHe6HPNAftlZYT5","FlowID":"FL_67","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_294","Description":"Item 37 (id 1855)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_295","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_296","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. So many times here I think to myself, \"It would have taken absolutely 0 energy\/time for this person to just not engage.\" This is one of those times, and I'm rolling my eyes at the drama you've created through needlessly insulting somebody. Even if you're right and she is using some fake breathy Marilyn Monroe voice, who cares? What did you gain by speaking up?"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that her voice change has been bothering you for a long time and seems put on to you. Even if she is deliberately using a breathier voice, she is allowed to speak how she wants. Telling her it is fake, annoying, and not sexy only created hurt and unnecessary drama. You did not need to comment on it."},{"Description":"ord_1855","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1855","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_297","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that her voice change has been bothering you for a long time and seems put on to you. Even if she is deliberately using a breathier voice, she is allowed to speak how she wants. Telling her it is fake, annoying, and not sexy only created hurt and unnecessary drama. You did not need to comment on it."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. So many times here I think to myself, \"It would have taken absolutely 0 energy\/time for this person to just not engage.\" This is one of those times, and I'm rolling my eyes at the drama you've created through needlessly insulting somebody. Even if you're right and she is using some fake breathy Marilyn Monroe voice, who cares? What did you gain by speaking up?"},{"Description":"ord_1855","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1855","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_Nbvgqc54G02ez51","FlowID":"FL_68","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_298","Description":"Item 38 (id 3643)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_299","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_300","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You knew what the procedures were when you shopped there. It was your own mistake that caused this problem and you won't own up to it. Having to wait for them to print a new receipt for you wouldn't have taken that long, and now you definitely look like you were stealing something. I'm sorry your wife had to deal with that. Also, you're setting a fantastic example for your kids that the rules don't apply to them."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you did not want to spend more time at Costco over a receipt you accidentally threw away. But you knew the exit procedure when you shopped there, and losing the receipt was your own mistake. Waiting for a reprint likely would not have taken long, while walking out made it look as though you might be taking unpaid merchandise. It also put your wife in an uncomfortable position and showed your kids that rules can be ignored when they are inconvenient."},{"Description":"ord_3643","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3643","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_301","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you did not want to spend more time at Costco over a receipt you accidentally threw away. But you knew the exit procedure when you shopped there, and losing the receipt was your own mistake. Waiting for a reprint likely would not have taken long, while walking out made it look as though you might be taking unpaid merchandise. It also put your wife in an uncomfortable position and showed your kids that rules can be ignored when they are inconvenient."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You knew what the procedures were when you shopped there. It was your own mistake that caused this problem and you won't own up to it. Having to wait for them to print a new receipt for you wouldn't have taken that long, and now you definitely look like you were stealing something. I'm sorry your wife had to deal with that. Also, you're setting a fantastic example for your kids that the rules don't apply to them."},{"Description":"ord_3643","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3643","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_unrWwjDckDqMQKV","FlowID":"FL_69","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_302","Description":"Item 39 (id 3111)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_303","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_304","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You're completely oblivious to the fact that your son is clearly showing signs of depression and instead of trying to help him, you take all privacy away and punish him further. He won't eat unless you bring it to him? Stays in bed all day? Shows a lack of interest in everything? Messy living space? Dude open your eyes"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you saw his behavior as teenage moodiness and defiance, but staying in bed all day, barely eating unless food is brought to him, losing interest in everything, and living in a worsening mess can be signs of depression. Taking away his door, phone, and privacy may make him withdraw further rather than help him engage.\n\nPlease do not dismiss the concern as laziness or assume you would automatically know if he were depressed. Talking with a mental-health professional is a constructive next step, and it could help identify what is going on. Ignoring these signs could have serious consequences."},{"Description":"ord_3111","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3111","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_305","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you saw his behavior as teenage moodiness and defiance, but staying in bed all day, barely eating unless food is brought to him, losing interest in everything, and living in a worsening mess can be signs of depression. Taking away his door, phone, and privacy may make him withdraw further rather than help him engage.\n\nPlease do not dismiss the concern as laziness or assume you would automatically know if he were depressed. Talking with a mental-health professional is a constructive next step, and it could help identify what is going on. Ignoring these signs could have serious consequences."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You're completely oblivious to the fact that your son is clearly showing signs of depression and instead of trying to help him, you take all privacy away and punish him further. He won't eat unless you bring it to him? Stays in bed all day? Shows a lack of interest in everything? Messy living space? Dude open your eyes"},{"Description":"ord_3111","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3111","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_PlJ7tDtqVO0few5","FlowID":"FL_70","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_306","Description":"Item 40 (id 1538)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_307","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_308","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Sure you can save your own life but don't try to kill other people why you do it. And anyway there's no way a fire spreads that fast and with only 4 floors you shouldn't have been running, you were more likely to hurt yourself doing that"},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why the smoke and alarm made you want to get out as quickly as possible. But knocking over a child on a crowded stairwell could seriously injure them and make the evacuation more dangerous for everyone. In a four-floor building, moving quickly but carefully is likely safer than running and pushing past people."},{"Description":"ord_1538","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1538","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_309","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I see why the smoke and alarm made you want to get out as quickly as possible. But knocking over a child on a crowded stairwell could seriously injure them and make the evacuation more dangerous for everyone. In a four-floor building, moving quickly but carefully is likely safer than running and pushing past people."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Sure you can save your own life but don't try to kill other people why you do it. And anyway there's no way a fire spreads that fast and with only 4 floors you shouldn't have been running, you were more likely to hurt yourself doing that"},{"Description":"ord_1538","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1538","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_rZgYlqudf8unuFk","FlowID":"FL_71","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_310","Description":"Item 41 (id 2122)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_311","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_312","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Of course you're in the wrong. Seriously, you needed to ask?\n\nAsk yourself if you would be \"uncomfortable\" if he was kissing a woman and the whole \"not around my kids\" BS is bigoted, as it's based on some notion that seeing non-hetero affection will \"corrupt\" children and make them \"the gay\" which deep down, you think is a bad thing."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that their level of affection makes you uncomfortable and you want to set expectations in your home. But would you impose the same rule if your brother were kissing a woman or holding hands with her? Treating same-sex affection as inappropriate around your children sends the message that being gay is something to hide. Your brother and his partner deserve to feel welcome at Christmas."},{"Description":"ord_2122","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2122","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_313","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I get that their level of affection makes you uncomfortable and you want to set expectations in your home. But would you impose the same rule if your brother were kissing a woman or holding hands with her? Treating same-sex affection as inappropriate around your children sends the message that being gay is something to hide. Your brother and his partner deserve to feel welcome at Christmas."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Of course you're in the wrong. Seriously, you needed to ask?\n\nAsk yourself if you would be \"uncomfortable\" if he was kissing a woman and the whole \"not around my kids\" BS is bigoted, as it's based on some notion that seeing non-hetero affection will \"corrupt\" children and make them \"the gay\" which deep down, you think is a bad thing."},{"Description":"ord_2122","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2122","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_YJn58Je70r0OQev","FlowID":"FL_72","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_314","Description":"Item 42 (id 3874)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_315","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_316","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You didn't forget to get people Christmas gifts, you chose not to. You chose to get your BF something so knock off with that defence. Chrisrmas comes the same time each year, if you had wanted to you could have done something for your mom, and family. The choice not to was a choice.\n\nEven if the chocolates had been for everyone, gorging yourself on them was the wrong way to behave and you are old enough to know better."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I think you are saying that, as a 14-year-old without a job, you did not have much available for family gifts, even though you bought gifts for your boyfriend. Still, choosing to buy for your boyfriend while getting nothing for your family was a choice. Christmas is predictable, and you could have planned or made something for your mom and family.\n\nAnd even if the chocolates had been for everyone, eating nearly all of a shared box was inconsiderate. At 14, you are old enough to know to check before helping yourself to someone else's gift."},{"Description":"ord_3874","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3874","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_317","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I think you are saying that, as a 14-year-old without a job, you did not have much available for family gifts, even though you bought gifts for your boyfriend. Still, choosing to buy for your boyfriend while getting nothing for your family was a choice. Christmas is predictable, and you could have planned or made something for your mom and family.\n\nAnd even if the chocolates had been for everyone, eating nearly all of a shared box was inconsiderate. At 14, you are old enough to know to check before helping yourself to someone else's gift."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. You didn't forget to get people Christmas gifts, you chose not to. You chose to get your BF something so knock off with that defence. Chrisrmas comes the same time each year, if you had wanted to you could have done something for your mom, and family. The choice not to was a choice.\n\nEven if the chocolates had been for everyone, gorging yourself on them was the wrong way to behave and you are old enough to know better."},{"Description":"ord_3874","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3874","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_bRzI2fDcKP5UM6I","FlowID":"FL_73","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_318","Description":"Item 43 (id 2901)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_319","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_320","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, that's really gross. Your feet are probably still covered in bacteria from stepping around in a hot moist shower and walking over the bathroom floor which probably has molecules of fecal matter. Fecal matter that's been going on her face for months.\n\nPlease apologize to her profusely and get your own soft towel."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you see this as sharing a towel after you are already clean from the shower. But feet still pick up bacteria from the warm, damp shower and the bathroom floor, which can contain fecal matter. That has been going onto her face for months without her knowing.\n\nPlease apologize sincerely and get your own soft towel."},{"Description":"ord_2901","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2901","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_321","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you see this as sharing a towel after you are already clean from the shower. But feet still pick up bacteria from the warm, damp shower and the bathroom floor, which can contain fecal matter. That has been going onto her face for months without her knowing.\n\nPlease apologize sincerely and get your own soft towel."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, that's really gross. Your feet are probably still covered in bacteria from stepping around in a hot moist shower and walking over the bathroom floor which probably has molecules of fecal matter. Fecal matter that's been going on her face for months.\n\nPlease apologize to her profusely and get your own soft towel."},{"Description":"ord_2901","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2901","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_LKVNcuz9BEUc6Cy","FlowID":"FL_74","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_322","Description":"Item 44 (id 2640)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_323","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_324","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Probably yes, you're in the wrong.\n\nDid you pay attention to who got them. Give sour candies to 13 years olds - fun, not in the wrong.\n\nGive them to 3 or 4 years olds who cant read and wont know what they are, yes, huge wrong move.\n\nAlso selfish wrong move for doing it motivated by wanting to eat the good candy yourself and pawn off the sour stuff on unsuspecting children."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you wanted to save some of the Reese's cups for yourself, and sour candy can be fun for older kids who know what they're getting. But giving \"Toxic Waste\" candy to very young children who cannot read the label or anticipate how sour it is was not a thoughtful choice.\n\nYou also said you did not consider age, so some 3- or 4-year-olds may have gotten it. Keeping a couple dozen Reese's for yourself did not require pawning off the sour candy on unsuspecting little kids."},{"Description":"ord_2640","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2640","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_325","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you wanted to save some of the Reese's cups for yourself, and sour candy can be fun for older kids who know what they're getting. But giving \"Toxic Waste\" candy to very young children who cannot read the label or anticipate how sour it is was not a thoughtful choice.\n\nYou also said you did not consider age, so some 3- or 4-year-olds may have gotten it. Keeping a couple dozen Reese's for yourself did not require pawning off the sour candy on unsuspecting little kids."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Probably yes, you're in the wrong.\n\nDid you pay attention to who got them. Give sour candies to 13 years olds - fun, not in the wrong.\n\nGive them to 3 or 4 years olds who cant read and wont know what they are, yes, huge wrong move.\n\nAlso selfish wrong move for doing it motivated by wanting to eat the good candy yourself and pawn off the sour stuff on unsuspecting children."},{"Description":"ord_2640","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2640","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_EM1gdJNVPLShuKu","FlowID":"FL_75","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_326","Description":"Item 45 (id 3102)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_327","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_328","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Oh lord, sending d pics, just don't. It wasn't an accident. Poll does not = pole. Sarcasm is a thing and I can see that isn't where she was going with the conversation. There was some innuendo but unsolicited is still unsolicited until it's specifically \"lemme see yo d\"."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you read the exchange as mutual sexual innuendo and thought she was asking for a picture. But sending a d*** pic is not an accident, and a \"poll\" is not a \"pole.\" There may have been some flirting, yet unsolicited is still unsolicited until she clearly says she wants to see it."},{"Description":"ord_3102","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3102","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_329","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you read the exchange as mutual sexual innuendo and thought she was asking for a picture. But sending a d*** pic is not an accident, and a \"poll\" is not a \"pole.\" There may have been some flirting, yet unsolicited is still unsolicited until she clearly says she wants to see it."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Oh lord, sending d pics, just don't. It wasn't an accident. Poll does not = pole. Sarcasm is a thing and I can see that isn't where she was going with the conversation. There was some innuendo but unsolicited is still unsolicited until it's specifically \"lemme see yo d\"."},{"Description":"ord_3102","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3102","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_AC32ivSLOhdoujm","FlowID":"FL_76","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_330","Description":"Item 46 (id 1455)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_331","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_332","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. If you knew there was a discrepancy and they overfunded you, then you have that cashier getting written up for being short on their drawer later when they clock out. Yeah, they should've paid closer attention but they made an honest mistake. You made a dishonest choice."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you did try to flag that the change seemed wrong, but once you knew there was a discrepancy, the right thing was to return the extra money. The cashier may be written up when their drawer comes up short. They made an honest mistake; keeping the overpayment was a dishonest choice."},{"Description":"ord_1455","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1455","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_333","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you did try to flag that the change seemed wrong, but once you knew there was a discrepancy, the right thing was to return the extra money. The cashier may be written up when their drawer comes up short. They made an honest mistake; keeping the overpayment was a dishonest choice."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. If you knew there was a discrepancy and they overfunded you, then you have that cashier getting written up for being short on their drawer later when they clock out. Yeah, they should've paid closer attention but they made an honest mistake. You made a dishonest choice."},{"Description":"ord_1455","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1455","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_5AE2zvW3ck2TjHq","FlowID":"FL_77","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_334","Description":"Item 47 (id 2412)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_335","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_336","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. He wasn't a stranger. He was someone from church that your wife knew. And she might not be in a position in which she would need other people's help if it didn't also seem glaringly obvious that the twins' father was too busy for them, unless it's to assert his alpha male dominance."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you were alarmed to see someone you did not know picking up your child. But he was someone from church whom your wife knew, not a stranger to her. It also sounds like she has been relying on support from that community while caring for one-year-old twins, especially when you are working. Getting to know the people who help her would be a more constructive response than refusing to meet them and telling her she should not need help."},{"Description":"ord_2412","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2412","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_337","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that you were alarmed to see someone you did not know picking up your child. But he was someone from church whom your wife knew, not a stranger to her. It also sounds like she has been relying on support from that community while caring for one-year-old twins, especially when you are working. Getting to know the people who help her would be a more constructive response than refusing to meet them and telling her she should not need help."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. He wasn't a stranger. He was someone from church that your wife knew. And she might not be in a position in which she would need other people's help if it didn't also seem glaringly obvious that the twins' father was too busy for them, unless it's to assert his alpha male dominance."},{"Description":"ord_2412","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2412","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_VtRi4GuJh53BZGq","FlowID":"FL_78","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_338","Description":"Item 48 (id 1874)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_339","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_340","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong, come on. She's recovering and wants your support, of-f***-course you'd be in the wrong to go party rn.\n\nThere will be other parties, but leaving your recovering GF when she asked you to stay with her is a d move that could seriously damage your relationship."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong. I see why you want to take the rare chance to see your friends, especially when you do not get out often. But your girlfriend is recovering from a hospital stay, is on pain medication, and has directly asked for your support. There will be other parties; staying with her tonight shows that you can be relied on when she needs you, and leaving could seriously damage the relationship."},{"Description":"ord_1874","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1874","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_341","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong. I see why you want to take the rare chance to see your friends, especially when you do not get out often. But your girlfriend is recovering from a hospital stay, is on pain medication, and has directly asked for your support. There will be other parties; staying with her tonight shows that you can be relied on when she needs you, and leaving could seriously damage the relationship."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong, come on. She's recovering and wants your support, of-f***-course you'd be in the wrong to go party rn.\n\nThere will be other parties, but leaving your recovering GF when she asked you to stay with her is a d move that could seriously damage your relationship."},{"Description":"ord_1874","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1874","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_y6RRATYzRDTVTHK","FlowID":"FL_79","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_342","Description":"Item 49 (id 3675)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_343","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_344","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It is extremely tasteless and it will be a terrible idea to cosplay as them. I think you must know that the symbol is now forever associated with Nazism. Even if you explain what it is\/about the character to people, you're just going to come off as someone who really wanted to wear a swastika out in public."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you understand the symbol's older meaning and the manga's historical setting. In public, though, a swastika is overwhelmingly associated with Nazism, and wearing one as part of a cosplay will likely read as someone looking for an excuse to display it. Explaining the character afterward probably will not change that impression."},{"Description":"ord_3675","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3675","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_345","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It sounds like you understand the symbol's older meaning and the manga's historical setting. In public, though, a swastika is overwhelmingly associated with Nazism, and wearing one as part of a cosplay will likely read as someone looking for an excuse to display it. Explaining the character afterward probably will not change that impression."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. It is extremely tasteless and it will be a terrible idea to cosplay as them. I think you must know that the symbol is now forever associated with Nazism. Even if you explain what it is\/about the character to people, you're just going to come off as someone who really wanted to wear a swastika out in public."},{"Description":"ord_3675","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3675","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_eDC7n3SatdnVaL1","FlowID":"FL_80","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_346","Description":"Item 50 (id 2466)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_347","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_348","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. If he didn't bring it up, why did you? That's not normal conversation topics, especially because you disagree with him doing it, it was to shame him.\n\nAbsolutely he should not be doing that and it's worth it to have a private conversation with him, but don't publicly embarrass him."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that his bragging about fraternity friends made you feel the drinking behavior was relevant to what he was saying. Still, bringing up that he vomits to keep drinking publicly embarrassed him, especially when he had chosen not to share it himself.\n\nThat behavior is concerning, and it would be reasonable to talk to him privately about it. Publicly exposing it, though, came across as shaming him rather than helping."},{"Description":"ord_2466","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2466","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_349","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. I understand that his bragging about fraternity friends made you feel the drinking behavior was relevant to what he was saying. Still, bringing up that he vomits to keep drinking publicly embarrassed him, especially when he had chosen not to share it himself.\n\nThat behavior is concerning, and it would be reasonable to talk to him privately about it. Publicly exposing it, though, came across as shaming him rather than helping."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. If he didn't bring it up, why did you? That's not normal conversation topics, especially because you disagree with him doing it, it was to shame him.\n\nAbsolutely he should not be doing that and it's worth it to have a private conversation with him, but don't publicly embarrass him."},{"Description":"ord_2466","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2466","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_lD6rhbajze63LTd","FlowID":"FL_81","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_350","Description":"Item 51 (id 2228)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_351","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_352","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Probably you would be in the wrong if you went to a former director based on rumors and ambiguous observations.\n\nWhat you have is secondhand gossip plus circumstances that could mean many things: texting, leaving an event together, and standing in a stairwell are not proof of an affair. Reporting it could seriously harm both of them professionally and personally, especially Sandy, who may end up carrying more of the reputational damage despite there being no evidence of wrongdoing.\n\nA reasonable approach:\n\n- Do not contact management\/HR\/the former director unless you have direct knowledge of misconduct relevant to work-e.g., coercion, harassment, a reporting-line conflict, favoritism, misuse of company resources, etc.\n- If Carlos is genuinely a close friend and you are worried for him, you could have one private, non-accusatory conversation: \"People are talking, and I care about you. I don't need details, but be aware this is becoming visible and could affect you.\" Then leave it alone.\n- I would be especially cautious about confronting Sandy. She may not be doing anything inappropriate, and even if she is, Carlos's marriage is primarily Carlos's responsibility.\n- Don't become a conduit for workplace gossip. Tell people who are \"chirping\" to stop speculating and, if they have an actual work-related concern, use the appropriate internal process themselves.\n\nYou have moved on from the company. Unless someone is being harmed or there is a real workplace-policy issue, the kindest and safest move is to stay out of the investigation\/exposure business. A discreet heads-up to Carlos, if you truly are friends, is the furthest I'd go."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Probably you would be in the wrong if you contacted a former director based on rumors and ambiguous observations.\n\nIt makes sense that you don't want to watch people you care about get hurt, especially after spending so long with that group. But what you have is secondhand gossip and situations that could have innocent explanations: texting, leaving an event together, and standing in a stairwell do not establish an affair. Taking that to management could cause serious professional and personal harm, particularly to Sandy, without clear evidence that either person did anything wrong.\n\nI would not contact management, HR, or the former director unless you have direct knowledge of a work-related issue such as harassment, coercion, a reporting-line conflict, favoritism, or misuse of company resources.\n\nIf Carlos is truly a close friend and your concern is for him rather than proving anything, you could have one private, non-accusatory conversation: \"People are talking, and I care about you. I don't need details, but you should know it's becoming visible and could affect you.\" Then leave it there.\n\nI'd be especially careful about approaching Sandy. She may not be doing anything inappropriate, and regardless, Carlos is responsible for his own marriage.\n\nTry not to become the conduit for workplace gossip. You can tell the people giving you \"chirps\" that speculation is not helpful, and that if they have a genuine workplace concern, they should use the company's internal process themselves.\n\nSince you've moved on, staying out of an investigation or exposure effort is likely the kindest and safest choice. A discreet heads-up to Carlos, if you are genuinely close, is the furthest I'd go."},{"Description":"ord_2228","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2228","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_353","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Probably you would be in the wrong if you contacted a former director based on rumors and ambiguous observations.\n\nIt makes sense that you don't want to watch people you care about get hurt, especially after spending so long with that group. But what you have is secondhand gossip and situations that could have innocent explanations: texting, leaving an event together, and standing in a stairwell do not establish an affair. Taking that to management could cause serious professional and personal harm, particularly to Sandy, without clear evidence that either person did anything wrong.\n\nI would not contact management, HR, or the former director unless you have direct knowledge of a work-related issue such as harassment, coercion, a reporting-line conflict, favoritism, or misuse of company resources.\n\nIf Carlos is truly a close friend and your concern is for him rather than proving anything, you could have one private, non-accusatory conversation: \"People are talking, and I care about you. I don't need details, but you should know it's becoming visible and could affect you.\" Then leave it there.\n\nI'd be especially careful about approaching Sandy. She may not be doing anything inappropriate, and regardless, Carlos is responsible for his own marriage.\n\nTry not to become the conduit for workplace gossip. You can tell the people giving you \"chirps\" that speculation is not helpful, and that if they have a genuine workplace concern, they should use the company's internal process themselves.\n\nSince you've moved on, staying out of an investigation or exposure effort is likely the kindest and safest choice. A discreet heads-up to Carlos, if you are genuinely close, is the furthest I'd go."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Probably you would be in the wrong if you went to a former director based on rumors and ambiguous observations.\n\nWhat you have is secondhand gossip plus circumstances that could mean many things: texting, leaving an event together, and standing in a stairwell are not proof of an affair. Reporting it could seriously harm both of them professionally and personally, especially Sandy, who may end up carrying more of the reputational damage despite there being no evidence of wrongdoing.\n\nA reasonable approach:\n\n- Do not contact management\/HR\/the former director unless you have direct knowledge of misconduct relevant to work-e.g., coercion, harassment, a reporting-line conflict, favoritism, misuse of company resources, etc.\n- If Carlos is genuinely a close friend and you are worried for him, you could have one private, non-accusatory conversation: \"People are talking, and I care about you. I don't need details, but be aware this is becoming visible and could affect you.\" Then leave it alone.\n- I would be especially cautious about confronting Sandy. She may not be doing anything inappropriate, and even if she is, Carlos's marriage is primarily Carlos's responsibility.\n- Don't become a conduit for workplace gossip. Tell people who are \"chirping\" to stop speculating and, if they have an actual work-related concern, use the appropriate internal process themselves.\n\nYou have moved on from the company. Unless someone is being harmed or there is a real workplace-policy issue, the kindest and safest move is to stay out of the investigation\/exposure business. A discreet heads-up to Carlos, if you truly are friends, is the furthest I'd go."},{"Description":"ord_2228","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2228","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_OWb0tJu25d3GSqm","FlowID":"FL_82","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_354","Description":"Item 52 (id 524)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_355","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_356","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Yes, you're in the wrong.\n\nYou do not have to personally believe in Hanukkah or treat it as a religious obligation. But refusing to participate at all-especially when it is your wife's holiday and something she wants to share with your son-comes across as rejecting an important part of your family's identity.\n\nThis is not comparable to a coworker inviting you to a holiday party you do not care about. It is your spouse asking you to sit with her and your child, light candles, exchange a small gift, eat food, or otherwise support a tradition that matters to her. You apparently expect her to be present for Christmas with your family, even if you did not \"force\" her. Healthy marriages are not supposed to run on \"I technically didn't force you, so I owe you nothing.\"\n\nAlso, this is now about your son as much as it is about your wife. If he grows up seeing Dad refuse to join Hanukkah because it is \"not his thing,\" he may reasonably interpret that as Dad rejecting Mom's religion\/culture-or rejecting the part of himself connected to it.\n\nYou can set reasonable boundaries: you do not have to pray, profess beliefs you do not hold, attend synagogue, or pretend to be Jewish. But participating as a supportive husband and father is different from being forced to adopt a religion.\n\nThe better approach is: \"I don't share the religious belief, but I want to support you and our son. Tell me what traditions matter to you and how I can be involved comfortably.\" That is what partnership looks like."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Yes, you're in the wrong.\n\nYou do not have to believe in Hanukkah, consider it personally meaningful, or take part in religious practices that conflict with your beliefs. But I think refusing to participate at all, when it is important to your wife and something she wants to share with your son, communicates rejection of a meaningful part of your family.\n\nYou are right that she chose to celebrate Christmas with your family and that you did not force her. But marriage should not be based on the minimum obligation of \"I didn't force you, so I do not need to support what matters to you.\" She has been part of your family's Christmas traditions; it is reasonable for her to want you to be present for hers.\n\nThis is also about your son. If he grows up seeing you refuse Hanukkah because it is \"not your thing,\" he may understandably see that as you rejecting his mother's religion or culture, or the part of his own background connected to it.\n\nYou can have boundaries. You do not need to pray, claim beliefs you do not hold, attend synagogue, or pretend to be Jewish. But lighting candles, sharing food, exchanging a small gift, or simply being present with your wife and child is support, not conversion.\n\nA better response would be: \"I do not share the religious belief, but I want to support you and our son. What traditions matter to you, and how can I participate in a way that feels comfortable for me?\""},{"Description":"ord_524","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_524","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_357","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Yes, you're in the wrong.\n\nYou do not have to believe in Hanukkah, consider it personally meaningful, or take part in religious practices that conflict with your beliefs. But I think refusing to participate at all, when it is important to your wife and something she wants to share with your son, communicates rejection of a meaningful part of your family.\n\nYou are right that she chose to celebrate Christmas with your family and that you did not force her. But marriage should not be based on the minimum obligation of \"I didn't force you, so I do not need to support what matters to you.\" She has been part of your family's Christmas traditions; it is reasonable for her to want you to be present for hers.\n\nThis is also about your son. If he grows up seeing you refuse Hanukkah because it is \"not your thing,\" he may understandably see that as you rejecting his mother's religion or culture, or the part of his own background connected to it.\n\nYou can have boundaries. You do not need to pray, claim beliefs you do not hold, attend synagogue, or pretend to be Jewish. But lighting candles, sharing food, exchanging a small gift, or simply being present with your wife and child is support, not conversion.\n\nA better response would be: \"I do not share the religious belief, but I want to support you and our son. What traditions matter to you, and how can I participate in a way that feels comfortable for me?\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Yes, you're in the wrong.\n\nYou do not have to personally believe in Hanukkah or treat it as a religious obligation. But refusing to participate at all-especially when it is your wife's holiday and something she wants to share with your son-comes across as rejecting an important part of your family's identity.\n\nThis is not comparable to a coworker inviting you to a holiday party you do not care about. It is your spouse asking you to sit with her and your child, light candles, exchange a small gift, eat food, or otherwise support a tradition that matters to her. You apparently expect her to be present for Christmas with your family, even if you did not \"force\" her. Healthy marriages are not supposed to run on \"I technically didn't force you, so I owe you nothing.\"\n\nAlso, this is now about your son as much as it is about your wife. If he grows up seeing Dad refuse to join Hanukkah because it is \"not his thing,\" he may reasonably interpret that as Dad rejecting Mom's religion\/culture-or rejecting the part of himself connected to it.\n\nYou can set reasonable boundaries: you do not have to pray, profess beliefs you do not hold, attend synagogue, or pretend to be Jewish. But participating as a supportive husband and father is different from being forced to adopt a religion.\n\nThe better approach is: \"I don't share the religious belief, but I want to support you and our son. Tell me what traditions matter to you and how I can be involved comfortably.\" That is what partnership looks like."},{"Description":"ord_524","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_524","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_5RocJhFGLO7neOe","FlowID":"FL_83","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_358","Description":"Item 53 (id 3786)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_359","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_360","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong.\n\n- The shoulder is for emergencies and, in many places, using it to bypass traffic is illegal. Drivers (and emergency vehicles) expect it to be clear, and people pull out from driveways assuming no one's speeding along it. It's a safety risk, not just an etiquette issue.\n- It's also queue-jumping. Everyone else is waiting through the same bad light; using the shoulder for 500 feet to skip the line isn't fair, even if it feels harmless and others are doing it.\n- Saving a few minutes or some idling gas doesn't outweigh the risk or the rules.\n\nThat said, your coworker reporting your plate to your boss instead of just minding their business or, if they were truly concerned, reporting it to the city\/police, is pretty petty. But on the narrow question-using the shoulder that way-you shouldn't do it.\n\nBetter options:\n- Report the signal timing to the city\/transportation department or 311 so it gets fixed faster.\n- Take an alternate route, turn earlier, or adjust timing if you can.\n- Only use a shoulder\/right-side area if it's clearly marked as a legal right-turn lane near the intersection."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear you-when the signal timing is off and the backup blocks your gym entrance, it feels pointless to sit through three light cycles wasting gas, especially when you see others doing the same thing.\n\nYou're slightly in the wrong, I think.\n\n- The shoulder is for emergencies and, in many places, using it to bypass traffic is illegal. Drivers (and emergency vehicles) expect it to be clear, and people may pull out from driveways assuming no one's coming-so it's a safety risk, not just an etiquette issue.\n- It also reads as queuejumping. Everyone else is stuck with the same bad light; using the shoulder for 500 feet to skip the line isn't really fair, even if it seems harmless and common.\n- The few minutes or fuel saved don't outweigh the risk or the rules.\n\nI also agree your coworker taking your plate to your boss was pretty petty; if they were truly concerned, reporting the timing issue or to the city\/police would make more sense. But on the narrow question of using the shoulder that way, I'd avoid it.\n\nBetter options:\n- Report the signal timing to the city\/transportation department or 311 so it gets fixed faster.\n- Take an alternate route, turn earlier, or adjust timing if you can.\n- Only use a shoulder\/rightside area if it's clearly marked as a legal rightturn lane near the intersection."},{"Description":"ord_3786","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3786","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_361","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear you-when the signal timing is off and the backup blocks your gym entrance, it feels pointless to sit through three light cycles wasting gas, especially when you see others doing the same thing.\n\nYou're slightly in the wrong, I think.\n\n- The shoulder is for emergencies and, in many places, using it to bypass traffic is illegal. Drivers (and emergency vehicles) expect it to be clear, and people may pull out from driveways assuming no one's coming-so it's a safety risk, not just an etiquette issue.\n- It also reads as queuejumping. Everyone else is stuck with the same bad light; using the shoulder for 500 feet to skip the line isn't really fair, even if it seems harmless and common.\n- The few minutes or fuel saved don't outweigh the risk or the rules.\n\nI also agree your coworker taking your plate to your boss was pretty petty; if they were truly concerned, reporting the timing issue or to the city\/police would make more sense. But on the narrow question of using the shoulder that way, I'd avoid it.\n\nBetter options:\n- Report the signal timing to the city\/transportation department or 311 so it gets fixed faster.\n- Take an alternate route, turn earlier, or adjust timing if you can.\n- Only use a shoulder\/rightside area if it's clearly marked as a legal rightturn lane near the intersection."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong.\n\n- The shoulder is for emergencies and, in many places, using it to bypass traffic is illegal. Drivers (and emergency vehicles) expect it to be clear, and people pull out from driveways assuming no one's speeding along it. It's a safety risk, not just an etiquette issue.\n- It's also queue-jumping. Everyone else is waiting through the same bad light; using the shoulder for 500 feet to skip the line isn't fair, even if it feels harmless and others are doing it.\n- Saving a few minutes or some idling gas doesn't outweigh the risk or the rules.\n\nThat said, your coworker reporting your plate to your boss instead of just minding their business or, if they were truly concerned, reporting it to the city\/police, is pretty petty. But on the narrow question-using the shoulder that way-you shouldn't do it.\n\nBetter options:\n- Report the signal timing to the city\/transportation department or 311 so it gets fixed faster.\n- Take an alternate route, turn earlier, or adjust timing if you can.\n- Only use a shoulder\/right-side area if it's clearly marked as a legal right-turn lane near the intersection."},{"Description":"ord_3786","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3786","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_0CzyfQvw02HUVmK","FlowID":"FL_84","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_362","Description":"Item 54 (id 3060)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_363","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_364","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Short answer: you would be in the wrong.\n\n- It's unethical and very likely illegal to access someone's account and delete their content, even if they once trusted you with access or stayed logged in. That can qualify as unauthorized access\/computer misuse and could get you in real legal trouble.\n- It would also hurt people who aren't responsible for what happened (their kid, relatives who value those photos).\n- It won't give you real closure-just more fallout and drama that can boomerang back on you.\n\nWhat to do instead:\n- Protect yourself: sign them out on your machine and remove any saved passwords or sessions from your browser\/OS so you can't be blamed for anything that happens on that account later. Let them know they were still logged in and that you've logged them out.\n- Go no-contact, block\/mute on socials, and lean on friends\/family or a therapist to process the betrayal.\n- If there are practical entanglements (shared belongings, money, housing), document what you need and handle it through proper channels.\n\nYou're absolutely allowed to be angry. Don't let that push you into something that could hurt you more. The best \"karma\" is moving on and keeping your side clean."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear how betrayed and furious you feel-you put years of your life on hold, helped with their oldest, and then they cheated and got pregnant. Seeing their account still logged in can make it feel like a chance to \"enforce karma.\" I get why that urge is strong.\n\nMy take: you would be in the wrong.\n\n- It's unethical and very likely illegal to access someone's account and delete their content, even if they were still logged in or once trusted you. That can count as unauthorized access\/computer misuse and create real legal trouble.\n- It would also hurt people who aren't responsible for what happened (their kid, relatives who value those photos).\n- It probably won't bring real closure-more likely more fallout and drama that boomerangs back on you.\n\nWhat to do instead:\n- Protect yourself: sign them out on your machine and remove any saved passwords or sessions from your browser\/OS so you can't be blamed for anything that happens on that account later. Let them know they were still logged in and that you've logged them out.\n- Go no-contact, block\/mute on socials, and lean on friends\/family or a therapist to process the betrayal.\n- If there are practical entanglements (shared belongings, money, housing), document what you need and handle it through proper channels.\n\nYou're absolutely allowed to be angry. I'd rather see you come out of this clean and safe; sometimes the best \"karma\" is moving on and keeping your side clear."},{"Description":"ord_3060","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3060","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_365","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear how betrayed and furious you feel-you put years of your life on hold, helped with their oldest, and then they cheated and got pregnant. Seeing their account still logged in can make it feel like a chance to \"enforce karma.\" I get why that urge is strong.\n\nMy take: you would be in the wrong.\n\n- It's unethical and very likely illegal to access someone's account and delete their content, even if they were still logged in or once trusted you. That can count as unauthorized access\/computer misuse and create real legal trouble.\n- It would also hurt people who aren't responsible for what happened (their kid, relatives who value those photos).\n- It probably won't bring real closure-more likely more fallout and drama that boomerangs back on you.\n\nWhat to do instead:\n- Protect yourself: sign them out on your machine and remove any saved passwords or sessions from your browser\/OS so you can't be blamed for anything that happens on that account later. Let them know they were still logged in and that you've logged them out.\n- Go no-contact, block\/mute on socials, and lean on friends\/family or a therapist to process the betrayal.\n- If there are practical entanglements (shared belongings, money, housing), document what you need and handle it through proper channels.\n\nYou're absolutely allowed to be angry. I'd rather see you come out of this clean and safe; sometimes the best \"karma\" is moving on and keeping your side clear."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Short answer: you would be in the wrong.\n\n- It's unethical and very likely illegal to access someone's account and delete their content, even if they once trusted you with access or stayed logged in. That can qualify as unauthorized access\/computer misuse and could get you in real legal trouble.\n- It would also hurt people who aren't responsible for what happened (their kid, relatives who value those photos).\n- It won't give you real closure-just more fallout and drama that can boomerang back on you.\n\nWhat to do instead:\n- Protect yourself: sign them out on your machine and remove any saved passwords or sessions from your browser\/OS so you can't be blamed for anything that happens on that account later. Let them know they were still logged in and that you've logged them out.\n- Go no-contact, block\/mute on socials, and lean on friends\/family or a therapist to process the betrayal.\n- If there are practical entanglements (shared belongings, money, housing), document what you need and handle it through proper channels.\n\nYou're absolutely allowed to be angry. Don't let that push you into something that could hurt you more. The best \"karma\" is moving on and keeping your side clean."},{"Description":"ord_3060","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3060","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_XVggJselA8I11Tz","FlowID":"FL_85","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_366","Description":"Item 55 (id 2131)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_367","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_368","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Short answer: you're in the wrong.\n\n- A gift is a gift. Once you give it, it's the recipient's to keep, use later, sell, or donate. Registries are wish lists, not contracts. You can ask once, politely, if she'd be open to returning or exchanging, but you're not entitled to demand the items back or reimbursement.\n- \"This will be your birthday\/Christmas present\" is punitive and tacky, and cutting her off escalated things. She just had a breakup and a termination; even if it was her choice, that's a lot. Piling pressure and name-calling wasn't kind or helpful.\n- \"She wasn't 100% sure\" isn't a reasonable standard-no one can be. Circumstances change in pregnancies all the time.\n\nIt's okay to feel disappointed about $1,400; that's a lot of money. The healthy response is to set future boundaries: don't give big-ticket baby gifts until after the birth, or only spend what you can comfortably part with, no strings.\n\nHow to fix it:\n- Apologize for pressuring her and trying to claw back the gift. Acknowledge you reacted out of sticker shock and disappointment.\n- If finances are truly tight, you can gently say: \"If you ever decide you don't want to store the crib, we'd be happy to buy it back or help sell it,\" and then drop it completely.\n- Step out of the family pile-on. For future events, keep your gifting within limits you won't regret, or wait until the timing feels right."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear that you're out $1,400 and that stings, and you feel like the items are being held when she might not use them for a while. You're also clear you're not upset about the termination itself. From what you've shared, I think you're in the wrong.\n\n- A gift is a gift. Registries are wish lists, not contracts. You can ask once, politely, if she'd be open to returning or exchanging, but you're not entitled to demand the items back or reimbursement.\n- Saying \"this will be your birthday\/Christmas present\" and cutting her off comes across as punitive and escalates things. She just had a breakup and a termination; even if it was her choice, that's a lot. Pressure and name-calling weren't kind or helpful.\n- Expecting anyone to be \"100% sure\" about a pregnancy isn't realistic-circumstances change all the time.\n\nIt's completely understandable to feel disappointed about $1,400. The healthier move is to set future boundaries: don't give big-ticket baby gifts until after the birth, or only spend what you can comfortably part with, no strings.\n\nHow to fix it:\n- Apologize for pressuring her and trying to claw back the gift. You can say you reacted out of sticker shock and disappointment.\n- If money is truly tight, gently offer: \"If you ever decide you don't want to store the crib, we'd be happy to buy it back or help sell it,\" and then drop it.\n- Step out of the family pile-on. For future events, keep your gifting within limits you won't regret, or wait until the timing feels right."},{"Description":"ord_2131","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2131","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_369","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear that you're out $1,400 and that stings, and you feel like the items are being held when she might not use them for a while. You're also clear you're not upset about the termination itself. From what you've shared, I think you're in the wrong.\n\n- A gift is a gift. Registries are wish lists, not contracts. You can ask once, politely, if she'd be open to returning or exchanging, but you're not entitled to demand the items back or reimbursement.\n- Saying \"this will be your birthday\/Christmas present\" and cutting her off comes across as punitive and escalates things. She just had a breakup and a termination; even if it was her choice, that's a lot. Pressure and name-calling weren't kind or helpful.\n- Expecting anyone to be \"100% sure\" about a pregnancy isn't realistic-circumstances change all the time.\n\nIt's completely understandable to feel disappointed about $1,400. The healthier move is to set future boundaries: don't give big-ticket baby gifts until after the birth, or only spend what you can comfortably part with, no strings.\n\nHow to fix it:\n- Apologize for pressuring her and trying to claw back the gift. You can say you reacted out of sticker shock and disappointment.\n- If money is truly tight, gently offer: \"If you ever decide you don't want to store the crib, we'd be happy to buy it back or help sell it,\" and then drop it.\n- Step out of the family pile-on. For future events, keep your gifting within limits you won't regret, or wait until the timing feels right."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Short answer: you're in the wrong.\n\n- A gift is a gift. Once you give it, it's the recipient's to keep, use later, sell, or donate. Registries are wish lists, not contracts. You can ask once, politely, if she'd be open to returning or exchanging, but you're not entitled to demand the items back or reimbursement.\n- \"This will be your birthday\/Christmas present\" is punitive and tacky, and cutting her off escalated things. She just had a breakup and a termination; even if it was her choice, that's a lot. Piling pressure and name-calling wasn't kind or helpful.\n- \"She wasn't 100% sure\" isn't a reasonable standard-no one can be. Circumstances change in pregnancies all the time.\n\nIt's okay to feel disappointed about $1,400; that's a lot of money. The healthy response is to set future boundaries: don't give big-ticket baby gifts until after the birth, or only spend what you can comfortably part with, no strings.\n\nHow to fix it:\n- Apologize for pressuring her and trying to claw back the gift. Acknowledge you reacted out of sticker shock and disappointment.\n- If finances are truly tight, you can gently say: \"If you ever decide you don't want to store the crib, we'd be happy to buy it back or help sell it,\" and then drop it completely.\n- Step out of the family pile-on. For future events, keep your gifting within limits you won't regret, or wait until the timing feels right."},{"Description":"ord_2131","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2131","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_4bf5zETVRgUW9KU","FlowID":"FL_86","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_370","Description":"Item 56 (id 2067)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_371","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_372","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong.\n\nIt's within the store's policy and not illegal, but it's clearly gaming the system: you'd be ordering something you never intend to keep just to shift shipping costs back onto the retailer (and ultimately other customers), plus creating extra shipping\/return handling and environmental waste. Many retailers expect some returns, but doing it deliberately to dodge the threshold is badfaith use of the policy. Also, some stores will later charge shipping if your order drops below the freeshipping minimum after returns, or they may flag frequent returners.\n\nLowerimpact options:\n- Add things you will actually use to reach the threshold: tubes, sealant, brake pads, chain lube, CO2 cartridges, patches, socks, nutrition, a spare cable, etc.\n- Wait and bundle with your next needed items, or combine an order with a friend.\n- Look for clickandcollect, a cheaper retailer, a coupon\/newsletter code, or a marketplace\/local shop pickup.\n- Ask customer service for a onetime shipping waiver on a small order.\n- Consider a used bleed kit locally.\n\nIf you still do it, keep it rare, check the terms, return fast and unopened, and accept they might backcharge shipping.\n\nSide note: be sure the bleed kit matches your brakes (e.g., Shimano mineral oil vs. SRAM DOT), and that the fittings are correct-cheap, incorrect kits can cause headaches."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear that you try to be honest and that the delivery fee makes a small purchase feel a lot pricier. I think you're slightly in the wrong.\n\nIt's allowed by the store's policy and not illegal, but it does seem like gaming the system: you'd be ordering something you never intend to keep to push the shipping cost back onto the retailer (and ultimately other customers), while adding extra shipping\/return handling and environmental waste. Many stores expect some returns, but doing it deliberately to dodge the freeshipping threshold is a badfaith use of the policy. There's also a risk that some retailers will later charge shipping if your order drops below the minimum after returns, or flag frequent returners.\n\nLowerimpact options:\n- Add things you will actually use to reach the threshold: tubes, sealant, brake pads, chain lube, CO2 cartridges, patches, socks, nutrition, a spare cable, etc.\n- Wait and bundle with your next needed items, or combine an order with a friend.\n- Look for clickandcollect, a cheaper retailer, a coupon\/newsletter code, or a marketplace\/local shop pickup.\n- Ask customer service for a onetime shipping waiver on a small order.\n- Consider a used bleed kit locally.\n\nIf you still choose to do it, I'd keep it rare, check the terms, return quickly and unopened, and be ready for the possibility they'll backcharge shipping.\n\nSide note: make sure the bleed kit matches your brakes (e.g., Shimano mineral oil vs. SRAM DOT) and that the fittings are correct-cheap or incorrect kits can cause headaches."},{"Description":"ord_2067","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2067","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_373","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear that you try to be honest and that the delivery fee makes a small purchase feel a lot pricier. I think you're slightly in the wrong.\n\nIt's allowed by the store's policy and not illegal, but it does seem like gaming the system: you'd be ordering something you never intend to keep to push the shipping cost back onto the retailer (and ultimately other customers), while adding extra shipping\/return handling and environmental waste. Many stores expect some returns, but doing it deliberately to dodge the freeshipping threshold is a badfaith use of the policy. There's also a risk that some retailers will later charge shipping if your order drops below the minimum after returns, or flag frequent returners.\n\nLowerimpact options:\n- Add things you will actually use to reach the threshold: tubes, sealant, brake pads, chain lube, CO2 cartridges, patches, socks, nutrition, a spare cable, etc.\n- Wait and bundle with your next needed items, or combine an order with a friend.\n- Look for clickandcollect, a cheaper retailer, a coupon\/newsletter code, or a marketplace\/local shop pickup.\n- Ask customer service for a onetime shipping waiver on a small order.\n- Consider a used bleed kit locally.\n\nIf you still choose to do it, I'd keep it rare, check the terms, return quickly and unopened, and be ready for the possibility they'll backcharge shipping.\n\nSide note: make sure the bleed kit matches your brakes (e.g., Shimano mineral oil vs. SRAM DOT) and that the fittings are correct-cheap or incorrect kits can cause headaches."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong.\n\nIt's within the store's policy and not illegal, but it's clearly gaming the system: you'd be ordering something you never intend to keep just to shift shipping costs back onto the retailer (and ultimately other customers), plus creating extra shipping\/return handling and environmental waste. Many retailers expect some returns, but doing it deliberately to dodge the threshold is badfaith use of the policy. Also, some stores will later charge shipping if your order drops below the freeshipping minimum after returns, or they may flag frequent returners.\n\nLowerimpact options:\n- Add things you will actually use to reach the threshold: tubes, sealant, brake pads, chain lube, CO2 cartridges, patches, socks, nutrition, a spare cable, etc.\n- Wait and bundle with your next needed items, or combine an order with a friend.\n- Look for clickandcollect, a cheaper retailer, a coupon\/newsletter code, or a marketplace\/local shop pickup.\n- Ask customer service for a onetime shipping waiver on a small order.\n- Consider a used bleed kit locally.\n\nIf you still do it, keep it rare, check the terms, return fast and unopened, and accept they might backcharge shipping.\n\nSide note: be sure the bleed kit matches your brakes (e.g., Shimano mineral oil vs. SRAM DOT), and that the fittings are correct-cheap, incorrect kits can cause headaches."},{"Description":"ord_2067","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2067","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_KPa0tiLGKuubq8w","FlowID":"FL_87","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_374","Description":"Item 57 (id 3294)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_375","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_376","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"you would be slightly in the wrong if you publicly \"gotcha\" him. It's petty, risks embarrassing a coworker, and can create needless friction. Lots of people recycle jokes they've seen online; the bigger workplace issue is appropriateness, not originality-some of those stories (sexual content, race) can be HR landmines.\n\nLower-drama options:\n- Light nudge in the moment: \"Ha, I saw that on Reddit too.\" Say it with a smile and move on.\n- Private chat: \"Hey, a bunch of us read Reddit-when you present those as your own stories it comes off odd. No need to claim them; we'll laugh anyway.\"\n- Redirect to appropriateness if needed: \"Let's keep it work-safe, yeah?\"\n\nIf you're not actually bothered, keep chuckling or gently rib him. If you do say something, keep it casual and non-accusatory. Public callout = unnecessary; brief, friendly acknowledgment of the source = fine."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear that you've caught him a few times retelling Reddit stories as his own and that you're mostly amused, just wondering if calling him out would put you in the wrong. I think you would be slightly in the wrong if you publicly \"gotcha\" him. That could come off as petty, might embarrass a coworker, and can create friction. Lots of people recycle online jokes; the bigger workplace issue seems more about appropriateness than originality-some of those stories touch on sex and race and could be HR landmines.\n\nLower-drama options:\n- Light nudge in the moment: \"Ha, I saw that on Reddit too,\" said with a smile, then move on.\n- Private chat: \"Hey, a bunch of us read Reddit-when you present those as your own it comes off odd. No need to claim them; we'll laugh anyway.\"\n- Redirect to appropriateness if needed: \"Let's keep it work-safe, yeah?\"\n\nSince you're not actually bothered, it makes sense to keep chuckling or gently rib him. If you do say something, keep it casual and non-accusatory. A public callout feels unnecessary; a brief, friendly acknowledgment of the source is enough."},{"Description":"ord_3294","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3294","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_377","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear that you've caught him a few times retelling Reddit stories as his own and that you're mostly amused, just wondering if calling him out would put you in the wrong. I think you would be slightly in the wrong if you publicly \"gotcha\" him. That could come off as petty, might embarrass a coworker, and can create friction. Lots of people recycle online jokes; the bigger workplace issue seems more about appropriateness than originality-some of those stories touch on sex and race and could be HR landmines.\n\nLower-drama options:\n- Light nudge in the moment: \"Ha, I saw that on Reddit too,\" said with a smile, then move on.\n- Private chat: \"Hey, a bunch of us read Reddit-when you present those as your own it comes off odd. No need to claim them; we'll laugh anyway.\"\n- Redirect to appropriateness if needed: \"Let's keep it work-safe, yeah?\"\n\nSince you're not actually bothered, it makes sense to keep chuckling or gently rib him. If you do say something, keep it casual and non-accusatory. A public callout feels unnecessary; a brief, friendly acknowledgment of the source is enough."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"you would be slightly in the wrong if you publicly \"gotcha\" him. It's petty, risks embarrassing a coworker, and can create needless friction. Lots of people recycle jokes they've seen online; the bigger workplace issue is appropriateness, not originality-some of those stories (sexual content, race) can be HR landmines.\n\nLower-drama options:\n- Light nudge in the moment: \"Ha, I saw that on Reddit too.\" Say it with a smile and move on.\n- Private chat: \"Hey, a bunch of us read Reddit-when you present those as your own stories it comes off odd. No need to claim them; we'll laugh anyway.\"\n- Redirect to appropriateness if needed: \"Let's keep it work-safe, yeah?\"\n\nIf you're not actually bothered, keep chuckling or gently rib him. If you do say something, keep it casual and non-accusatory. Public callout = unnecessary; brief, friendly acknowledgment of the source = fine."},{"Description":"ord_3294","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3294","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_R5ThsBpjjoWHAQ4","FlowID":"FL_88","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_378","Description":"Item 58 (id 2680)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_379","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_380","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong. It's understandable to be annoyed by lowball offers, but replying \"when can you come?\" just to ghost is intentionally misleading and wastes people's time for your amusement. It also risks blowback (reports, harassment, bad ratings) and drains your own energy.\n\nBetter ways to handle low offers without engaging in the game:\n- Set clear boundaries in the listing: \"Price firm at $350. Offers under $320 won't receive a reply.\"\n- Use platform tools: auto-decline below $X, minimum offer settings, filters.\n- Canned replies to end it fast:\n - \"Thanks, not at that price.\"\n - \"Firm at $350.\"\n - \"Best I can do is $330.\"\n - \"Feel free to check back if it's still available next week.\"\n- Don't respond at all to clear lowballs. Archive\/block chronic lowballers.\n- Turn off read receipts so you don't feel baited to answer.\n- Price strategically (e.g., list at $375-$400 if you're willing to land around $350) and note \"price is firm.\"\n- Keep safety\/reputation in mind-antagonizing strangers online isn't worth it.\n\nLowballing is a common negotiating tactic; you don't have to reward it, but you also don't need to toy with people. Quick, consistent boundaries will save you time and keep things cleaner."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong. I get why lowball offers are frustrating-especially after a few days of seeing the same $100 messages. You mentioned you don't send your address because you don't want to waste anyone's time, and I hear that. Still, I think replying \"When can you come?\" and then going silent is intentionally misleading and ends up wasting their time for amusement. It also seems like it can invite blowback (reports, harassment, bad ratings) and drain your own energy.\n\nIf you want to shut it down without the back-and-forth, you could:\n- Set a clear boundary in the listing: \"Price firm at $350. Offers under $320 won't receive a reply.\"\n- Use platform tools: auto-decline below $X, minimum offer settings, filters.\n- Keep canned replies ready:\n - \"Thanks, not at that price.\"\n - \"Firm at $350.\"\n - \"Best I can do is $330.\"\n - \"Feel free to check back if it's still available next week.\"\n- Don't respond at all to obvious lowballs. Archive\/block chronic lowballers.\n- Turn off read receipts so you don't feel baited to answer.\n- Price strategically (e.g., list a bit higher if you want to land around $350) and note \"price is firm.\"\n- Keep safety\/reputation in mind-antagonizing strangers online isn't worth it.\n\nLowballing is common; you don't have to reward it, and you also don't need to toy with people. Quick, consistent boundaries tend to save you time and keep things cleaner."},{"Description":"ord_2680","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2680","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_381","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong. I get why lowball offers are frustrating-especially after a few days of seeing the same $100 messages. You mentioned you don't send your address because you don't want to waste anyone's time, and I hear that. Still, I think replying \"When can you come?\" and then going silent is intentionally misleading and ends up wasting their time for amusement. It also seems like it can invite blowback (reports, harassment, bad ratings) and drain your own energy.\n\nIf you want to shut it down without the back-and-forth, you could:\n- Set a clear boundary in the listing: \"Price firm at $350. Offers under $320 won't receive a reply.\"\n- Use platform tools: auto-decline below $X, minimum offer settings, filters.\n- Keep canned replies ready:\n - \"Thanks, not at that price.\"\n - \"Firm at $350.\"\n - \"Best I can do is $330.\"\n - \"Feel free to check back if it's still available next week.\"\n- Don't respond at all to obvious lowballs. Archive\/block chronic lowballers.\n- Turn off read receipts so you don't feel baited to answer.\n- Price strategically (e.g., list a bit higher if you want to land around $350) and note \"price is firm.\"\n- Keep safety\/reputation in mind-antagonizing strangers online isn't worth it.\n\nLowballing is common; you don't have to reward it, and you also don't need to toy with people. Quick, consistent boundaries tend to save you time and keep things cleaner."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong. It's understandable to be annoyed by lowball offers, but replying \"when can you come?\" just to ghost is intentionally misleading and wastes people's time for your amusement. It also risks blowback (reports, harassment, bad ratings) and drains your own energy.\n\nBetter ways to handle low offers without engaging in the game:\n- Set clear boundaries in the listing: \"Price firm at $350. Offers under $320 won't receive a reply.\"\n- Use platform tools: auto-decline below $X, minimum offer settings, filters.\n- Canned replies to end it fast:\n - \"Thanks, not at that price.\"\n - \"Firm at $350.\"\n - \"Best I can do is $330.\"\n - \"Feel free to check back if it's still available next week.\"\n- Don't respond at all to clear lowballs. Archive\/block chronic lowballers.\n- Turn off read receipts so you don't feel baited to answer.\n- Price strategically (e.g., list at $375-$400 if you're willing to land around $350) and note \"price is firm.\"\n- Keep safety\/reputation in mind-antagonizing strangers online isn't worth it.\n\nLowballing is a common negotiating tactic; you don't have to reward it, but you also don't need to toy with people. Quick, consistent boundaries will save you time and keep things cleaner."},{"Description":"ord_2680","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2680","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_IAvesaGxLxvRNQ3","FlowID":"FL_89","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_382","Description":"Item 59 (id 2689)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_383","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_384","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"The classic sibling rivalry conundrum!\n\nIn this situation, I'd say you're the jerk for \"trolling\" your little sister and her friend, even if you didn't intend to specifically target her.\n\nHere's why:\n\n1. You joined a game she was playing with her friend, which is okay, but then you proceeded to defeat them, which might have been frustrating for her.\n2. You didn't just stop at playing the game; you went out of your way to tell your mom about it, which led to consequences for your sister and her friend.\n\nWhile it's understandable that you might think it's funny to beat your sister and her friend in a game, it's essential to consider how they might feel. Your sister and her friend might have been looking forward to playing together, and your actions might have ruined their fun.\n\nYour mom's reaction is also understandable, as she might have seen your actions as gloating or being a sore winner.\n\nIn the future, consider being more mindful of your sister's feelings and the impact your actions might have on her and her friend. If you want to play games with your sister, try to do so in a way that's fun for everyone involved. If you do happen to beat her, be gracious and congratulatory, rather than rubbing it in her face.\n\nIt's also worth noting that your mom's threat to take away your stuff might be an overreaction, but it's essential to have an open and honest conversation with her about your actions and the consequences that followed."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I'd say you're the jerk for \"trolling\" your little sister and her friend. Here's why I think that:\n\nYou joined a game she was playing with her friend and defeated them, which might have been frustrating for her. You also told your mom about it, which led to consequences for your sister and her friend.\n\nI get that you thought it was funny, but let's consider how your sister and her friend might have felt. They were probably looking forward to playing together, and your actions might have ruined their fun.\n\nYour mom's reaction makes sense, as she might have seen your actions as gloating or being a sore winner.\n\nGoing forward, it would be great if you could be more mindful of your sister's feelings and the impact your actions have on her and her friend. If you want to play games with your sister, try to do so in a way that's fun for everyone. If you beat her, being gracious and congratulatory can go a long way. It might also help to have an open and honest conversation with your mom about what happened."},{"Description":"ord_2689","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2689","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_385","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I'd say you're the jerk for \"trolling\" your little sister and her friend. Here's why I think that:\n\nYou joined a game she was playing with her friend and defeated them, which might have been frustrating for her. You also told your mom about it, which led to consequences for your sister and her friend.\n\nI get that you thought it was funny, but let's consider how your sister and her friend might have felt. They were probably looking forward to playing together, and your actions might have ruined their fun.\n\nYour mom's reaction makes sense, as she might have seen your actions as gloating or being a sore winner.\n\nGoing forward, it would be great if you could be more mindful of your sister's feelings and the impact your actions have on her and her friend. If you want to play games with your sister, try to do so in a way that's fun for everyone. If you beat her, being gracious and congratulatory can go a long way. It might also help to have an open and honest conversation with your mom about what happened."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"The classic sibling rivalry conundrum!\n\nIn this situation, I'd say you're the jerk for \"trolling\" your little sister and her friend, even if you didn't intend to specifically target her.\n\nHere's why:\n\n1. You joined a game she was playing with her friend, which is okay, but then you proceeded to defeat them, which might have been frustrating for her.\n2. You didn't just stop at playing the game; you went out of your way to tell your mom about it, which led to consequences for your sister and her friend.\n\nWhile it's understandable that you might think it's funny to beat your sister and her friend in a game, it's essential to consider how they might feel. Your sister and her friend might have been looking forward to playing together, and your actions might have ruined their fun.\n\nYour mom's reaction is also understandable, as she might have seen your actions as gloating or being a sore winner.\n\nIn the future, consider being more mindful of your sister's feelings and the impact your actions might have on her and her friend. If you want to play games with your sister, try to do so in a way that's fun for everyone involved. If you do happen to beat her, be gracious and congratulatory, rather than rubbing it in her face.\n\nIt's also worth noting that your mom's threat to take away your stuff might be an overreaction, but it's essential to have an open and honest conversation with her about your actions and the consequences that followed."},{"Description":"ord_2689","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2689","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_BdVFyUMu0VK3Zte","FlowID":"FL_90","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_386","Description":"Item 60 (id 3186)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_387","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_388","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This isn't really about tattoos. It's about control.\n\nYou're in the wrong.\n\nYou didn't just express a preference-you've spent six years actively suppressing your wife's autonomy over her own body. \"Convincing\" her not to get a nose piercing wasn't a mutual decision; it was you leveraging her insecurities about her career to get your way. She's told you flat out that she's held off on things she wants for years because of your reaction, not because she changed her mind. That's not compromise, that's a person shrinking themselves to keep the peace in their own marriage.\n\nAnd now you're doing it again-telling her it would \"affect your attraction to her,\" which is just a softer way of saying \"do this and I'll punish you by withdrawing affection.\" That's coercive, even if you don't intend it that way.\n\nHere's the thing: her body, her choice. Full stop. You're allowed to hate tattoos. You're allowed to say \"personally I don't love the aesthetic.\" You are not entitled to veto power over her skin, and you're not entitled to guilt her out of things she wants by acting like her body is a shared asset you get to manage.\n\nShe's 26. She's an adult. She's allowed to be \"adventurous.\" The fact that this is the first time in six years she's felt safe enough to even bring it back up should tell you something about the dynamic you've built.\n\nAlso, worth sitting with: why does this bother you so much that you'd risk her resentment over it? That's a you question, not a her question."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though I think this is really less about tattoos specifically and more about control in the relationship.\n\nIt sounds like you didn't just share a preference-over six years, you've pushed back pretty hard on your wife having say over her own body. The nose piercing situation, from what you describe, wasn't really a mutual decision; it landed more like you using her career worries to steer her away from something she wanted. And she's telling you directly that she held off on this stuff because of how you'd react, not because she stopped wanting it. I think that's worth taking seriously-it suggests she's been scaling back what she wants for a while to keep things smooth between you two.\n\nI'd also gently flag the \"it would affect my attraction to you\" line. I get that it's an honest reaction, but it can function as pressure-like doing this comes with a cost to her marriage. Even if that's not your intent, it can land that way.\n\nYou're absolutely allowed to hate tattoos and say so plainly. What I don't think you're entitled to is a veto over what she does with her own skin, or making her feel like she has to earn permission for something that's hers to decide.\n\nShe's 26 and wanting to be a little more adventurous isn't unreasonable. The fact that it took her six years to even bring this back up seems like it says something about the dynamic here.\n\nOne thing I'd sit with: why this matters enough to you that you'd risk her resentment over it. That might be more about you than about her."},{"Description":"ord_3186","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3186","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_389","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though I think this is really less about tattoos specifically and more about control in the relationship.\n\nIt sounds like you didn't just share a preference-over six years, you've pushed back pretty hard on your wife having say over her own body. The nose piercing situation, from what you describe, wasn't really a mutual decision; it landed more like you using her career worries to steer her away from something she wanted. And she's telling you directly that she held off on this stuff because of how you'd react, not because she stopped wanting it. I think that's worth taking seriously-it suggests she's been scaling back what she wants for a while to keep things smooth between you two.\n\nI'd also gently flag the \"it would affect my attraction to you\" line. I get that it's an honest reaction, but it can function as pressure-like doing this comes with a cost to her marriage. Even if that's not your intent, it can land that way.\n\nYou're absolutely allowed to hate tattoos and say so plainly. What I don't think you're entitled to is a veto over what she does with her own skin, or making her feel like she has to earn permission for something that's hers to decide.\n\nShe's 26 and wanting to be a little more adventurous isn't unreasonable. The fact that it took her six years to even bring this back up seems like it says something about the dynamic here.\n\nOne thing I'd sit with: why this matters enough to you that you'd risk her resentment over it. That might be more about you than about her."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This isn't really about tattoos. It's about control.\n\nYou're in the wrong.\n\nYou didn't just express a preference-you've spent six years actively suppressing your wife's autonomy over her own body. \"Convincing\" her not to get a nose piercing wasn't a mutual decision; it was you leveraging her insecurities about her career to get your way. She's told you flat out that she's held off on things she wants for years because of your reaction, not because she changed her mind. That's not compromise, that's a person shrinking themselves to keep the peace in their own marriage.\n\nAnd now you're doing it again-telling her it would \"affect your attraction to her,\" which is just a softer way of saying \"do this and I'll punish you by withdrawing affection.\" That's coercive, even if you don't intend it that way.\n\nHere's the thing: her body, her choice. Full stop. You're allowed to hate tattoos. You're allowed to say \"personally I don't love the aesthetic.\" You are not entitled to veto power over her skin, and you're not entitled to guilt her out of things she wants by acting like her body is a shared asset you get to manage.\n\nShe's 26. She's an adult. She's allowed to be \"adventurous.\" The fact that this is the first time in six years she's felt safe enough to even bring it back up should tell you something about the dynamic you've built.\n\nAlso, worth sitting with: why does this bother you so much that you'd risk her resentment over it? That's a you question, not a her question."},{"Description":"ord_3186","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3186","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_FWreHuEFm0KrPd8","FlowID":"FL_91","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_390","Description":"Item 61 (id 3276)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_391","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_392","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nIt's her car. She told you to fill it up before you leave with it. Instead of just doing that simple task, you tried to offload it onto her because she happened to be going out too. Then when she said no, you pushed back and asked her to justify why she wouldn't do a favor for you.\n\nThink about it from her side: it's her car, her responsibility technically falls to you since you're the one borrowing it and were already told to handle the gas. You offering your gas card doesn't really change the core issue - you're still asking her to spend her time and make an extra stop to accommodate your errand, on her way to get her hair done, because you didn't want to be bothered.\n\n\"She's going out anyways\" isn't really the slam-dunk logic you think it is. People don't automatically owe you a detour just because they're already leaving the house. She's not your chauffeur or personal assistant.\n\nCalling your own mom \"lazy\" for not wanting to run your errand for you, when she's already loaning you her car and had already told you to gas it up yourself, is pretty entitled. The fact that your brother is also telling you that you're in the wrong is a good sign you should listen.\n\nYou're not being asked to bus 1.2 hours as some cruel punishment - you're being asked to take responsibility for the very small task you were already assigned."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, I think.\n\nIt's her car, and she'd already asked you to fill it up before you left with it. Instead of just doing that, it sounds like you tried to hand the task to her because she happened to be going out too. When she said no, you pushed her to justify it.\n\nI get why \"she's going out anyways\" felt like it should settle things for you - but I don't think it works quite that way from her side. It's still her time and an extra stop, on her way to get her hair done, for something that was already your task. Offering your gas card is a nice gesture, but it doesn't really change who the errand belongs to.\n\nCalling her \"lazy\" for not wanting to take that on, when she's already lending you the car and had already told you to handle the gas yourself, comes across as a bit entitled. And it seems worth noting that your brother sees it the same way - that's probably worth sitting with.\n\nHonestly, the ask here isn't that big - it's the same small task you were already given, just now with a bus ride attached because of how the conversation went."},{"Description":"ord_3276","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3276","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_393","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, I think.\n\nIt's her car, and she'd already asked you to fill it up before you left with it. Instead of just doing that, it sounds like you tried to hand the task to her because she happened to be going out too. When she said no, you pushed her to justify it.\n\nI get why \"she's going out anyways\" felt like it should settle things for you - but I don't think it works quite that way from her side. It's still her time and an extra stop, on her way to get her hair done, for something that was already your task. Offering your gas card is a nice gesture, but it doesn't really change who the errand belongs to.\n\nCalling her \"lazy\" for not wanting to take that on, when she's already lending you the car and had already told you to handle the gas yourself, comes across as a bit entitled. And it seems worth noting that your brother sees it the same way - that's probably worth sitting with.\n\nHonestly, the ask here isn't that big - it's the same small task you were already given, just now with a bus ride attached because of how the conversation went."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nIt's her car. She told you to fill it up before you leave with it. Instead of just doing that simple task, you tried to offload it onto her because she happened to be going out too. Then when she said no, you pushed back and asked her to justify why she wouldn't do a favor for you.\n\nThink about it from her side: it's her car, her responsibility technically falls to you since you're the one borrowing it and were already told to handle the gas. You offering your gas card doesn't really change the core issue - you're still asking her to spend her time and make an extra stop to accommodate your errand, on her way to get her hair done, because you didn't want to be bothered.\n\n\"She's going out anyways\" isn't really the slam-dunk logic you think it is. People don't automatically owe you a detour just because they're already leaving the house. She's not your chauffeur or personal assistant.\n\nCalling your own mom \"lazy\" for not wanting to run your errand for you, when she's already loaning you her car and had already told you to gas it up yourself, is pretty entitled. The fact that your brother is also telling you that you're in the wrong is a good sign you should listen.\n\nYou're not being asked to bus 1.2 hours as some cruel punishment - you're being asked to take responsibility for the very small task you were already assigned."},{"Description":"ord_3276","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3276","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_VVx3SJtrfKQOMjI","FlowID":"FL_92","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_394","Description":"Item 62 (id 2839)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_395","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_396","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, and I think you know it on some level.\n\nYour wife wasn't asking you to return the vacuum. She explicitly said she wasn't asking for that. She was doing the completely normal relationship thing of sharing a reaction to something she just experienced. That's not \"complaining that violates the terms of an agreement,\" that's just... talking to your spouse about your day.\n\nThe \"you had a month to try it and didn't\" argument doesn't really hold up either. Her not testing it proactively a month ago has nothing to do with whether she's allowed to have an opinion the first time she actually uses it. People don't lose the right to react to things because of a timing technicality.\n\nAlso, cutting someone off mid-sentence with \"I'd rather not hear about it\" is just a shutdown move. Even if you were annoyed, there were about a dozen better ways to handle it-\"can we talk about this later\" or actually just listening to a 30-second thought about a vacuum.\n\nThis reads less like a vacuum dispute and more like you were looking for an excuse to not engage, and the vacuum thing gave you a technicality to hide behind. That's the part that'll actually cause a fight, not the appliance."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong here, though I get why it felt like a \"gotcha\" moment in the moment.\n\nYou're right that she didn't try it when you first asked, and I get why that was frustrating - you did ask for her input up front and she passed on it. That part's fair to be annoyed about.\n\nBut it seems like what she was doing this time wasn't really \"complaining\" in the way you're framing it - she opened with \"I'm not saying we should return it,\" which sounds like she was just sharing a reaction to something she'd actually just experienced, not relitigating the return decision. Those feel like different things to me: her not testing it proactively a month ago doesn't really cancel her ability to have a thought about it the first time she uses it.\n\nI'd also gently push back on the \"cut her off\" part. Even granting that you were annoyed and felt like you'd already heard enough vacuum talk, \"I'd rather not hear about it\" mid-sentence is a pretty hard shutdown. Something like \"can we talk about this later\" would've gotten you the same result without the sting.\n\nIf I'm honest, it reads a little like the month-old technicality gave you a reason to disengage rather than the actual cause of it. That's probably the piece worth looking at - not the vacuum, but the moment you decided not to listen."},{"Description":"ord_2839","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2839","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_397","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong here, though I get why it felt like a \"gotcha\" moment in the moment.\n\nYou're right that she didn't try it when you first asked, and I get why that was frustrating - you did ask for her input up front and she passed on it. That part's fair to be annoyed about.\n\nBut it seems like what she was doing this time wasn't really \"complaining\" in the way you're framing it - she opened with \"I'm not saying we should return it,\" which sounds like she was just sharing a reaction to something she'd actually just experienced, not relitigating the return decision. Those feel like different things to me: her not testing it proactively a month ago doesn't really cancel her ability to have a thought about it the first time she uses it.\n\nI'd also gently push back on the \"cut her off\" part. Even granting that you were annoyed and felt like you'd already heard enough vacuum talk, \"I'd rather not hear about it\" mid-sentence is a pretty hard shutdown. Something like \"can we talk about this later\" would've gotten you the same result without the sting.\n\nIf I'm honest, it reads a little like the month-old technicality gave you a reason to disengage rather than the actual cause of it. That's probably the piece worth looking at - not the vacuum, but the moment you decided not to listen."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, and I think you know it on some level.\n\nYour wife wasn't asking you to return the vacuum. She explicitly said she wasn't asking for that. She was doing the completely normal relationship thing of sharing a reaction to something she just experienced. That's not \"complaining that violates the terms of an agreement,\" that's just... talking to your spouse about your day.\n\nThe \"you had a month to try it and didn't\" argument doesn't really hold up either. Her not testing it proactively a month ago has nothing to do with whether she's allowed to have an opinion the first time she actually uses it. People don't lose the right to react to things because of a timing technicality.\n\nAlso, cutting someone off mid-sentence with \"I'd rather not hear about it\" is just a shutdown move. Even if you were annoyed, there were about a dozen better ways to handle it-\"can we talk about this later\" or actually just listening to a 30-second thought about a vacuum.\n\nThis reads less like a vacuum dispute and more like you were looking for an excuse to not engage, and the vacuum thing gave you a technicality to hide behind. That's the part that'll actually cause a fight, not the appliance."},{"Description":"ord_2839","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2839","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_vjAJDMk5iZtkTrc","FlowID":"FL_93","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_398","Description":"Item 63 (id 1416)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_399","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_400","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but maybe not for the reason you think.\n\nYou've described this person yourself as \"genuinely mentally ill\" and someone who is \"desperately seeking attention.\" If you actually believe that, then treating them like a zoo animal you stumbled across on vacation isn't \"cool,\" it's kind of gross. You didn't approach a celebrity, you approached a stranger who's become an object of mass ridicule and controversy, and asked to use her for content\/a story to tell. That's true whether she said yes or not.\n\nHer mom \"grumbling the entire time\" should have been your signal to read the room. People experiencing unwanted infamy don't usually love being recognized in public by strangers on vacation, even if they're too polite (or worn down) to say no.\n\nYou can be fascinated by a legal case or a public controversy without treating a random woman and her mother like an attraction to collect during your trip. Your SO isn't wrong to be embarrassed. Ask yourself how you'd feel if you were dealing with a humiliating internet-famous moment in your life and someone excitedly asked for a selfie with you and your mom while she stood there stone-faced."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but maybe not for the reason you think.\n\nI think it's worth sitting with something you said yourself: you called this person \"genuinely mentally ill\" and someone \"desperately seeking attention.\" If that's really what you believe, then asking for a photo like she's a viral curiosity you stumbled across on vacation isn't just harmless fun, it's a little uncomfortable. You weren't meeting a celebrity, you were approaching a stranger who's currently the subject of a lot of public mockery, and asking to use that moment for a story to tell later. That's true regardless of whether she said yes.\n\nHer mom grumbling the whole time was probably a signal worth paying attention to. People living through unwanted internet infamy don't always feel able to say no to a stranger on the street, even if they're polite about it.\n\nI get that the case is genuinely fascinating to you, and there's nothing wrong with being interested in it. But being interested in a legal story is different from treating the actual people involved like an attraction to collect on your trip. Your SO being embarrassed makes sense to me here. It might help to ask yourself how you'd feel if you were in the middle of a humiliating viral moment and a stranger excitedly asked for a selfie with you and your mom while she stood there not saying much."},{"Description":"ord_1416","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1416","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_401","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but maybe not for the reason you think.\n\nI think it's worth sitting with something you said yourself: you called this person \"genuinely mentally ill\" and someone \"desperately seeking attention.\" If that's really what you believe, then asking for a photo like she's a viral curiosity you stumbled across on vacation isn't just harmless fun, it's a little uncomfortable. You weren't meeting a celebrity, you were approaching a stranger who's currently the subject of a lot of public mockery, and asking to use that moment for a story to tell later. That's true regardless of whether she said yes.\n\nHer mom grumbling the whole time was probably a signal worth paying attention to. People living through unwanted internet infamy don't always feel able to say no to a stranger on the street, even if they're polite about it.\n\nI get that the case is genuinely fascinating to you, and there's nothing wrong with being interested in it. But being interested in a legal story is different from treating the actual people involved like an attraction to collect on your trip. Your SO being embarrassed makes sense to me here. It might help to ask yourself how you'd feel if you were in the middle of a humiliating viral moment and a stranger excitedly asked for a selfie with you and your mom while she stood there not saying much."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but maybe not for the reason you think.\n\nYou've described this person yourself as \"genuinely mentally ill\" and someone who is \"desperately seeking attention.\" If you actually believe that, then treating them like a zoo animal you stumbled across on vacation isn't \"cool,\" it's kind of gross. You didn't approach a celebrity, you approached a stranger who's become an object of mass ridicule and controversy, and asked to use her for content\/a story to tell. That's true whether she said yes or not.\n\nHer mom \"grumbling the entire time\" should have been your signal to read the room. People experiencing unwanted infamy don't usually love being recognized in public by strangers on vacation, even if they're too polite (or worn down) to say no.\n\nYou can be fascinated by a legal case or a public controversy without treating a random woman and her mother like an attraction to collect during your trip. Your SO isn't wrong to be embarrassed. Ask yourself how you'd feel if you were dealing with a humiliating internet-famous moment in your life and someone excitedly asked for a selfie with you and your mom while she stood there stone-faced."},{"Description":"ord_1416","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1416","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_LSIycnRlcjZdInd","FlowID":"FL_94","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_402","Description":"Item 64 (id 3308)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_403","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_404","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. For the jealousy game, but let's talk about that push.\n\nDeliberately flirting with his friend to get back at him for talking to another girl isn't \"teasing\" or giving him \"a taste of his own medicine\" - it's manipulative. You weren't communicating your hurt feelings, you were staging a reaction. That's a passive-aggressive way to handle jealousy, and it's understandable why it blew up the way it did. If you were hurt that he was cold to you and chatting up someone else, the move is to say \"hey, what's going on, you're acting weird and it's making me feel bad\" - not orchestrate a counter-flirtation as payback.\n\nSo yes, on the immediate question: you engineered a situation to provoke a reaction, and it worked exactly as intended, just with more heat than you wanted.\n\nBut separately - he pushed you. That's not \"he got upset,\" that's a physical escalation that has nothing to do with who was pettier first. Being jealous or hurt doesn't justify shoving your partner. That's the part of this story I'd actually be more concerned about long-term, more than who was in the wrong for the party drama.\n\nSo: you're in the wrong for playing games instead of just talking to him, but that doesn't make what he did to you okay. Two separate issues, and the second one is the more serious one."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. For the jealousy game, but that push needs to be addressed separately.\n\nI think deliberately flirting with his friend to get back at him for talking to another girl isn't really \"teasing\" or giving him \"a taste of his own medicine\" - it comes across as more manipulative than that. It sounds like you weren't communicating your hurt feelings so much as staging a reaction, and I get why that blew up the way it did. If you were hurt that he was cold to you and chatting with someone else, it might have gone better to say \"hey, what's going on, you're acting weird and it's making me feel bad\" rather than orchestrating a counter-flirtation as payback.\n\nSo on the immediate question: it does seem like you engineered a situation to provoke a reaction, and it worked, just with more heat than you were probably going for.\n\nThat said, separately - he pushed you. That's not just \"he got upset,\" that's a physical escalation, and it's not something that should get measured against who was pettier first. Being jealous or hurt doesn't justify shoving your partner, and I'd honestly be more concerned about that part going forward than about who started the party drama.\n\nSo: you're in the wrong for playing games instead of just talking to him, but that doesn't make what he did to you okay. These are two separate issues, and I think the second one is the more serious one."},{"Description":"ord_3308","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3308","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_405","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. For the jealousy game, but that push needs to be addressed separately.\n\nI think deliberately flirting with his friend to get back at him for talking to another girl isn't really \"teasing\" or giving him \"a taste of his own medicine\" - it comes across as more manipulative than that. It sounds like you weren't communicating your hurt feelings so much as staging a reaction, and I get why that blew up the way it did. If you were hurt that he was cold to you and chatting with someone else, it might have gone better to say \"hey, what's going on, you're acting weird and it's making me feel bad\" rather than orchestrating a counter-flirtation as payback.\n\nSo on the immediate question: it does seem like you engineered a situation to provoke a reaction, and it worked, just with more heat than you were probably going for.\n\nThat said, separately - he pushed you. That's not just \"he got upset,\" that's a physical escalation, and it's not something that should get measured against who was pettier first. Being jealous or hurt doesn't justify shoving your partner, and I'd honestly be more concerned about that part going forward than about who started the party drama.\n\nSo: you're in the wrong for playing games instead of just talking to him, but that doesn't make what he did to you okay. These are two separate issues, and I think the second one is the more serious one."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. For the jealousy game, but let's talk about that push.\n\nDeliberately flirting with his friend to get back at him for talking to another girl isn't \"teasing\" or giving him \"a taste of his own medicine\" - it's manipulative. You weren't communicating your hurt feelings, you were staging a reaction. That's a passive-aggressive way to handle jealousy, and it's understandable why it blew up the way it did. If you were hurt that he was cold to you and chatting up someone else, the move is to say \"hey, what's going on, you're acting weird and it's making me feel bad\" - not orchestrate a counter-flirtation as payback.\n\nSo yes, on the immediate question: you engineered a situation to provoke a reaction, and it worked exactly as intended, just with more heat than you wanted.\n\nBut separately - he pushed you. That's not \"he got upset,\" that's a physical escalation that has nothing to do with who was pettier first. Being jealous or hurt doesn't justify shoving your partner. That's the part of this story I'd actually be more concerned about long-term, more than who was in the wrong for the party drama.\n\nSo: you're in the wrong for playing games instead of just talking to him, but that doesn't make what he did to you okay. Two separate issues, and the second one is the more serious one."},{"Description":"ord_3308","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3308","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_yXbSxoUITgGkmrH","FlowID":"FL_95","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_406","Description":"Item 65 (id 975)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_407","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_408","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, and I think you know it on some level or you wouldn't be asking.\n\nHere's the thing: it doesn't matter that your mom is accepting. It doesn't matter that the relationship seemed obvious. It doesn't matter that your mom already called them \"cute.\" None of that changes the fundamental issue, which is that it wasn't your information to confirm or deny out loud.\n\nYour sister gets to decide when, how, and to whom she discloses her relationship status and orientation. Even if your mom had 99% already figured it out, there's a real difference between \"I have a strong suspicion\" and having someone else state it as fact. You took that choice away from her and made it for her, in real time, without asking.\n\nYour reasoning (\"it seemed obvious to everyone,\" \"she's accepting anyway\") is the exact justification almost everyone uses after they out someone. It might even be true! But that's not really the point. The point is your sister had not yet decided to say it herself, and now she doesn't get to. You collapsed the timeline she was working on, whatever that timeline was and for whatever reasons she had (even if those reasons don't make total sense to you).\n\nYou also don't actually know that she hadn't talked to your mom about it in some other way, or that she wasn't planning to soon, or that there wasn't a specific reason she wanted to be the one to say the word \"girlfriend\" out loud first. That's information you don't have access to, which is exactly why it wasn't your call.\n\nA genuine apology here isn't \"sorry you're upset\" or \"I still think I was justified but sorry.\" It's \"I shouldn't have said that, it wasn't mine to share, I'm sorry.\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, I think - and it sounds like part of you suspects that too, or you wouldn't be asking.\n\nI get that your mom is accepting, that the relationship seemed obvious to everyone, and that she'd even called them \"cute\" before. Those are all fair points, and I don't doubt any of them. But I don't think they change the core issue: it wasn't your information to confirm out loud.\n\nYour sister gets to decide when, how, and to whom she discloses her relationship and orientation. Even if your mom had basically already figured it out, there's a real difference between someone quietly suspecting something and having someone else state it as fact. It seems like that choice got taken away from her in that moment, without her input.\n\nI'd also gently push back on the idea that you know for sure she hadn't already talked to your mom about it in her own way, or wasn't planning to soon, or didn't have a specific reason for wanting to be the one to say \"girlfriend\" out loud first. That's really something only she would know, which is part of why I don't think it was your call to make regardless of how obvious it seemed to you.\n\nIf you want to smooth things over, I think it'd help to lead with something like \"I shouldn't have said that, it wasn't mine to share, I'm sorry\" rather than \"sorry you're upset\" or \"sorry, but I still think I was right\" - even if you still feel your read on the situation was reasonable."},{"Description":"ord_975","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_975","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_409","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, I think - and it sounds like part of you suspects that too, or you wouldn't be asking.\n\nI get that your mom is accepting, that the relationship seemed obvious to everyone, and that she'd even called them \"cute\" before. Those are all fair points, and I don't doubt any of them. But I don't think they change the core issue: it wasn't your information to confirm out loud.\n\nYour sister gets to decide when, how, and to whom she discloses her relationship and orientation. Even if your mom had basically already figured it out, there's a real difference between someone quietly suspecting something and having someone else state it as fact. It seems like that choice got taken away from her in that moment, without her input.\n\nI'd also gently push back on the idea that you know for sure she hadn't already talked to your mom about it in her own way, or wasn't planning to soon, or didn't have a specific reason for wanting to be the one to say \"girlfriend\" out loud first. That's really something only she would know, which is part of why I don't think it was your call to make regardless of how obvious it seemed to you.\n\nIf you want to smooth things over, I think it'd help to lead with something like \"I shouldn't have said that, it wasn't mine to share, I'm sorry\" rather than \"sorry you're upset\" or \"sorry, but I still think I was right\" - even if you still feel your read on the situation was reasonable."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, and I think you know it on some level or you wouldn't be asking.\n\nHere's the thing: it doesn't matter that your mom is accepting. It doesn't matter that the relationship seemed obvious. It doesn't matter that your mom already called them \"cute.\" None of that changes the fundamental issue, which is that it wasn't your information to confirm or deny out loud.\n\nYour sister gets to decide when, how, and to whom she discloses her relationship status and orientation. Even if your mom had 99% already figured it out, there's a real difference between \"I have a strong suspicion\" and having someone else state it as fact. You took that choice away from her and made it for her, in real time, without asking.\n\nYour reasoning (\"it seemed obvious to everyone,\" \"she's accepting anyway\") is the exact justification almost everyone uses after they out someone. It might even be true! But that's not really the point. The point is your sister had not yet decided to say it herself, and now she doesn't get to. You collapsed the timeline she was working on, whatever that timeline was and for whatever reasons she had (even if those reasons don't make total sense to you).\n\nYou also don't actually know that she hadn't talked to your mom about it in some other way, or that she wasn't planning to soon, or that there wasn't a specific reason she wanted to be the one to say the word \"girlfriend\" out loud first. That's information you don't have access to, which is exactly why it wasn't your call.\n\nA genuine apology here isn't \"sorry you're upset\" or \"I still think I was justified but sorry.\" It's \"I shouldn't have said that, it wasn't mine to share, I'm sorry.\""},{"Description":"ord_975","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_975","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_BQEoTmjznvMok78","FlowID":"FL_96","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_410","Description":"Item 66 (id 3690)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_411","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_412","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, and I think if you step back you'll see why.\n\nThe card's answer is \"Ice\" - that's the actual answer, not \"water.\" The fact that the tweet included the phrase \"water ice\" (which is a specific, real term for frozen water, especially in planetary science contexts) doesn't mean \"water\" alone was the intended or correct answer. You cherry-picked one word out of a proper noun\/compound term to try to retroactively justify your answer.\n\nThis isn't really a pedantic technicality on their part - it's the actual answer printed on the card. If you'd said \"water ice\" that's arguably a discussion worth having. But you said \"water,\" which is meaningfully different from \"ice\" in a trivia context, especially about a specific NASA discovery.\n\nBeyond the rules-lawyering, the bigger issue is your reaction. Raging at a casual family game night over a Trivial Pursuit ruling is disproportionate. Even if you felt strongly you were right, family game night is supposed to be fun and low-stakes. Escalating to \"raging\" over a board game answer - even a legitimately close call - is going to make you the person who ruins game night, not the person who was robbed of a correct answer.\n\nTake the L on this one, and maybe apologize for the reaction even if you still privately think your answer had merit."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong, and I'll explain why.\n\nThe card's answer is \"Ice\" - that's the printed answer, not \"water.\" The tweet includes the phrase \"water ice,\" which is a specific, real term for frozen water in planetary science contexts - it doesn't mean \"water\" alone was the intended answer. It looks like one word got pulled out of a compound term to justify the answer after the fact.\n\nI get why you feel this is close - \"water\" is in there, so I understand the argument. But I don't think this is your family being pedantic; it's the actual answer on the card. If you'd said \"water ice,\" that's a real discussion to have. \"Water\" alone is meaningfully different from \"ice\" in a trivia context about a specific NASA discovery.\n\nBeyond the rules question, the bigger issue is the reaction. Raging at a casual family game night over a Trivial Pursuit ruling is disproportionate, even if you felt strongly you were right. Game night is supposed to be low-stakes fun, and escalating to \"raging\" over a board game answer risks making you the person who ruined the night, not the person who was robbed of a correct answer.\n\nI'd take the loss on this one and maybe apologize for the reaction, even if part of you still thinks the answer had merit."},{"Description":"ord_3690","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3690","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_413","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong, and I'll explain why.\n\nThe card's answer is \"Ice\" - that's the printed answer, not \"water.\" The tweet includes the phrase \"water ice,\" which is a specific, real term for frozen water in planetary science contexts - it doesn't mean \"water\" alone was the intended answer. It looks like one word got pulled out of a compound term to justify the answer after the fact.\n\nI get why you feel this is close - \"water\" is in there, so I understand the argument. But I don't think this is your family being pedantic; it's the actual answer on the card. If you'd said \"water ice,\" that's a real discussion to have. \"Water\" alone is meaningfully different from \"ice\" in a trivia context about a specific NASA discovery.\n\nBeyond the rules question, the bigger issue is the reaction. Raging at a casual family game night over a Trivial Pursuit ruling is disproportionate, even if you felt strongly you were right. Game night is supposed to be low-stakes fun, and escalating to \"raging\" over a board game answer risks making you the person who ruined the night, not the person who was robbed of a correct answer.\n\nI'd take the loss on this one and maybe apologize for the reaction, even if part of you still thinks the answer had merit."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, and I think if you step back you'll see why.\n\nThe card's answer is \"Ice\" - that's the actual answer, not \"water.\" The fact that the tweet included the phrase \"water ice\" (which is a specific, real term for frozen water, especially in planetary science contexts) doesn't mean \"water\" alone was the intended or correct answer. You cherry-picked one word out of a proper noun\/compound term to try to retroactively justify your answer.\n\nThis isn't really a pedantic technicality on their part - it's the actual answer printed on the card. If you'd said \"water ice\" that's arguably a discussion worth having. But you said \"water,\" which is meaningfully different from \"ice\" in a trivia context, especially about a specific NASA discovery.\n\nBeyond the rules-lawyering, the bigger issue is your reaction. Raging at a casual family game night over a Trivial Pursuit ruling is disproportionate. Even if you felt strongly you were right, family game night is supposed to be fun and low-stakes. Escalating to \"raging\" over a board game answer - even a legitimately close call - is going to make you the person who ruins game night, not the person who was robbed of a correct answer.\n\nTake the L on this one, and maybe apologize for the reaction even if you still privately think your answer had merit."},{"Description":"ord_3690","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3690","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_fIPJZUzQRGF3QVw","FlowID":"FL_97","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_414","Description":"Item 67 (id 2921)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_415","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_416","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI get the instinct-but this isn't your call to make. She's an adult, it's her body, her pregnancy, her legal property. Throwing away someone's belongings without consent isn't \"help,\" it's a unilateral decision that you had the right to overrule her autonomy because you disagreed with her choices. That's true even when the stakes feel high to you.\n\nA few things to sit with:\n- You don't actually know her full situation-cutting down, occasional smoker, stress relapse, etc. Nicotine addiction is brutal, especially compounded with pregnancy hormones and possibly a partner\/family who smokes too.\n- Throwing them away doesn't stop her from smoking. It just means she now has to notice they're missing, wonder if she misplaced them, maybe feel a flash of anxiety, and then go buy more. You haven't protected the baby; you've just added a layer of confusion\/gaslighting to her day and wasted her money.\n- If you're this concerned, the honest move would've been to talk to her directly-not confiscate her stuff secretly. Secretly taking someone's property \"for their own good\" isn't kindness, it's overstepping.\n\nGood on you for planning to come clean and repay her-that's the right move and will probably go over better than you expect, especially if you're honest and not preachy about it. Own it as what it was: well-intentioned but not okay."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong, though I get where the instinct came from.\n\nYou said yourself that it's her body, her pregnancy, and her possessions-and I think that's exactly right, which is part of why this doesn't sit well. Throwing away someone's belongings without asking, even with good intentions, ends up being a unilateral decision that overrides her say in her own choices. That's true even when the worry behind it feels urgent.\n\nA few things worth sitting with:\n- You probably don't know her full situation-cutting down, an occasional cigarette, a stress relapse, a partner who smokes too. Nicotine addiction is genuinely hard to shake, especially layered with pregnancy hormones.\n- Tossing the pack likely doesn't stop her from smoking anyway. It probably just means she notices they're gone, feels confused or anxious wondering if she misplaced them, and then buys another pack. So it may not have protected the baby-it just added stress and cost for her.\n- If this was weighing on you, I think the more honest route would've been to talk to her directly, rather than quietly taking her things \"for her own good.\"\n\nThat said, it sounds like you've already thought this through since posting-planning to be upfront with her and pay her back is a good call, and I'd guess it'll go over better than you're expecting if you're straightforward about it. I'd just frame it to yourself as: well-intentioned, but not something you were entitled to decide for her."},{"Description":"ord_2921","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2921","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_417","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong, though I get where the instinct came from.\n\nYou said yourself that it's her body, her pregnancy, and her possessions-and I think that's exactly right, which is part of why this doesn't sit well. Throwing away someone's belongings without asking, even with good intentions, ends up being a unilateral decision that overrides her say in her own choices. That's true even when the worry behind it feels urgent.\n\nA few things worth sitting with:\n- You probably don't know her full situation-cutting down, an occasional cigarette, a stress relapse, a partner who smokes too. Nicotine addiction is genuinely hard to shake, especially layered with pregnancy hormones.\n- Tossing the pack likely doesn't stop her from smoking anyway. It probably just means she notices they're gone, feels confused or anxious wondering if she misplaced them, and then buys another pack. So it may not have protected the baby-it just added stress and cost for her.\n- If this was weighing on you, I think the more honest route would've been to talk to her directly, rather than quietly taking her things \"for her own good.\"\n\nThat said, it sounds like you've already thought this through since posting-planning to be upfront with her and pay her back is a good call, and I'd guess it'll go over better than you're expecting if you're straightforward about it. I'd just frame it to yourself as: well-intentioned, but not something you were entitled to decide for her."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI get the instinct-but this isn't your call to make. She's an adult, it's her body, her pregnancy, her legal property. Throwing away someone's belongings without consent isn't \"help,\" it's a unilateral decision that you had the right to overrule her autonomy because you disagreed with her choices. That's true even when the stakes feel high to you.\n\nA few things to sit with:\n- You don't actually know her full situation-cutting down, occasional smoker, stress relapse, etc. Nicotine addiction is brutal, especially compounded with pregnancy hormones and possibly a partner\/family who smokes too.\n- Throwing them away doesn't stop her from smoking. It just means she now has to notice they're missing, wonder if she misplaced them, maybe feel a flash of anxiety, and then go buy more. You haven't protected the baby; you've just added a layer of confusion\/gaslighting to her day and wasted her money.\n- If you're this concerned, the honest move would've been to talk to her directly-not confiscate her stuff secretly. Secretly taking someone's property \"for their own good\" isn't kindness, it's overstepping.\n\nGood on you for planning to come clean and repay her-that's the right move and will probably go over better than you expect, especially if you're honest and not preachy about it. Own it as what it was: well-intentioned but not okay."},{"Description":"ord_2921","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2921","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_v6GHKKrjVlMfhWd","FlowID":"FL_98","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_418","Description":"Item 68 (id 1484)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_419","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_420","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This is such a low-stakes conflict that it's almost funny, but let's break it down.\n\nYou're in the wrong, mildly, but so is the poutine situation itself.\n\nHere's the thing: you were all drunk, cold, and hungry, and someone (your girlfriend) made the very human decision to start eating food that smelled amazing while walking. That's not some huge betrayal-that's just what happens when you're intoxicated and impatient with hot food in your hands.\n\nYour actual complaint boils down to:\n1. She slowed the walking pace\n2. The poutine got cold\n\nBut you have to ask yourself-was she actually slowing down significantly, or were you just irritated and looking for a reason to be annoyed? A 5-minute walk becoming a 6 or 7-minute walk in -10 degrees isn't the tragedy you're framing it as.\n\nAlso, it's her poutine too. You shared it, you're a couple, this isn't some sacred artifact she desecrated. If she was hungry and wanted to start eating, that's a pretty normal impulse that doesn't deserve genuine anger.\n\nGetting a little annoyed? Sure, that's understandable in the moment. But \"getting mad\" at your girlfriend over communal poutine consumption during a drunk night out is the kind of thing that's going to sound absurd to you tomorrow (and honestly sounds a little absurd now).\n\nEat the cold poutine, laugh about it, move on. This is not the hill to die on."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This is such a low-stakes conflict that it's almost funny, but let's break it down.\n\nI'd lean you're in the wrong here, mildly - though I get why the cold poutine was annoying.\n\nYou were all drunk, cold, and hungry, and your girlfriend made a pretty human decision to start eating food that smelled amazing while walking. I don't think that's some huge betrayal - it's just what happens when you're intoxicated and impatient with hot food in your hands.\n\nYour complaint seems to boil down to: she slowed the pace, and the poutine got cold. Both fair annoyances in the moment. But it's worth asking yourself whether she was actually slowing things down that much, or whether you were just irritated and looking for something to pin it on. A 5-minute walk becoming a 6 or 7-minute walk in -10 degrees isn't quite the tragedy it might've felt like at the time.\n\nAlso worth remembering: it's her poutine too. You shared it, you're a couple, so this isn't like she got into something that wasn't hers. If she was hungry and wanted to start eating, that's a normal impulse, even if the timing was inconvenient.\n\nSo I get being a little annoyed - that tracks. But \"getting mad\" at her over shared poutine on a drunk night out is probably going to feel a bit much in hindsight (and maybe already does).\n\nEat the cold poutine, laugh about it, move on. Doesn't seem like the hill to die on."},{"Description":"ord_1484","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1484","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_421","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This is such a low-stakes conflict that it's almost funny, but let's break it down.\n\nI'd lean you're in the wrong here, mildly - though I get why the cold poutine was annoying.\n\nYou were all drunk, cold, and hungry, and your girlfriend made a pretty human decision to start eating food that smelled amazing while walking. I don't think that's some huge betrayal - it's just what happens when you're intoxicated and impatient with hot food in your hands.\n\nYour complaint seems to boil down to: she slowed the pace, and the poutine got cold. Both fair annoyances in the moment. But it's worth asking yourself whether she was actually slowing things down that much, or whether you were just irritated and looking for something to pin it on. A 5-minute walk becoming a 6 or 7-minute walk in -10 degrees isn't quite the tragedy it might've felt like at the time.\n\nAlso worth remembering: it's her poutine too. You shared it, you're a couple, so this isn't like she got into something that wasn't hers. If she was hungry and wanted to start eating, that's a normal impulse, even if the timing was inconvenient.\n\nSo I get being a little annoyed - that tracks. But \"getting mad\" at her over shared poutine on a drunk night out is probably going to feel a bit much in hindsight (and maybe already does).\n\nEat the cold poutine, laugh about it, move on. Doesn't seem like the hill to die on."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This is such a low-stakes conflict that it's almost funny, but let's break it down.\n\nYou're in the wrong, mildly, but so is the poutine situation itself.\n\nHere's the thing: you were all drunk, cold, and hungry, and someone (your girlfriend) made the very human decision to start eating food that smelled amazing while walking. That's not some huge betrayal-that's just what happens when you're intoxicated and impatient with hot food in your hands.\n\nYour actual complaint boils down to:\n1. She slowed the walking pace\n2. The poutine got cold\n\nBut you have to ask yourself-was she actually slowing down significantly, or were you just irritated and looking for a reason to be annoyed? A 5-minute walk becoming a 6 or 7-minute walk in -10 degrees isn't the tragedy you're framing it as.\n\nAlso, it's her poutine too. You shared it, you're a couple, this isn't some sacred artifact she desecrated. If she was hungry and wanted to start eating, that's a pretty normal impulse that doesn't deserve genuine anger.\n\nGetting a little annoyed? Sure, that's understandable in the moment. But \"getting mad\" at your girlfriend over communal poutine consumption during a drunk night out is the kind of thing that's going to sound absurd to you tomorrow (and honestly sounds a little absurd now).\n\nEat the cold poutine, laugh about it, move on. This is not the hill to die on."},{"Description":"ord_1484","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1484","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_TUrVlf0w12fr9EM","FlowID":"FL_99","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_422","Description":"Item 69 (id 3062)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_423","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_424","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, with some understanding for why you feel this way.\n\nHere's the thing: needing a legal name change as \"proof\" of seriousness isn't really a fair bar. Legal name changes cost money, require paperwork, court dates in some places, updating IDs\/documents, etc. Plenty of people go by a different name for years - sometimes their whole life - without ever legally changing it, for all kinds of valid reasons (cost, family pressure, uncertainty about whether they want to fully commit, or just not seeing the need since a legal document doesn't determine who they are).\n\nYour frustration about the pattern of switching names 5 times is understandable - that's genuinely hard to keep up with, and it's fair to feel tired of relearning. But the current name has stuck for 3 years, which is a pretty long stretch. That's not really \"still deciding\" territory anymore.\n\nMaking legal paperwork the condition for you to respect what she wants to be called comes across less like reasonable pushback and more like a power move - basically saying \"I won't take you seriously until you jump through a hoop I've decided matters.\" Names carry real weight for how people feel seen by their family, and continuing to use a name she's told you she doesn't want, especially now that it's been consistent for years, is going to read as disrespectful no matter how you frame the \"logic\" behind it.\n\nIf the switching bothered you, that would've been a fair thing to express years ago. But now that it's stable, the legal-name ultimatum feels like you're holding onto old resentment more than reacting to her current behavior."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though I get why you feel worn out by this.\n\nI think requiring a legal name change as \"proof\" she's serious sets the bar in a place that isn't quite fair. Legal name changes cost money, involve paperwork, sometimes court dates, updating IDs and documents - and plenty of people go by a different name for years, or their whole life, without ever making it official, for all kinds of legitimate reasons (cost, uncertainty, or just not feeling like a legal document defines who they are).\n\nYour frustration about the pattern of switching names five times makes sense to me - that's genuinely hard to keep up with, and it's fair to feel tired of relearning a name each time. But it sounds like the current name has stuck for about three years now, which is a pretty long stretch - that seems more like a settled choice than \"still deciding.\"\n\nMaking legal paperwork the condition for using the name she's asked for comes across, I think, less like reasonable pushback and more like you're asking her to jump through a hoop before you'll take her seriously. Names carry real weight for how people feel seen by their family, and continuing to use a name she's told you she doesn't want - especially now that it's been consistent for years - is likely to land as disrespectful, even with the reasoning behind it.\n\nIf the switching bothered you, it might've helped to say so years ago. But now that it's stable, I'd gently say the legal-name condition feels more like it's coming from old frustration than from what's actually happening now."},{"Description":"ord_3062","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3062","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_425","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though I get why you feel worn out by this.\n\nI think requiring a legal name change as \"proof\" she's serious sets the bar in a place that isn't quite fair. Legal name changes cost money, involve paperwork, sometimes court dates, updating IDs and documents - and plenty of people go by a different name for years, or their whole life, without ever making it official, for all kinds of legitimate reasons (cost, uncertainty, or just not feeling like a legal document defines who they are).\n\nYour frustration about the pattern of switching names five times makes sense to me - that's genuinely hard to keep up with, and it's fair to feel tired of relearning a name each time. But it sounds like the current name has stuck for about three years now, which is a pretty long stretch - that seems more like a settled choice than \"still deciding.\"\n\nMaking legal paperwork the condition for using the name she's asked for comes across, I think, less like reasonable pushback and more like you're asking her to jump through a hoop before you'll take her seriously. Names carry real weight for how people feel seen by their family, and continuing to use a name she's told you she doesn't want - especially now that it's been consistent for years - is likely to land as disrespectful, even with the reasoning behind it.\n\nIf the switching bothered you, it might've helped to say so years ago. But now that it's stable, I'd gently say the legal-name condition feels more like it's coming from old frustration than from what's actually happening now."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, with some understanding for why you feel this way.\n\nHere's the thing: needing a legal name change as \"proof\" of seriousness isn't really a fair bar. Legal name changes cost money, require paperwork, court dates in some places, updating IDs\/documents, etc. Plenty of people go by a different name for years - sometimes their whole life - without ever legally changing it, for all kinds of valid reasons (cost, family pressure, uncertainty about whether they want to fully commit, or just not seeing the need since a legal document doesn't determine who they are).\n\nYour frustration about the pattern of switching names 5 times is understandable - that's genuinely hard to keep up with, and it's fair to feel tired of relearning. But the current name has stuck for 3 years, which is a pretty long stretch. That's not really \"still deciding\" territory anymore.\n\nMaking legal paperwork the condition for you to respect what she wants to be called comes across less like reasonable pushback and more like a power move - basically saying \"I won't take you seriously until you jump through a hoop I've decided matters.\" Names carry real weight for how people feel seen by their family, and continuing to use a name she's told you she doesn't want, especially now that it's been consistent for years, is going to read as disrespectful no matter how you frame the \"logic\" behind it.\n\nIf the switching bothered you, that would've been a fair thing to express years ago. But now that it's stable, the legal-name ultimatum feels like you're holding onto old resentment more than reacting to her current behavior."},{"Description":"ord_3062","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3062","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_R3SE8kLt0OzfbZH","FlowID":"FL_100","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_426","Description":"Item 70 (id 1881)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_427","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_428","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but let's be precise about why, because you're conflating two different things: having opinions and disagreements versus insulting someone.\n\nYou're right that you're allowed to disagree with your friend. You're right that you're allowed to criticize his opinion, explain why you think he's wrong, argue the point, whatever. That's normal discourse between friends who trust each other enough to disagree.\n\nBut \"criticizing an opinion\" and \"calling it stupid\" are not the same thing. One is engaging with the substance of what someone said. The other is a dismissive insult that shuts down actual discussion and just makes the other person feel belittled. You can say \"I think you're wrong because X\" without saying \"that's stupid.\" The fact that you're framing this as \"I'm allowed to criticize him\" when what you actually did was insult him tells me you already know the distinction but are choosing to blur it because it makes your behavior look more defensible.\n\nAlso, \"if he decides to insult me back sometimes, that's acceptable too\" is a pretty telling standard to set. You're basically saying insults are fine as long as they're occasional and mutual, which just means you've agreed to a friendship where you periodically get to be a jerk to each other and call it normal.\n\nYour friend didn't say you can't disagree with him or that all conflict must be avoided. He said don't insult his opinions. That's a completely reasonable boundary, and you calling his opinion \"stupid\" is exactly the behavior he was asking you to stop."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong here, but let me be clear about why-because I think you're conflating two different things: having opinions and disagreements versus insulting someone.\n\nYou're right that you're allowed to disagree with your friend, and I agree with you there. You're also right that you're allowed to criticize his opinion, explain why you think he's wrong, argue the point, whatever-that's normal discourse between friends who trust each other enough to disagree.\n\nBut I think \"criticizing an opinion\" and \"calling it stupid\" aren't quite the same thing. One engages with the substance of what someone said; the other tends to land as dismissive and can make the other person feel belittled rather than heard. It seems possible to say \"I think you're wrong because X\" without adding \"that's stupid.\" When you frame this as \"I'm allowed to criticize him,\" while what actually happened was calling his opinion stupid, I think that blurs a distinction worth keeping clear.\n\nI'd also gently push back on \"if he insults me back sometimes, that's acceptable too.\" That standard basically means insults are fine as long as they're occasional and mutual-which I don't think is the same as having a healthy way to disagree.\n\nFrom what you've described, your friend didn't say you can't disagree with him or that all conflict must be avoided-he said don't insult his opinions. I think that's a reasonable boundary to ask for, and it sounds like what he's really asking for is to keep the disagreement about ideas rather than about him."},{"Description":"ord_1881","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1881","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_429","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong here, but let me be clear about why-because I think you're conflating two different things: having opinions and disagreements versus insulting someone.\n\nYou're right that you're allowed to disagree with your friend, and I agree with you there. You're also right that you're allowed to criticize his opinion, explain why you think he's wrong, argue the point, whatever-that's normal discourse between friends who trust each other enough to disagree.\n\nBut I think \"criticizing an opinion\" and \"calling it stupid\" aren't quite the same thing. One engages with the substance of what someone said; the other tends to land as dismissive and can make the other person feel belittled rather than heard. It seems possible to say \"I think you're wrong because X\" without adding \"that's stupid.\" When you frame this as \"I'm allowed to criticize him,\" while what actually happened was calling his opinion stupid, I think that blurs a distinction worth keeping clear.\n\nI'd also gently push back on \"if he insults me back sometimes, that's acceptable too.\" That standard basically means insults are fine as long as they're occasional and mutual-which I don't think is the same as having a healthy way to disagree.\n\nFrom what you've described, your friend didn't say you can't disagree with him or that all conflict must be avoided-he said don't insult his opinions. I think that's a reasonable boundary to ask for, and it sounds like what he's really asking for is to keep the disagreement about ideas rather than about him."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but let's be precise about why, because you're conflating two different things: having opinions and disagreements versus insulting someone.\n\nYou're right that you're allowed to disagree with your friend. You're right that you're allowed to criticize his opinion, explain why you think he's wrong, argue the point, whatever. That's normal discourse between friends who trust each other enough to disagree.\n\nBut \"criticizing an opinion\" and \"calling it stupid\" are not the same thing. One is engaging with the substance of what someone said. The other is a dismissive insult that shuts down actual discussion and just makes the other person feel belittled. You can say \"I think you're wrong because X\" without saying \"that's stupid.\" The fact that you're framing this as \"I'm allowed to criticize him\" when what you actually did was insult him tells me you already know the distinction but are choosing to blur it because it makes your behavior look more defensible.\n\nAlso, \"if he decides to insult me back sometimes, that's acceptable too\" is a pretty telling standard to set. You're basically saying insults are fine as long as they're occasional and mutual, which just means you've agreed to a friendship where you periodically get to be a jerk to each other and call it normal.\n\nYour friend didn't say you can't disagree with him or that all conflict must be avoided. He said don't insult his opinions. That's a completely reasonable boundary, and you calling his opinion \"stupid\" is exactly the behavior he was asking you to stop."},{"Description":"ord_1881","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1881","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_mBVPvkHUIuKgnkG","FlowID":"FL_101","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_430","Description":"Item 71 (id 3282)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_431","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_432","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nThe \"agreement\" you had was built on a specific financial reality: he had two incomes and could comfortably cover everything. That reality changed because he lost a job-not because he decided to renege on a promise out of laziness or spite. He didn't choose to be laid off. Expecting him to still single-handedly cover rent and food while working only a part-time job isn't him breaking a promise; it's him navigating an emergency using the resources he actually has.\n\nAsking you to pitch in for basic living expenses during a job loss isn't a betrayal-it's literally what partnership looks like when things get hard. If anything, it would be more concerning if he didn't ask for help and just sank you both into debt to preserve some idea of always being the sole provider.\n\nYour loss of attraction here says a lot. It sounds like part of what drew you to him wasn't just him as a person, but the arrangement of being fully financially provided for. That's your prerogative to want in a relationship, but it's not really fair to frame his job loss as a moral failure on his part. He's not failing to \"hold up his end\"-the terms changed because his circumstances changed, and he's asking you to flex with him the way partners are supposed to.\n\nIf you genuinely don't want a relationship where you might ever need to contribute financially, that's a legitimate thing to know about yourself. But dress it up as \"he broke his promise\" isn't accurate, and breaking up with someone because they lost their job and need support feels like poor timing at best, and pretty cold at worst."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong.\n\nI hear you that this was a real agreement between you two, and I get why it stings to feel like the terms changed on you-that's a fair thing to notice and be upset about. But I think the situation behind the change matters a lot here: he didn't decide to renege out of laziness or spite, he lost a job. That's not him breaking a promise, that's him navigating an emergency with the resources he actually has left.\n\nAsking you to help with rent and food during a job loss isn't a betrayal of the deal-it's honestly what partnership tends to look like when things get hard. It might actually be more concerning if he didn't ask for help and just quietly sank you both into debt to keep up the original arrangement.\n\nI also think your loss of attraction is telling you something worth paying attention to. It sounds like part of what drew you to him may have been the arrangement itself-being fully provided for-as much as him as a person. That's okay to want and be honest with yourself about. But I don't think it's fair to frame his job loss as a moral failure or as him not holding up his end. The terms shifted because his circumstances shifted, and he's asking you to flex with him, not punishing you.\n\nIf you know deep down that you don't want a relationship where you might ever need to contribute financially, that's useful self-knowledge. But I'd gently push back on calling this \"he broke his promise\"-and breaking up with him specifically because he lost his job and needs support right now does feel like rough timing, at best."},{"Description":"ord_3282","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3282","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_433","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong.\n\nI hear you that this was a real agreement between you two, and I get why it stings to feel like the terms changed on you-that's a fair thing to notice and be upset about. But I think the situation behind the change matters a lot here: he didn't decide to renege out of laziness or spite, he lost a job. That's not him breaking a promise, that's him navigating an emergency with the resources he actually has left.\n\nAsking you to help with rent and food during a job loss isn't a betrayal of the deal-it's honestly what partnership tends to look like when things get hard. It might actually be more concerning if he didn't ask for help and just quietly sank you both into debt to keep up the original arrangement.\n\nI also think your loss of attraction is telling you something worth paying attention to. It sounds like part of what drew you to him may have been the arrangement itself-being fully provided for-as much as him as a person. That's okay to want and be honest with yourself about. But I don't think it's fair to frame his job loss as a moral failure or as him not holding up his end. The terms shifted because his circumstances shifted, and he's asking you to flex with him, not punishing you.\n\nIf you know deep down that you don't want a relationship where you might ever need to contribute financially, that's useful self-knowledge. But I'd gently push back on calling this \"he broke his promise\"-and breaking up with him specifically because he lost his job and needs support right now does feel like rough timing, at best."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nThe \"agreement\" you had was built on a specific financial reality: he had two incomes and could comfortably cover everything. That reality changed because he lost a job-not because he decided to renege on a promise out of laziness or spite. He didn't choose to be laid off. Expecting him to still single-handedly cover rent and food while working only a part-time job isn't him breaking a promise; it's him navigating an emergency using the resources he actually has.\n\nAsking you to pitch in for basic living expenses during a job loss isn't a betrayal-it's literally what partnership looks like when things get hard. If anything, it would be more concerning if he didn't ask for help and just sank you both into debt to preserve some idea of always being the sole provider.\n\nYour loss of attraction here says a lot. It sounds like part of what drew you to him wasn't just him as a person, but the arrangement of being fully financially provided for. That's your prerogative to want in a relationship, but it's not really fair to frame his job loss as a moral failure on his part. He's not failing to \"hold up his end\"-the terms changed because his circumstances changed, and he's asking you to flex with him the way partners are supposed to.\n\nIf you genuinely don't want a relationship where you might ever need to contribute financially, that's a legitimate thing to know about yourself. But dress it up as \"he broke his promise\" isn't accurate, and breaking up with someone because they lost their job and need support feels like poor timing at best, and pretty cold at worst."},{"Description":"ord_3282","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3282","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_v2bWeNc7AZteBXd","FlowID":"FL_102","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_434","Description":"Item 72 (id 1382)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_435","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_436","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, though I don't think your intentions were malicious.\n\nWhat you did was string someone along for 3 months while slowly withdrawing, hoping she'd catch feelings-fatigue on her own timeline instead of just being honest with her. That's not kindness, even though I believe you thought it was. Real kindness would have been telling her directly: \"I've noticed I'm losing romantic feelings, and I think we should talk about where this is going.\" That gives her actual information to work with and the agency to process it in real time.\n\nInstead, you unilaterally decided you knew what was best for her emotional journey and tried to engineer her feelings without her consent or knowledge. You spent three months making her feel like something was wrong with her, or with the relationship, without ever naming it. She probably sensed the distance and had no idea why, which is arguably worse than a clean breakup because at least clean breakups come with clarity.\n\nThe outcome proves this: she was just as in love with you at the end as at the beginning. Your plan didn't work, and worse, it likely added confusion and self-doubt to what was already going to be a painful experience for her.\n\nYour friends are right. When you know you're done, the respectful thing is to end it clearly and let both people grieve and move on. Slow-fading someone isn't gentler, it just delays and often intensifies the pain, and it takes away the other person's ability to understand what's happening to their own relationship."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, though I don't think your intentions were malicious - it sounds like you genuinely believed you were being kind.\n\nThat said, I think what happened was that you strung her along for 3 months while slowly withdrawing, hoping she'd catch feelings-fatigue on her own timeline instead of you just being honest with her. I get that this felt like kindness to you, but I'd say real kindness would have been telling her directly: \"I've noticed I'm losing romantic feelings, and I think we should talk about where this is going.\" That gives her actual information to work with and the agency to process it in real time.\n\nInstead, it seems like you decided on your own what was best for her emotional journey and tried to engineer her feelings without her knowledge. For three months she may have sensed something was off without ever being told what, which can feel worse than a clean breakup, since at least that comes with clarity.\n\nYou already noticed the outcome yourself: she was just as in love with you at the end as at the beginning. So the plan didn't get you the gentler landing you were hoping for, and it may have added confusion and self-doubt on top of what was already going to be a painful thing for her.\n\nYour friends have a point here. When you know you're done, ending it clearly lets both people grieve and move on. I think the slow fade, even with good intentions, doesn't really soften things, it just delays them and takes away the other person's ability to understand what's actually happening in their own relationship."},{"Description":"ord_1382","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1382","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_437","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, though I don't think your intentions were malicious - it sounds like you genuinely believed you were being kind.\n\nThat said, I think what happened was that you strung her along for 3 months while slowly withdrawing, hoping she'd catch feelings-fatigue on her own timeline instead of you just being honest with her. I get that this felt like kindness to you, but I'd say real kindness would have been telling her directly: \"I've noticed I'm losing romantic feelings, and I think we should talk about where this is going.\" That gives her actual information to work with and the agency to process it in real time.\n\nInstead, it seems like you decided on your own what was best for her emotional journey and tried to engineer her feelings without her knowledge. For three months she may have sensed something was off without ever being told what, which can feel worse than a clean breakup, since at least that comes with clarity.\n\nYou already noticed the outcome yourself: she was just as in love with you at the end as at the beginning. So the plan didn't get you the gentler landing you were hoping for, and it may have added confusion and self-doubt on top of what was already going to be a painful thing for her.\n\nYour friends have a point here. When you know you're done, ending it clearly lets both people grieve and move on. I think the slow fade, even with good intentions, doesn't really soften things, it just delays them and takes away the other person's ability to understand what's actually happening in their own relationship."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Here, though I don't think your intentions were malicious.\n\nWhat you did was string someone along for 3 months while slowly withdrawing, hoping she'd catch feelings-fatigue on her own timeline instead of just being honest with her. That's not kindness, even though I believe you thought it was. Real kindness would have been telling her directly: \"I've noticed I'm losing romantic feelings, and I think we should talk about where this is going.\" That gives her actual information to work with and the agency to process it in real time.\n\nInstead, you unilaterally decided you knew what was best for her emotional journey and tried to engineer her feelings without her consent or knowledge. You spent three months making her feel like something was wrong with her, or with the relationship, without ever naming it. She probably sensed the distance and had no idea why, which is arguably worse than a clean breakup because at least clean breakups come with clarity.\n\nThe outcome proves this: she was just as in love with you at the end as at the beginning. Your plan didn't work, and worse, it likely added confusion and self-doubt to what was already going to be a painful experience for her.\n\nYour friends are right. When you know you're done, the respectful thing is to end it clearly and let both people grieve and move on. Slow-fading someone isn't gentler, it just delays and often intensifies the pain, and it takes away the other person's ability to understand what's happening to their own relationship."},{"Description":"ord_1382","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1382","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_R6h9Pw6fSa4WhU6","FlowID":"FL_103","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_438","Description":"Item 73 (id 3211)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_439","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_440","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong, but honestly your dad's reaction sounds way more disproportionate than what you did.\n\nWhy you're slightly in the wrong: You ate the last slice without checking if anyone else wanted it first. That's a normal little breach of dinner-table etiquette-when there's a shared meal with limited slices of something, a quick \"anyone want this?\" is just common courtesy. It sounds like it was genuinely unintentional\/thoughtless rather than malicious, which matters.\n\nWhy this isn't a big deal: It was one slice of pizza. An accidental mistake, not a calculated jerk move.\n\nWhy your dad's reaction is the bigger issue here: A 49-year-old throwing a \"hissy fit,\" calling his kid a d***, and storming off over a slice of pizza when there were reasonable alternatives offered (picking off the meat, ordering something else, etc.) is a wildly outsized reaction to a minor mistake. That's not proportionate parenting or adult conflict resolution-that's someone who struggles to regulate frustration over small things.\n\nThe apology piece: You can acknowledge \"sorry, I wasn't thinking, I should've asked\" - that's fair and costs you nothing. But your mom framing this as entirely your fault while ignoring your dad calling you names is letting him off the hook for an overreaction.\n\nVerdict: You're slightly in the wrong for the slice, but your dad owes you more of an apology than you owe him."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're slightly in the wrong, but honestly, your dad's reaction sounds a lot more disproportionate than what you did.\n\nWhy you're slightly in the wrong: You ate the last slice without checking if anyone else wanted it first. That's a small breach of dinner-table etiquette-when there's a shared meal with limited slices, a quick \"anyone want this?\" is just common courtesy. It sounds like it was genuinely unintentional\/thoughtless rather than malicious, which matters.\n\nWhy this isn't a big deal: It was one slice of pizza, and an accidental mistake at that-not a calculated move against him.\n\nWhy I think your dad's reaction is the bigger issue here: I hear that from his side, this was still frustrating-it was the slice he specifically wanted. But a 49-year-old getting furious, calling his kid a d***, and storming off over a slice of pizza, even with reasonable alternatives on the table (picking off the meat, ordering something else), seems like a pretty outsized reaction to a minor mistake.\n\nThe apology piece: You could reasonably say something like \"sorry, I wasn't thinking, I should've asked\"-that seems fair and costs you nothing. But your mom framing this as entirely your fault while not addressing your dad calling you names seems to let him off the hook for overreacting.\n\nVerdict: You're slightly in the wrong for the slice, but I think your dad owes you at least as much of an apology as you owe him."},{"Description":"ord_3211","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3211","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_441","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're slightly in the wrong, but honestly, your dad's reaction sounds a lot more disproportionate than what you did.\n\nWhy you're slightly in the wrong: You ate the last slice without checking if anyone else wanted it first. That's a small breach of dinner-table etiquette-when there's a shared meal with limited slices, a quick \"anyone want this?\" is just common courtesy. It sounds like it was genuinely unintentional\/thoughtless rather than malicious, which matters.\n\nWhy this isn't a big deal: It was one slice of pizza, and an accidental mistake at that-not a calculated move against him.\n\nWhy I think your dad's reaction is the bigger issue here: I hear that from his side, this was still frustrating-it was the slice he specifically wanted. But a 49-year-old getting furious, calling his kid a d***, and storming off over a slice of pizza, even with reasonable alternatives on the table (picking off the meat, ordering something else), seems like a pretty outsized reaction to a minor mistake.\n\nThe apology piece: You could reasonably say something like \"sorry, I wasn't thinking, I should've asked\"-that seems fair and costs you nothing. But your mom framing this as entirely your fault while not addressing your dad calling you names seems to let him off the hook for overreacting.\n\nVerdict: You're slightly in the wrong for the slice, but I think your dad owes you at least as much of an apology as you owe him."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong, but honestly your dad's reaction sounds way more disproportionate than what you did.\n\nWhy you're slightly in the wrong: You ate the last slice without checking if anyone else wanted it first. That's a normal little breach of dinner-table etiquette-when there's a shared meal with limited slices of something, a quick \"anyone want this?\" is just common courtesy. It sounds like it was genuinely unintentional\/thoughtless rather than malicious, which matters.\n\nWhy this isn't a big deal: It was one slice of pizza. An accidental mistake, not a calculated jerk move.\n\nWhy your dad's reaction is the bigger issue here: A 49-year-old throwing a \"hissy fit,\" calling his kid a d***, and storming off over a slice of pizza when there were reasonable alternatives offered (picking off the meat, ordering something else, etc.) is a wildly outsized reaction to a minor mistake. That's not proportionate parenting or adult conflict resolution-that's someone who struggles to regulate frustration over small things.\n\nThe apology piece: You can acknowledge \"sorry, I wasn't thinking, I should've asked\" - that's fair and costs you nothing. But your mom framing this as entirely your fault while ignoring your dad calling you names is letting him off the hook for an overreaction.\n\nVerdict: You're slightly in the wrong for the slice, but your dad owes you more of an apology than you owe him."},{"Description":"ord_3211","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3211","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_lTaZCdWvGH7ECE7","FlowID":"FL_104","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_442","Description":"Item 74 (id 3150)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_443","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_444","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, and it sounds like you already know it. Using \"retards\" as an insult is offensive regardless of the context, and you compounded it by being loud enough that they heard you talk about them behind their backs instead of just addressing them directly (or not at all, since it wasn't really your business how they handled their own dish).\n\nYour feelings about respecting specimens are valid and probably a good instinct for someone in your field. But you approached their table uninvited, they were cold because you were essentially policing them, and then you responded to that by mocking them with a slur to your friends. That's not really about worm ethics anymore, that's just being petty and cruel because your feelings got hurt.\n\nThe fact that you saw one of them get upset and you still didn't say anything until an edit\/update later says a lot. Good on you for actually apologizing and making it right, that matters. But yeah, you're in the wrong in the moment."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though it sounds like you already knew that going in. I think using \"retards\" as an insult is offensive regardless of context, and it sounds like it was made worse by being loud enough that they heard you talking about them to your friends rather than saying anything directly to them (or just letting it go, since it wasn't really your business how they handled their own dish).\n\nI hear you that you care about respecting the specimens, and that's a good instinct for someone in your field. That said, it seems like you approached their table uninvited, they were cold probably because it felt like you were policing them, and then you responded to that coldness by mocking them with a slur to your friends. At that point it feels less about worm ethics and more about being hurt and lashing out.\n\nI'd also gently point out that you noticed one of them looked upset in the moment, and it took until the edit for things to start getting made right. Still, it's genuinely good that you owned it, apologized, and cleared the air with them, that counts for a lot. But in the moment, yeah, you're in the wrong."},{"Description":"ord_3150","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3150","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_445","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though it sounds like you already knew that going in. I think using \"retards\" as an insult is offensive regardless of context, and it sounds like it was made worse by being loud enough that they heard you talking about them to your friends rather than saying anything directly to them (or just letting it go, since it wasn't really your business how they handled their own dish).\n\nI hear you that you care about respecting the specimens, and that's a good instinct for someone in your field. That said, it seems like you approached their table uninvited, they were cold probably because it felt like you were policing them, and then you responded to that coldness by mocking them with a slur to your friends. At that point it feels less about worm ethics and more about being hurt and lashing out.\n\nI'd also gently point out that you noticed one of them looked upset in the moment, and it took until the edit for things to start getting made right. Still, it's genuinely good that you owned it, apologized, and cleared the air with them, that counts for a lot. But in the moment, yeah, you're in the wrong."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, and it sounds like you already know it. Using \"retards\" as an insult is offensive regardless of the context, and you compounded it by being loud enough that they heard you talk about them behind their backs instead of just addressing them directly (or not at all, since it wasn't really your business how they handled their own dish).\n\nYour feelings about respecting specimens are valid and probably a good instinct for someone in your field. But you approached their table uninvited, they were cold because you were essentially policing them, and then you responded to that by mocking them with a slur to your friends. That's not really about worm ethics anymore, that's just being petty and cruel because your feelings got hurt.\n\nThe fact that you saw one of them get upset and you still didn't say anything until an edit\/update later says a lot. Good on you for actually apologizing and making it right, that matters. But yeah, you're in the wrong in the moment."},{"Description":"ord_3150","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3150","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_YyQZwvp8Q8u1LLS","FlowID":"FL_105","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_446","Description":"Item 75 (id 2019)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_447","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_448","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (Gently)\n\nLook, I get it. The totally tropical taste hits different during a heatwave. But let's break this down:\n\nThe case against you:\n- It's a shared fridge, in a shared business\n- Your customers are paying customers - presumably some of that money contributes to fridge-stocking\n- You literally listed SEVEN other beverage options, including Rio, which you correctly identified as \"poor man's Lilt\" but still a legitimate substitute in a pinch\n- Hiding it implies you knew this wasn't quite kosher - if it was fully fine, you'd just... drink it openly\n\nThe case for you:\n- Your business partner buggered off with the card, leaving you in a logistical nightmare\n- A heatwave is genuinely miserable and everyone becomes slightly unhinged about hydration\n- You did leave six other perfectly good options for customers\n- It's one can, not the whole stock\n\nVerdict: You're in the wrong, but only a little one. Like, a \"I'll judge you at brunch but still hang out with you\" mildly in the wrong. You've essentially rationed tropical fruit juice like it's the last chopper out of Saigon, while your paying customers are stuck choosing between Coke Zero and Lucozade.\n\nSuggested restitution: Come clean, buy a multipack with your own money when the card situation resolves, and maybe don't hide beverages from your clientele like a squirrel hoarding acorns. Your studio, your rules, sure - but sneaky rationing during a heatwave is peak \"small stakes, big drama\" behavior.\n\nAlso, side note - your business partner left you stranded without banking access during a heatwave? That feels like the bigger Am I wrong for situation here, but that's a whole separate thread."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (gently)\n\nI get it, honestly - the totally tropical taste really does hit different in a heatwave, and I think most people would feel a pang about losing access to it. You're right that your business partner leaving you without card access is a rough spot to be in, and a heatwave does make everyone a little unhinged about hydration. That's a fair point.\n\nThat said, here's where I land on the wrong side:\n\n- It's a shared fridge in a shared business, and your paying customers presumably have some claim on what's in there\n- You listed seven other options, including Rio, which by your own admission is a legitimate (if lesser) substitute - so it's not like customers were left with nothing\n- The hiding part is really what tips it for me - if you felt fine about it, you probably wouldn't have felt the need to hide the can. That instinct suggests you already knew it wasn't quite fair to your customers\n\nSo: you're in the wrong, but a small one. More \"side-eye at brunch\" than genuine villain. It's one can, not the whole stock, and the impulse behind it is very relatable.\n\nIf it were me, I'd probably come clean about it, maybe restock with my own money once the card situation sorts itself out, and try not to make a habit of squirreling away drinks from the people paying to be there.\n\nAlso - your business partner leaving you stranded without banking access during a heatwave feels like it might be its own Am I wrong for question, just a different one than the one you asked."},{"Description":"ord_2019","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2019","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_449","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (gently)\n\nI get it, honestly - the totally tropical taste really does hit different in a heatwave, and I think most people would feel a pang about losing access to it. You're right that your business partner leaving you without card access is a rough spot to be in, and a heatwave does make everyone a little unhinged about hydration. That's a fair point.\n\nThat said, here's where I land on the wrong side:\n\n- It's a shared fridge in a shared business, and your paying customers presumably have some claim on what's in there\n- You listed seven other options, including Rio, which by your own admission is a legitimate (if lesser) substitute - so it's not like customers were left with nothing\n- The hiding part is really what tips it for me - if you felt fine about it, you probably wouldn't have felt the need to hide the can. That instinct suggests you already knew it wasn't quite fair to your customers\n\nSo: you're in the wrong, but a small one. More \"side-eye at brunch\" than genuine villain. It's one can, not the whole stock, and the impulse behind it is very relatable.\n\nIf it were me, I'd probably come clean about it, maybe restock with my own money once the card situation sorts itself out, and try not to make a habit of squirreling away drinks from the people paying to be there.\n\nAlso - your business partner leaving you stranded without banking access during a heatwave feels like it might be its own Am I wrong for question, just a different one than the one you asked."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (Gently)\n\nLook, I get it. The totally tropical taste hits different during a heatwave. But let's break this down:\n\nThe case against you:\n- It's a shared fridge, in a shared business\n- Your customers are paying customers - presumably some of that money contributes to fridge-stocking\n- You literally listed SEVEN other beverage options, including Rio, which you correctly identified as \"poor man's Lilt\" but still a legitimate substitute in a pinch\n- Hiding it implies you knew this wasn't quite kosher - if it was fully fine, you'd just... drink it openly\n\nThe case for you:\n- Your business partner buggered off with the card, leaving you in a logistical nightmare\n- A heatwave is genuinely miserable and everyone becomes slightly unhinged about hydration\n- You did leave six other perfectly good options for customers\n- It's one can, not the whole stock\n\nVerdict: You're in the wrong, but only a little one. Like, a \"I'll judge you at brunch but still hang out with you\" mildly in the wrong. You've essentially rationed tropical fruit juice like it's the last chopper out of Saigon, while your paying customers are stuck choosing between Coke Zero and Lucozade.\n\nSuggested restitution: Come clean, buy a multipack with your own money when the card situation resolves, and maybe don't hide beverages from your clientele like a squirrel hoarding acorns. Your studio, your rules, sure - but sneaky rationing during a heatwave is peak \"small stakes, big drama\" behavior.\n\nAlso, side note - your business partner left you stranded without banking access during a heatwave? That feels like the bigger Am I wrong for situation here, but that's a whole separate thread."},{"Description":"ord_2019","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2019","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_3av7amaWKlDtMVt","FlowID":"FL_106","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_450","Description":"Item 76 (id 2486)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_451","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_452","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Here's the thing-you already know why \"calm down\" doesn't work. You said it yourself: it minimizes and dismisses whatever someone is feeling in the moment. You understand the mechanism perfectly when it's done to you, but you did it anyway and now you're looking for a technicality to excuse it (something like \"but I wasn't the one who caused her stress, so it doesn't count the same way\").\n\nIt still counts. Telling someone to calm down when they're stressed almost never lands as soothing-it lands as dismissive, even if your intentions were fine. She was pacing, anxious about time, snapped at you not because you caused the situation but because you were the nearest target for her stress. That's a very human (if not ideal) reaction. Your suggestion to use the kitchen sink was helpful. Repeating \"breathe, calm down\" after she'd already shushed you once wasn't really landing as help-it was more likely to be heard as \"your reaction is inconvenient for me right now.\"\n\nYou're not a villain here. You didn't cause her lateness, you tried to problem-solve, and she was short with you. That's understandable to feel annoyed about. But the specific act of telling her to calm down, after she'd already told you she didn't want to hear it, and after you personally know how that phrase feels from the receiving end-that part is on you.\n\nYou're in the wrong. Minor, but real. Probably better to just let her have the moment next time, or say something like \"let me know if you want help\" and step back."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear you that being shushed twice while you were just trying to help wasn't a fun position to be in, and it makes sense that you'd feel a bit stung by her directing frustration at you when you didn't cause the lateness.\n\nThat said, I think you already know why \"calm down\" lands badly-you said it yourself, that it minimizes and dismisses whatever someone is feeling in the moment. You get the mechanism perfectly when it's done to you, which makes it a little harder to fully excuse doing it to her, even with good intentions.\n\nIn the moment, she was pacing, anxious about time, and probably snapped at you not because you caused it but because you were simply the nearest person when the stress boiled over. That's a very human, if not ideal, reaction. Your kitchen-sink suggestion was genuinely helpful. But repeating \"breathe, calm down\" after she'd already shushed you once likely didn't land as soothing-it probably came across more like \"your reaction is inconvenient for me right now,\" even if that's not how you meant it.\n\nSo I don't think you're the villain here-you didn't cause her lateness, and you were trying to problem-solve while she was short with you. That's understandable to feel a little annoyed about. But the specific choice to keep telling her to calm down, after she'd already told you she didn't want to hear it, and after you personally know how that phrase feels from the receiving end, is the part I'd own.\n\nYou're slightly in the wrong, I think. Maybe next time it's easier to just let her have the moment, or offer something like \"let me know if you want help\" and step back."},{"Description":"ord_2486","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2486","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_453","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I hear you that being shushed twice while you were just trying to help wasn't a fun position to be in, and it makes sense that you'd feel a bit stung by her directing frustration at you when you didn't cause the lateness.\n\nThat said, I think you already know why \"calm down\" lands badly-you said it yourself, that it minimizes and dismisses whatever someone is feeling in the moment. You get the mechanism perfectly when it's done to you, which makes it a little harder to fully excuse doing it to her, even with good intentions.\n\nIn the moment, she was pacing, anxious about time, and probably snapped at you not because you caused it but because you were simply the nearest person when the stress boiled over. That's a very human, if not ideal, reaction. Your kitchen-sink suggestion was genuinely helpful. But repeating \"breathe, calm down\" after she'd already shushed you once likely didn't land as soothing-it probably came across more like \"your reaction is inconvenient for me right now,\" even if that's not how you meant it.\n\nSo I don't think you're the villain here-you didn't cause her lateness, and you were trying to problem-solve while she was short with you. That's understandable to feel a little annoyed about. But the specific choice to keep telling her to calm down, after she'd already told you she didn't want to hear it, and after you personally know how that phrase feels from the receiving end, is the part I'd own.\n\nYou're slightly in the wrong, I think. Maybe next time it's easier to just let her have the moment, or offer something like \"let me know if you want help\" and step back."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Here's the thing-you already know why \"calm down\" doesn't work. You said it yourself: it minimizes and dismisses whatever someone is feeling in the moment. You understand the mechanism perfectly when it's done to you, but you did it anyway and now you're looking for a technicality to excuse it (something like \"but I wasn't the one who caused her stress, so it doesn't count the same way\").\n\nIt still counts. Telling someone to calm down when they're stressed almost never lands as soothing-it lands as dismissive, even if your intentions were fine. She was pacing, anxious about time, snapped at you not because you caused the situation but because you were the nearest target for her stress. That's a very human (if not ideal) reaction. Your suggestion to use the kitchen sink was helpful. Repeating \"breathe, calm down\" after she'd already shushed you once wasn't really landing as help-it was more likely to be heard as \"your reaction is inconvenient for me right now.\"\n\nYou're not a villain here. You didn't cause her lateness, you tried to problem-solve, and she was short with you. That's understandable to feel annoyed about. But the specific act of telling her to calm down, after she'd already told you she didn't want to hear it, and after you personally know how that phrase feels from the receiving end-that part is on you.\n\nYou're in the wrong. Minor, but real. Probably better to just let her have the moment next time, or say something like \"let me know if you want help\" and step back."},{"Description":"ord_2486","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2486","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_bKu7EOLlAV383Vh","FlowID":"FL_107","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_454","Description":"Item 77 (id 1377)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_455","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_456","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nA server being friendly, smiley, and attentive is... her job. Restaurants train staff to be personable because it improves tips and gets people to come back. Unless she was doing something actually inappropriate (touching him, making explicit comments, ignoring you entirely for extended periods while fawning over him), \"overly smiley\" is just... customer service.\n\nYour boyfriend-the person actually being flirted with, if it was happening-didn't perceive it that way. That's a pretty significant data point you're choosing to ignore.\n\nPunishing her financially and with a passive-aggressive note based on your unverified perception of her demeanor is unfair to her. She works for tips; you deciding she was flirting (when the target of the alleged flirting disagreed) and then stiffing her over it is you taking out your insecurity on someone's paycheck.\n\nAnd Would I be wrong if you called to complain? Yes. You'd be reporting someone for doing her job, based on a vibe your own boyfriend told you wasn't real. If she gets in trouble or loses hours over this, that's a real consequence to her for something you can't actually substantiate.\n\nIf you genuinely feel insecure in your relationship or have trust issues that make you read into interactions like this, that's worth examining-but it's not this waitress's job to manage that for you, and she shouldn't lose income over it."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI get that in the moment it really did feel like flirting to you, and it clearly ruined your night - that's a rough way to end a nice dinner out. But a server being friendly, smiley, and attentive is largely just her job. Restaurants train staff to be personable because it improves tips and gets people to come back. Unless she was doing something actually inappropriate (touching him, explicit comments, ignoring you for extended periods while fawning over him), \"overly smiley\" reads as customer service rather than flirting.\n\nHere's the thing though: your boyfriend, the person actually on the receiving end if it was happening, didn't perceive it that way. That's worth sitting with, even if it's frustrating to hear.\n\nPunishing her financially and with a note based on your read of her demeanor, when the person she was allegedly flirting with disagreed, feels unfair to her. She works for tips, and she's now out one because of a perception that isn't shared by anyone else involved.\n\nAs for calling to complain - I think that would also put you in the wrong. You'd be reporting her for doing her job based on a read your own boyfriend told you wasn't accurate. If she loses hours or gets in trouble over this, that's a real cost to her for something that's hard to substantiate.\n\nIf you find you often feel this uneasy in situations like this, it might be worth exploring why - not because you're wrong to have feelings about it, but so you're not carrying that weight (or passing consequences onto servers) going forward."},{"Description":"ord_1377","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1377","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_457","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI get that in the moment it really did feel like flirting to you, and it clearly ruined your night - that's a rough way to end a nice dinner out. But a server being friendly, smiley, and attentive is largely just her job. Restaurants train staff to be personable because it improves tips and gets people to come back. Unless she was doing something actually inappropriate (touching him, explicit comments, ignoring you for extended periods while fawning over him), \"overly smiley\" reads as customer service rather than flirting.\n\nHere's the thing though: your boyfriend, the person actually on the receiving end if it was happening, didn't perceive it that way. That's worth sitting with, even if it's frustrating to hear.\n\nPunishing her financially and with a note based on your read of her demeanor, when the person she was allegedly flirting with disagreed, feels unfair to her. She works for tips, and she's now out one because of a perception that isn't shared by anyone else involved.\n\nAs for calling to complain - I think that would also put you in the wrong. You'd be reporting her for doing her job based on a read your own boyfriend told you wasn't accurate. If she loses hours or gets in trouble over this, that's a real cost to her for something that's hard to substantiate.\n\nIf you find you often feel this uneasy in situations like this, it might be worth exploring why - not because you're wrong to have feelings about it, but so you're not carrying that weight (or passing consequences onto servers) going forward."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nA server being friendly, smiley, and attentive is... her job. Restaurants train staff to be personable because it improves tips and gets people to come back. Unless she was doing something actually inappropriate (touching him, making explicit comments, ignoring you entirely for extended periods while fawning over him), \"overly smiley\" is just... customer service.\n\nYour boyfriend-the person actually being flirted with, if it was happening-didn't perceive it that way. That's a pretty significant data point you're choosing to ignore.\n\nPunishing her financially and with a passive-aggressive note based on your unverified perception of her demeanor is unfair to her. She works for tips; you deciding she was flirting (when the target of the alleged flirting disagreed) and then stiffing her over it is you taking out your insecurity on someone's paycheck.\n\nAnd Would I be wrong if you called to complain? Yes. You'd be reporting someone for doing her job, based on a vibe your own boyfriend told you wasn't real. If she gets in trouble or loses hours over this, that's a real consequence to her for something you can't actually substantiate.\n\nIf you genuinely feel insecure in your relationship or have trust issues that make you read into interactions like this, that's worth examining-but it's not this waitress's job to manage that for you, and she shouldn't lose income over it."},{"Description":"ord_1377","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1377","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_XZmslLlMAgmjj0y","FlowID":"FL_108","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_458","Description":"Item 78 (id 602)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_459","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_460","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Here's an honest take: you're in the wrong, but mildly, and it's fixable.\n\nHere's the thing - paying bills and doing chores aren't actually substitutes for each other. They're different contributions to a household, and living in a home involves both financial upkeep AND physical upkeep. The fact that you contribute money doesn't mean the dishes stop needing to be washed or the bathroom stops needing to be cleaned. Someone still has to do it, and \"I paid for groceries so I shouldn't have to help cook\" isn't really how shared living works.\n\nThat said, your frustration isn't baseless. It sounds like the real issue is fairness among siblings - you're working, going to school, AND contributing financially, while your younger siblings do none of that and also skip chores. That's a legitimate grievance. If that's true, the fix isn't \"I pay so I'm exempt,\" it's \"everyone who lives here does age-appropriate chores, and it's not fair that I'm doing more of everything.\"\n\nA few things worth sitting with:\n- Does your mom actually need that money from you, or is this more about teaching responsibility? That changes the conversation a lot.\n- \"I go to school\" is valid, but so is \"I clean the bathroom once a week\" - these aren't mutually exclusive.\n- The stronger, more persuasive argument to your mom isn't \"I pay so I shouldn't clean,\" it's \"It's unfair that I'm contributing money AND chores while my siblings do neither.\"\n\nYou're not the world's biggest jerk here, but the \"I pay, therefore I'm exempt\" logic is where you're losing the argument - and probably why your mom pushed back hard."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're slightly in the wrong, but honestly a pretty fixable one.\n\nI hear you that you're contributing financially, going to school, and it feels like your siblings get a pass while you're doing more of everything - that frustration seems real and worth taking seriously.\n\nThat said, I think paying bills and doing chores aren't really substitutes for each other. They're just different kinds of contributions to a household, and living somewhere tends to involve both. The money you put in doesn't make the dishes or the bathroom stop needing attention - someone still has to do that work, and \"I paid for groceries so I shouldn't have to help cook\" isn't quite how shared living usually works.\n\nA couple things that might be worth sitting with:\n- Does your mom actually need that money from you, or is part of this about her wanting you to build responsibility? That might change how you think about the ask.\n- \"I go to school\" is a real constraint, but it doesn't necessarily rule out something small and consistent, like handling the bathroom once a week.\n- I think the stronger case to make to your mom isn't \"I pay, so I shouldn't have to clean\" - it's \"It's not fair that I'm contributing money and chores while my siblings do neither.\" That's the actual issue, and it's a legitimate one.\n\nYou're not the villain here, but I think the \"I pay, therefore I'm exempt\" framing is where the argument loses ground - and probably why your mom pushed back so hard."},{"Description":"ord_602","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_602","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_461","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're slightly in the wrong, but honestly a pretty fixable one.\n\nI hear you that you're contributing financially, going to school, and it feels like your siblings get a pass while you're doing more of everything - that frustration seems real and worth taking seriously.\n\nThat said, I think paying bills and doing chores aren't really substitutes for each other. They're just different kinds of contributions to a household, and living somewhere tends to involve both. The money you put in doesn't make the dishes or the bathroom stop needing attention - someone still has to do that work, and \"I paid for groceries so I shouldn't have to help cook\" isn't quite how shared living usually works.\n\nA couple things that might be worth sitting with:\n- Does your mom actually need that money from you, or is part of this about her wanting you to build responsibility? That might change how you think about the ask.\n- \"I go to school\" is a real constraint, but it doesn't necessarily rule out something small and consistent, like handling the bathroom once a week.\n- I think the stronger case to make to your mom isn't \"I pay, so I shouldn't have to clean\" - it's \"It's not fair that I'm contributing money and chores while my siblings do neither.\" That's the actual issue, and it's a legitimate one.\n\nYou're not the villain here, but I think the \"I pay, therefore I'm exempt\" framing is where the argument loses ground - and probably why your mom pushed back so hard."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Here's an honest take: you're in the wrong, but mildly, and it's fixable.\n\nHere's the thing - paying bills and doing chores aren't actually substitutes for each other. They're different contributions to a household, and living in a home involves both financial upkeep AND physical upkeep. The fact that you contribute money doesn't mean the dishes stop needing to be washed or the bathroom stops needing to be cleaned. Someone still has to do it, and \"I paid for groceries so I shouldn't have to help cook\" isn't really how shared living works.\n\nThat said, your frustration isn't baseless. It sounds like the real issue is fairness among siblings - you're working, going to school, AND contributing financially, while your younger siblings do none of that and also skip chores. That's a legitimate grievance. If that's true, the fix isn't \"I pay so I'm exempt,\" it's \"everyone who lives here does age-appropriate chores, and it's not fair that I'm doing more of everything.\"\n\nA few things worth sitting with:\n- Does your mom actually need that money from you, or is this more about teaching responsibility? That changes the conversation a lot.\n- \"I go to school\" is valid, but so is \"I clean the bathroom once a week\" - these aren't mutually exclusive.\n- The stronger, more persuasive argument to your mom isn't \"I pay so I shouldn't clean,\" it's \"It's unfair that I'm contributing money AND chores while my siblings do neither.\"\n\nYou're not the world's biggest jerk here, but the \"I pay, therefore I'm exempt\" logic is where you're losing the argument - and probably why your mom pushed back hard."},{"Description":"ord_602","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_602","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_Y4UBzltXXWwNx6I","FlowID":"FL_109","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_462","Description":"Item 79 (id 2516)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_463","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_464","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This is a tricky one, but I don't think you'd be in the wrong for having a conversation about it - you would be in the wrong if you demanded or pushed hard after she's heard you out.\n\nYou have a legitimate personal connection to this name. It's not that you dislike the name aesthetically or have a random superstition - your husband watched his brother die at age 7, then his parents replaced that child with a same-named sibling with the same birthday, and THAT child got sick too until they changed his name. That's a genuinely heavy, painful family history, not just \"baby omen\" folklore you picked up somewhere.\n\nIt's reasonable to:\n- Tell your sister once, clearly, why this name carries weight for your husband specifically\n- Explain that it's not about telling her how to parent, but that you're asking as family who lived through this\n- Let her know it might be hard for your husband to hear that name directed at a new baby, given his history\n\nIt's NOT reasonable to:\n- Repeatedly bring it up if she says no\n- Get cold or resentful toward her or the baby if she keeps the name\n- Frame it as \"you HAVE to remove it\" rather than \"please consider removing it\"\n\nUltimately it is her child and her choice. But sharing real, personal trauma around a specific name isn't the same as generic name-shaming. She might not have fully considered how it would land for your husband emotionally, separate from the \"omen\" angle.\n\nHave the conversation once, be honest about why it matters to your husband, then respect whatever she decides. If she keeps the name, that's her right, and you'll need to make peace with it for the sake of the relationship."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you wouldn't be in the wrong for having this conversation with her - it seems like you'd only be in the wrong if you pushed hard or kept bringing it up after she's heard you out.\n\nYou mentioned you're prepared to be told this is their kid and their choice, and I think that's a fair thing to hold onto. It is ultimately her decision to make. But I'd also say you have a legitimate personal connection to this name here, not just a random superstition. Your husband watched his brother die at age 7, then his parents named the next child the same name with the same birthday, and that child got sick too until they changed his name. That's a heavy, specific family history, not just \"baby omen\" folklore.\n\nSo it seems reasonable to:\n- Tell your sister once, clearly, why this name carries weight for your husband specifically\n- Frame it as sharing something painful as family who lived through it, not as instructions on how to parent\n- Let her know it might be hard for your husband to hear that name on a new baby, given what he went through\n\nIt probably wouldn't land well to:\n- Bring it up again and again if she says no\n- Go cold or resentful toward her or the baby if she keeps the name\n- Say \"you have to remove it\" instead of \"please consider it\"\n\nShe may not have fully weighed how this would feel for your husband emotionally, apart from the omen angle, so it's worth naming that clearly once. After that, I think the kindest path is to be honest with her, then really let her decide and make peace with whatever she chooses, for the relationship's sake."},{"Description":"ord_2516","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2516","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_465","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you wouldn't be in the wrong for having this conversation with her - it seems like you'd only be in the wrong if you pushed hard or kept bringing it up after she's heard you out.\n\nYou mentioned you're prepared to be told this is their kid and their choice, and I think that's a fair thing to hold onto. It is ultimately her decision to make. But I'd also say you have a legitimate personal connection to this name here, not just a random superstition. Your husband watched his brother die at age 7, then his parents named the next child the same name with the same birthday, and that child got sick too until they changed his name. That's a heavy, specific family history, not just \"baby omen\" folklore.\n\nSo it seems reasonable to:\n- Tell your sister once, clearly, why this name carries weight for your husband specifically\n- Frame it as sharing something painful as family who lived through it, not as instructions on how to parent\n- Let her know it might be hard for your husband to hear that name on a new baby, given what he went through\n\nIt probably wouldn't land well to:\n- Bring it up again and again if she says no\n- Go cold or resentful toward her or the baby if she keeps the name\n- Say \"you have to remove it\" instead of \"please consider it\"\n\nShe may not have fully weighed how this would feel for your husband emotionally, apart from the omen angle, so it's worth naming that clearly once. After that, I think the kindest path is to be honest with her, then really let her decide and make peace with whatever she chooses, for the relationship's sake."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"This is a tricky one, but I don't think you'd be in the wrong for having a conversation about it - you would be in the wrong if you demanded or pushed hard after she's heard you out.\n\nYou have a legitimate personal connection to this name. It's not that you dislike the name aesthetically or have a random superstition - your husband watched his brother die at age 7, then his parents replaced that child with a same-named sibling with the same birthday, and THAT child got sick too until they changed his name. That's a genuinely heavy, painful family history, not just \"baby omen\" folklore you picked up somewhere.\n\nIt's reasonable to:\n- Tell your sister once, clearly, why this name carries weight for your husband specifically\n- Explain that it's not about telling her how to parent, but that you're asking as family who lived through this\n- Let her know it might be hard for your husband to hear that name directed at a new baby, given his history\n\nIt's NOT reasonable to:\n- Repeatedly bring it up if she says no\n- Get cold or resentful toward her or the baby if she keeps the name\n- Frame it as \"you HAVE to remove it\" rather than \"please consider removing it\"\n\nUltimately it is her child and her choice. But sharing real, personal trauma around a specific name isn't the same as generic name-shaming. She might not have fully considered how it would land for your husband emotionally, separate from the \"omen\" angle.\n\nHave the conversation once, be honest about why it matters to your husband, then respect whatever she decides. If she keeps the name, that's her right, and you'll need to make peace with it for the sake of the relationship."},{"Description":"ord_2516","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2516","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_bVLW6iODPYuHP2k","FlowID":"FL_110","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_466","Description":"Item 80 (id 1738)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_467","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_468","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYour sister didn't hijack your wedding; she used a personal, vulnerable story to illustrate what an incredible, compassionate man your husband is and why she knew he was the right partner for you.\n\nUsing a personal anecdote to show the groom's character is a very standard Maid of Honor speech format. Even if you felt bringing up mental illness was too heavy for a wedding, her intent was clearly to honor your husband and celebrate your marriage.\n\nHere is why your reaction makes you in the wrong:\n\n1. The guests' reaction was normal human empathy, not a betrayal. Guests spending a few minutes telling her she was brave doesn't mean your entire wedding was ruined or that you weren't the center of attention for the other 95% of the day.\n2. Kicking her out solved nothing. The speech was already over. Kicking your own sister-your Maid of Honor-out of the reception was purely punitive, driven by jealousy and resentment that the spotlight shifted away from you for a brief moment.\n3. Your husband disagrees with you. The story was about him being a supportive brother-in-law, and even he thinks you went way too far.\n4. She left quietly. If she were truly a selfish drama queen trying to make the day about herself, she would have caused a massive scene when you kicked her out. Instead, she took the high road and left.\n\nYou let your insecurity and obsession with having 100% of the attention ruin a milestone moment and deeply damage your relationship with your sister. You owe her a massive apology."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI understand your perspective, and I agree that bringing up topics like mental illness can sometimes feel heavy or out of place during a wedding celebration. It is completely natural to want your wedding day to remain focused on your new marriage.\n\nHowever, it seems that your sister's intention was not to hijack your day, but rather to use a vulnerable personal story to illustrate what a compassionate and supportive man your husband is and why she knew he was the right partner for you. Sharing an anecdote to highlight the groom's character is a very standard format for a Maid of Honor speech.\n\nHere is why your response was too harsh:\n\n The guests' reaction was brief empathy: Guests taking a few moments to offer kind words to your sister does not mean the entire day stopped being about you and your husband. It was just a natural, momentary human reaction.\n Asking her to leave did not resolve the situation: Because the speech had already ended, having your sister and Maid of Honor leave the reception was purely punitive.\n Your husband's perspective is important: The story directly involved your husband being a caring brother-in-law, and he also felt that kicking her out went too far.\n She handled the exit gracefully: Rather than creating a disruption or arguing, she respected your request and left quietly.\n\nWhile it is understandable to feel sensitive about the focus shifting at your reception, asking your sister to leave caused unnecessary harm to your relationship. To help move forward and repair that bond, it would be best to offer her a sincere apology."},{"Description":"ord_1738","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1738","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_469","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI understand your perspective, and I agree that bringing up topics like mental illness can sometimes feel heavy or out of place during a wedding celebration. It is completely natural to want your wedding day to remain focused on your new marriage.\n\nHowever, it seems that your sister's intention was not to hijack your day, but rather to use a vulnerable personal story to illustrate what a compassionate and supportive man your husband is and why she knew he was the right partner for you. Sharing an anecdote to highlight the groom's character is a very standard format for a Maid of Honor speech.\n\nHere is why your response was too harsh:\n\n The guests' reaction was brief empathy: Guests taking a few moments to offer kind words to your sister does not mean the entire day stopped being about you and your husband. It was just a natural, momentary human reaction.\n Asking her to leave did not resolve the situation: Because the speech had already ended, having your sister and Maid of Honor leave the reception was purely punitive.\n Your husband's perspective is important: The story directly involved your husband being a caring brother-in-law, and he also felt that kicking her out went too far.\n She handled the exit gracefully: Rather than creating a disruption or arguing, she respected your request and left quietly.\n\nWhile it is understandable to feel sensitive about the focus shifting at your reception, asking your sister to leave caused unnecessary harm to your relationship. To help move forward and repair that bond, it would be best to offer her a sincere apology."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nYour sister didn't hijack your wedding; she used a personal, vulnerable story to illustrate what an incredible, compassionate man your husband is and why she knew he was the right partner for you.\n\nUsing a personal anecdote to show the groom's character is a very standard Maid of Honor speech format. Even if you felt bringing up mental illness was too heavy for a wedding, her intent was clearly to honor your husband and celebrate your marriage.\n\nHere is why your reaction makes you in the wrong:\n\n1. The guests' reaction was normal human empathy, not a betrayal. Guests spending a few minutes telling her she was brave doesn't mean your entire wedding was ruined or that you weren't the center of attention for the other 95% of the day.\n2. Kicking her out solved nothing. The speech was already over. Kicking your own sister-your Maid of Honor-out of the reception was purely punitive, driven by jealousy and resentment that the spotlight shifted away from you for a brief moment.\n3. Your husband disagrees with you. The story was about him being a supportive brother-in-law, and even he thinks you went way too far.\n4. She left quietly. If she were truly a selfish drama queen trying to make the day about herself, she would have caused a massive scene when you kicked her out. Instead, she took the high road and left.\n\nYou let your insecurity and obsession with having 100% of the attention ruin a milestone moment and deeply damage your relationship with your sister. You owe her a massive apology."},{"Description":"ord_1738","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1738","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_1TZzNr47Evar9RG","FlowID":"FL_111","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_470","Description":"Item 81 (id 1049)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_471","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_472","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Massively.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why:\n\n1. It was your wedding anniversary: You left your wife at home to solo-parent your three children all day and night on your own anniversary, after specifically telling her you would be home shortly after the 11:00 AM ceremony.\n2. \"Time got away from me\" does not cover 12 hours: An 11:00 AM ceremony means you should have been home around 1:00 PM or 2:00 PM at the latest. Staying until midnight is not a minor slip of time-it is an entire half of a day. The sun literally set, afternoon turned into night, and at no point did you think, \"Hey, it's 7:00 PM, I told my wife I'd be home hours ago on our anniversary\"?\n3. You caused genuine panic: Your wife was so worried about your whereabouts that she had to call a mutual friend to find out if you were okay. Leaving a spouse completely in the dark for 12+ hours when they expect you home in two is terrifying.\n4. The \"metal roof\" excuse is insulting: The issue isn't that your phone had bad reception; the issue is that you never tried to check in. If you had stepped outside at 1:00 PM, 3:00 PM, or 6:00 PM to call her, you would have realized you had no service and found a way to reach her. Blaming the building's roof ignores the fact that you completely forgot your family and your commitments for 13 hours.\n\nYou completely blew off your anniversary, abandoned your wife with three kids for the entire day, vanished without communication, and are now making excuses instead of taking accountability.\n\nYou owe your wife a massive, unreserved apology, with zero excuses about cell reception."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong in this situation.\n\nI acknowledge that you felt time got away from you during the event and that the building's metal roof blocked your cell reception. I also agree with you that the plan you set was to attend the 11:00 AM ceremony and then return home so you could spend your anniversary together as a family with your three kids.\n\nHowever, staying until midnight meant leaving your wife to solo-parent all day and evening without any updates, which understandably caused her enough worry that she felt she needed to call a mutual friend to find out where you were. While time can sometimes slip by at social gatherings, twelve hours is a very long stretch to go without checking in. The core issue seems to be that stepping outside at some point during the day to check your phone would have allowed you to stay in touch and follow through on the plans you made.\n\nTaking full accountability and offering her a sincere, unreserved apology-without focusing on the cell reception-would be the most positive step forward to address the situation."},{"Description":"ord_1049","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1049","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_473","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think you're in the wrong in this situation.\n\nI acknowledge that you felt time got away from you during the event and that the building's metal roof blocked your cell reception. I also agree with you that the plan you set was to attend the 11:00 AM ceremony and then return home so you could spend your anniversary together as a family with your three kids.\n\nHowever, staying until midnight meant leaving your wife to solo-parent all day and evening without any updates, which understandably caused her enough worry that she felt she needed to call a mutual friend to find out where you were. While time can sometimes slip by at social gatherings, twelve hours is a very long stretch to go without checking in. The core issue seems to be that stepping outside at some point during the day to check your phone would have allowed you to stay in touch and follow through on the plans you made.\n\nTaking full accountability and offering her a sincere, unreserved apology-without focusing on the cell reception-would be the most positive step forward to address the situation."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Massively.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why:\n\n1. It was your wedding anniversary: You left your wife at home to solo-parent your three children all day and night on your own anniversary, after specifically telling her you would be home shortly after the 11:00 AM ceremony.\n2. \"Time got away from me\" does not cover 12 hours: An 11:00 AM ceremony means you should have been home around 1:00 PM or 2:00 PM at the latest. Staying until midnight is not a minor slip of time-it is an entire half of a day. The sun literally set, afternoon turned into night, and at no point did you think, \"Hey, it's 7:00 PM, I told my wife I'd be home hours ago on our anniversary\"?\n3. You caused genuine panic: Your wife was so worried about your whereabouts that she had to call a mutual friend to find out if you were okay. Leaving a spouse completely in the dark for 12+ hours when they expect you home in two is terrifying.\n4. The \"metal roof\" excuse is insulting: The issue isn't that your phone had bad reception; the issue is that you never tried to check in. If you had stepped outside at 1:00 PM, 3:00 PM, or 6:00 PM to call her, you would have realized you had no service and found a way to reach her. Blaming the building's roof ignores the fact that you completely forgot your family and your commitments for 13 hours.\n\nYou completely blew off your anniversary, abandoned your wife with three kids for the entire day, vanished without communication, and are now making excuses instead of taking accountability.\n\nYou owe your wife a massive, unreserved apology, with zero excuses about cell reception."},{"Description":"ord_1049","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1049","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_JedN3el8ZvnCnFG","FlowID":"FL_112","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_474","Description":"Item 82 (id 3012)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_475","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_476","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (though this is a hilarious, low-stakes situation).\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. Pickles are wet and messy: Handing someone a whole, dripping pickle in a living room without at least a napkin or a small plate is already risky for your furniture and their hands.\n2. Basic hosting etiquette: When you host someone and offer them food, providing basic tableware upon request is standard. Yes, eating a whole pickle with a knife and fork is eccentric, but it's not an unreasonable or difficult request to fulfill.\n3. The dishwashing argument was petty: You made a stand over washing a single plate, a knife, and a fork. That takes roughly 20 seconds to rinse and put in the dishwasher or wash by hand. Cross-examining your guest with \"Were you planning on washing those?\" was needlessly combative and patronizing.\n\nYour friend has a quirky way of eating pickles. The normal, hospitable reaction is to laugh, give him a hard time playfully, hand him the plate and fork, and watch him eat a pickle like a Victorian aristocrat. Instead, you turned a 10-second favor into a hostile standoff."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though it seems like a very funny, low-stakes situation overall.\n\nI can certainly understand your perspective: to you, creating three dirty dishes for a single snack seems unnecessary, and I agree that eating a pickle with a knife and fork is definitely an eccentric preference.\n\nHowever, it still seems that you were in the wrong here for a few reasons:\n\n1. Pickles are often wet and messy: Handing someone a whole pickle in a living room without a plate or napkin can easily lead to dripping on hands or furniture, so wanting a barrier is fairly practical.\n2. Standard hospitality includes basic tableware: Even though his preference is quirky, being a welcoming host usually involves accommodating simple requests for dishes and cutlery.\n3. The reaction felt a bit combative: Asking him if he planned to wash the dishes seemed needlessly sharp over something that takes roughly twenty seconds to rinse or load into a dishwasher.\n\nIn situations like this, it is usually much better to just share a laugh, playfully tease him about his formal eating style, and hand over the plate and fork so you can both enjoy the hangout."},{"Description":"ord_3012","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3012","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_477","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though it seems like a very funny, low-stakes situation overall.\n\nI can certainly understand your perspective: to you, creating three dirty dishes for a single snack seems unnecessary, and I agree that eating a pickle with a knife and fork is definitely an eccentric preference.\n\nHowever, it still seems that you were in the wrong here for a few reasons:\n\n1. Pickles are often wet and messy: Handing someone a whole pickle in a living room without a plate or napkin can easily lead to dripping on hands or furniture, so wanting a barrier is fairly practical.\n2. Standard hospitality includes basic tableware: Even though his preference is quirky, being a welcoming host usually involves accommodating simple requests for dishes and cutlery.\n3. The reaction felt a bit combative: Asking him if he planned to wash the dishes seemed needlessly sharp over something that takes roughly twenty seconds to rinse or load into a dishwasher.\n\nIn situations like this, it is usually much better to just share a laugh, playfully tease him about his formal eating style, and hand over the plate and fork so you can both enjoy the hangout."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (though this is a hilarious, low-stakes situation).\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. Pickles are wet and messy: Handing someone a whole, dripping pickle in a living room without at least a napkin or a small plate is already risky for your furniture and their hands.\n2. Basic hosting etiquette: When you host someone and offer them food, providing basic tableware upon request is standard. Yes, eating a whole pickle with a knife and fork is eccentric, but it's not an unreasonable or difficult request to fulfill.\n3. The dishwashing argument was petty: You made a stand over washing a single plate, a knife, and a fork. That takes roughly 20 seconds to rinse and put in the dishwasher or wash by hand. Cross-examining your guest with \"Were you planning on washing those?\" was needlessly combative and patronizing.\n\nYour friend has a quirky way of eating pickles. The normal, hospitable reaction is to laugh, give him a hard time playfully, hand him the plate and fork, and watch him eat a pickle like a Victorian aristocrat. Instead, you turned a 10-second favor into a hostile standoff."},{"Description":"ord_3012","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3012","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_uOlbpvs3LXyz5e9","FlowID":"FL_113","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_478","Description":"Item 83 (id 2473)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_479","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_480","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You arrived at the absolute last second\nArriving at 7:59 PM for a service that closes at 8:00 PM-especially when there is already a line-is cutting it too close. Check cashing takes several minutes per person (verifying ID, entering information, printing, counting cash). The worker had to draw the line somewhere so she could balance her drawer and go home on time. The people already in line before you got served; you were behind the cutoff.\n\n2. \"Out of money\" is relative to the check amount\nRegisters have strict cash limits at the end of a shift, and check-cashing desks frequently do cash drops to keep limited amounts on hand for safety. If your dad's check was for $500 and the next guy's check was for $60, she genuinely may not have had enough cash in the drawer for your dad's check, but had just enough for the other person.\n\n3. Your reaction was immature and hostile\n The labeling: Calling an hourly retail worker a \"B****\" in your post simply for doing her job and enforcing closing times shows a lack of respect.\n The hovering and comments: Hovering around the counter to monitor her transactions and loudly making passive-aggressive remarks (\"Guess she found some more money\") is childish and intimidating behavior aimed at someone who cannot easily argue back without risking their job.\n\nIt is completely understandable to feel stressed and frustrated when you need that money for dinner, but taking that frustration out on a retail employee who is just following closing protocol makes you in the wrong here."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI can completely understand why your family felt upset in that moment. As you mentioned, you were counting on that money to buy dinner, and I agree that seeing the next person get their check cashed after you were told the desk was closed and out of funds would feel very frustrating.\n\nHowever, looking at the situation from the store's perspective, I think the employee was just doing her job:\n\n Timing: Arriving at 7:59 PM for a service that closes at 8:00 PM-especially when there are already people waiting-seems to be cutting it too close. Processing a check takes several minutes to verify ID and count cash, and workers sometimes have to set a firm cutoff so they can balance their registers and finish their shifts on time.\n Cash availability: \"Out of money\" is often relative to the specific amount written on a check. For safety reasons, registers have strict cash limits, so it seems possible she simply didn't have enough funds left in her drawer to cover your dad's amount, but had enough for a smaller check.\n* The response: While it is natural to feel annoyed, it is much better to treat retail staff with patience. Hovering near the counter to monitor her, making loud passive-aggressive remarks, and calling the worker names takes out understandable personal stress on an employee who was likely just adhering to store closing policies."},{"Description":"ord_2473","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2473","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_481","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI can completely understand why your family felt upset in that moment. As you mentioned, you were counting on that money to buy dinner, and I agree that seeing the next person get their check cashed after you were told the desk was closed and out of funds would feel very frustrating.\n\nHowever, looking at the situation from the store's perspective, I think the employee was just doing her job:\n\n Timing: Arriving at 7:59 PM for a service that closes at 8:00 PM-especially when there are already people waiting-seems to be cutting it too close. Processing a check takes several minutes to verify ID and count cash, and workers sometimes have to set a firm cutoff so they can balance their registers and finish their shifts on time.\n Cash availability: \"Out of money\" is often relative to the specific amount written on a check. For safety reasons, registers have strict cash limits, so it seems possible she simply didn't have enough funds left in her drawer to cover your dad's amount, but had enough for a smaller check.\n* The response: While it is natural to feel annoyed, it is much better to treat retail staff with patience. Hovering near the counter to monitor her, making loud passive-aggressive remarks, and calling the worker names takes out understandable personal stress on an employee who was likely just adhering to store closing policies."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You arrived at the absolute last second\nArriving at 7:59 PM for a service that closes at 8:00 PM-especially when there is already a line-is cutting it too close. Check cashing takes several minutes per person (verifying ID, entering information, printing, counting cash). The worker had to draw the line somewhere so she could balance her drawer and go home on time. The people already in line before you got served; you were behind the cutoff.\n\n2. \"Out of money\" is relative to the check amount\nRegisters have strict cash limits at the end of a shift, and check-cashing desks frequently do cash drops to keep limited amounts on hand for safety. If your dad's check was for $500 and the next guy's check was for $60, she genuinely may not have had enough cash in the drawer for your dad's check, but had just enough for the other person.\n\n3. Your reaction was immature and hostile\n The labeling: Calling an hourly retail worker a \"B****\" in your post simply for doing her job and enforcing closing times shows a lack of respect.\n The hovering and comments: Hovering around the counter to monitor her transactions and loudly making passive-aggressive remarks (\"Guess she found some more money\") is childish and intimidating behavior aimed at someone who cannot easily argue back without risking their job.\n\nIt is completely understandable to feel stressed and frustrated when you need that money for dinner, but taking that frustration out on a retail employee who is just following closing protocol makes you in the wrong here."},{"Description":"ord_2473","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2473","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_S4B8fVWsvuF5Zkr","FlowID":"FL_114","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_482","Description":"Item 84 (id 916)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_483","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_484","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Your mom is completely right, and you need a major attitude check.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. Your daughter invaded a stranger's personal space: The necklace was around the other child's neck. Grabbing something off another person's body without asking is not okay-it is invasive, startling, and could have choked or hurt the other kid.\n2. Your daughter is 8, not a toddler: An 8-year-old is old enough to understand personal boundaries and knows you must ask before touching other people or their belongings.\n3. The other child's reaction was completely justified: A stranger ran up, invaded her personal space, and put their hands near her throat\/chest. Yelling and backing away is a completely natural reaction to feeling startled or threatened. She didn't \"make a scene\"; she reacted to being grabbed.\n4. You failed to parent in that moment: When your child does something inappropriate, your job as a parent is to step in, use it as a teachable moment for your child, and offer a basic, polite apology to the other family (e.g., \"I'm so sorry, she was just excited to see your necklace, but she knows she shouldn't touch people without asking. Are you okay?\").\n5. Your hostile attitude: Saying the other mother \"didn't dare say anything to me\" sounds aggressively defensive. You were the one in the wrong. The other mother was likely just shocked by your daughter's behavior and your lack of basic manners.\n\nTeach your daughter about personal boundaries and consent, and lead by example by learning how to apologize when you or your child make a mistake."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI can understand that your daughter was likely just excited and curious to look at the flashing necklace, and I hear that it felt to you like the other child made an unnecessary scene. However, I think your mother makes a valid point here.\n\nHere is why it seems like an apology was needed:\n\n1. Personal space and safety: Because the necklace was physically around the other child's neck, running up and grabbing it can easily feel startling, invasive, and potentially unsafe.\n2. Age-appropriate boundaries: At 8 years old, it seems fair to expect that a child can understand the importance of asking before touching someone else or their belongings.\n3. The other child's reaction: When a stranger suddenly reaches toward someone's neck or chest, yelling and stepping back is a very natural and understandable reaction to feeling surprised or threatened.\n4. The opportunity to guide your child: In moments like these, it is really helpful to step in, offer a quick and courteous apology to the other family, and use the situation as a constructive learning moment for your daughter about respecting personal space.\n5. Managing defensiveness: It seems like thinking the other mother \"didn't dare\" say anything comes across as unnecessarily defensive. It is more likely that she was simply startled by what happened and hoped for a standard polite acknowledgment.\n\nTaking responsibility and offering a simple apology when a mistake happens is a great way to model good manners and healthy boundaries for your daughter."},{"Description":"ord_916","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_916","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_485","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI can understand that your daughter was likely just excited and curious to look at the flashing necklace, and I hear that it felt to you like the other child made an unnecessary scene. However, I think your mother makes a valid point here.\n\nHere is why it seems like an apology was needed:\n\n1. Personal space and safety: Because the necklace was physically around the other child's neck, running up and grabbing it can easily feel startling, invasive, and potentially unsafe.\n2. Age-appropriate boundaries: At 8 years old, it seems fair to expect that a child can understand the importance of asking before touching someone else or their belongings.\n3. The other child's reaction: When a stranger suddenly reaches toward someone's neck or chest, yelling and stepping back is a very natural and understandable reaction to feeling surprised or threatened.\n4. The opportunity to guide your child: In moments like these, it is really helpful to step in, offer a quick and courteous apology to the other family, and use the situation as a constructive learning moment for your daughter about respecting personal space.\n5. Managing defensiveness: It seems like thinking the other mother \"didn't dare\" say anything comes across as unnecessarily defensive. It is more likely that she was simply startled by what happened and hoped for a standard polite acknowledgment.\n\nTaking responsibility and offering a simple apology when a mistake happens is a great way to model good manners and healthy boundaries for your daughter."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong. Your mom is completely right, and you need a major attitude check.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. Your daughter invaded a stranger's personal space: The necklace was around the other child's neck. Grabbing something off another person's body without asking is not okay-it is invasive, startling, and could have choked or hurt the other kid.\n2. Your daughter is 8, not a toddler: An 8-year-old is old enough to understand personal boundaries and knows you must ask before touching other people or their belongings.\n3. The other child's reaction was completely justified: A stranger ran up, invaded her personal space, and put their hands near her throat\/chest. Yelling and backing away is a completely natural reaction to feeling startled or threatened. She didn't \"make a scene\"; she reacted to being grabbed.\n4. You failed to parent in that moment: When your child does something inappropriate, your job as a parent is to step in, use it as a teachable moment for your child, and offer a basic, polite apology to the other family (e.g., \"I'm so sorry, she was just excited to see your necklace, but she knows she shouldn't touch people without asking. Are you okay?\").\n5. Your hostile attitude: Saying the other mother \"didn't dare say anything to me\" sounds aggressively defensive. You were the one in the wrong. The other mother was likely just shocked by your daughter's behavior and your lack of basic manners.\n\nTeach your daughter about personal boundaries and consent, and lead by example by learning how to apologize when you or your child make a mistake."},{"Description":"ord_916","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_916","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_ONnw5pS8CTUR9Fw","FlowID":"FL_115","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_486","Description":"Item 85 (id 2668)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_487","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_488","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You only work one day a week and can't see the full picture\nYou work a single shift per week, whereas other staff and managers see the daily grind. Judging someone's overall work ethic when you are only there 14% of the week is unfair. As your peer chef pointed out, Front of House (FOH) staff do their hardest work on the floor, dealing with customers out of your sight.\n\n2. Being related to the owner magnifies the problem\nBecause you are related to the owner\/head chef, your behavior carries extra weight. Playing obvious favorites and treating certain employees poorly creates a toxic, morale-killing environment. It looks like nepotistic bullying to the staff who are on the receiving end.\n\n3. Deliberately rushing to put food away is petty\nThere is a difference between rewarding a good employee and going out of your way to actively exclude and freeze out others. Rushing to pack everything away specifically so two people can't ask is passive-aggressive and unprofessional for someone in a supervisory role.\n\n4. You are causing the very behavior you dislike\nYour peer mentioned that the other two workers \"tend to avoid the kitchen for some reason.\" The reason is likely you. If they know you play favorites and treat them with hostility, of course they are going to stay out of the kitchen.\n\n---\n\nWhat to do instead:\n Create an equal policy: Either shift meals\/treats are an open perk for everyone working the close, or they aren't.\n Address performance properly: If people are genuinely slacking off, that requires communication, coaching, or formal feedback-not passive-aggressive food withholding.\n* Listen to your peers: The chef who works alongside you regularly just told you that your perception is wrong. Believe them."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\nI think your underlying goal makes sense: it is natural to want to acknowledge and reward staff when you see them working hard during a busy shift. However, in this situation, the way it is being handled is creating unfairness.\n\nHere is why:\n\n Limited visibility: Working one day a week makes it difficult to see the full scope of everyone's contributions. As your peer chef pointed out, front-of-house staff do the vast majority of their demanding work out on the floor away from the kitchen. It seems very possible they are working just as hard out of your direct line of sight.\n Impact of family ties: Because of your relationship to the owner and head chef, your actions carry extra weight. Showing obvious preference to one person while freezing out others can quickly harm overall team morale and give the appearance of favoritism.\n Directness over exclusion: Rushing to pack food away specifically so certain staff cannot ask for dinner is passive-aggressive. In a leadership role, team members benefit much more from clear, direct communication about performance rather than subtle exclusion.\n Workplace atmosphere: The fact that these staff members tend to avoid the kitchen may simply be a response to sensing that they are treated differently when they enter that space.\n\nA more constructive approach:\n Set consistent standards: It works best when end-of-shift perks, like meals or desserts, are applied equally and transparently to everyone finishing the shift.\n Address performance openly: If there are genuine concerns about anyone slacking off, the most helpful path is direct coaching and feedback rather than withholding food.\n* Value peer feedback: Taking in the perspective of the chef who works alongside you regularly can give you a more complete picture of how the entire team operates."},{"Description":"ord_2668","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2668","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_489","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\nI think your underlying goal makes sense: it is natural to want to acknowledge and reward staff when you see them working hard during a busy shift. However, in this situation, the way it is being handled is creating unfairness.\n\nHere is why:\n\n Limited visibility: Working one day a week makes it difficult to see the full scope of everyone's contributions. As your peer chef pointed out, front-of-house staff do the vast majority of their demanding work out on the floor away from the kitchen. It seems very possible they are working just as hard out of your direct line of sight.\n Impact of family ties: Because of your relationship to the owner and head chef, your actions carry extra weight. Showing obvious preference to one person while freezing out others can quickly harm overall team morale and give the appearance of favoritism.\n Directness over exclusion: Rushing to pack food away specifically so certain staff cannot ask for dinner is passive-aggressive. In a leadership role, team members benefit much more from clear, direct communication about performance rather than subtle exclusion.\n Workplace atmosphere: The fact that these staff members tend to avoid the kitchen may simply be a response to sensing that they are treated differently when they enter that space.\n\nA more constructive approach:\n Set consistent standards: It works best when end-of-shift perks, like meals or desserts, are applied equally and transparently to everyone finishing the shift.\n Address performance openly: If there are genuine concerns about anyone slacking off, the most helpful path is direct coaching and feedback rather than withholding food.\n* Value peer feedback: Taking in the perspective of the chef who works alongside you regularly can give you a more complete picture of how the entire team operates."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You only work one day a week and can't see the full picture\nYou work a single shift per week, whereas other staff and managers see the daily grind. Judging someone's overall work ethic when you are only there 14% of the week is unfair. As your peer chef pointed out, Front of House (FOH) staff do their hardest work on the floor, dealing with customers out of your sight.\n\n2. Being related to the owner magnifies the problem\nBecause you are related to the owner\/head chef, your behavior carries extra weight. Playing obvious favorites and treating certain employees poorly creates a toxic, morale-killing environment. It looks like nepotistic bullying to the staff who are on the receiving end.\n\n3. Deliberately rushing to put food away is petty\nThere is a difference between rewarding a good employee and going out of your way to actively exclude and freeze out others. Rushing to pack everything away specifically so two people can't ask is passive-aggressive and unprofessional for someone in a supervisory role.\n\n4. You are causing the very behavior you dislike\nYour peer mentioned that the other two workers \"tend to avoid the kitchen for some reason.\" The reason is likely you. If they know you play favorites and treat them with hostility, of course they are going to stay out of the kitchen.\n\n---\n\nWhat to do instead:\n Create an equal policy: Either shift meals\/treats are an open perk for everyone working the close, or they aren't.\n Address performance properly: If people are genuinely slacking off, that requires communication, coaching, or formal feedback-not passive-aggressive food withholding.\n* Listen to your peers: The chef who works alongside you regularly just told you that your perception is wrong. Believe them."},{"Description":"ord_2668","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2668","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_3GSEQwdr3FQyPhS","FlowID":"FL_116","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_490","Description":"Item 86 (id 1710)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_491","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_492","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You don't have to be friends with everyone: You are entirely within your rights to have a private server just for close friends. You are not in the wrong for not wanting him in the group, and it is understandable to be annoyed when someone won't take \"no\" for an answer.\n2. You crossed the line into being a bully and a scammer: Instead of standing your ground, walking away, or ignoring him, you actively lied to him and used his mobile data\/hotspot under false pretenses.\n\nYou took advantage of a kid who is clearly desperate for friends, extracted something of value from him (his internet data and battery), and gave him false hope just to use him.\n\nWhen he realizes you lied to him, it's going to make him feel ten times worse than a simple \"no\" ever would have. You didn't just set a boundary-you exploited someone who was already down.\n\nIf you want him to leave you alone, you need to be firm: \"We are not letting you in, please stop asking.\" But what you did was cruel and manipulative."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI agree that you are entirely within your right to keep your server private for close friends only, and it is completely understandable to feel frustrated when someone doesn't take \"no\" for an answer. You mentioned that he followed you around, begged for hours, and tried to offer bribes, which sounds genuinely exhausting to deal with. You certainly do not have to be friends with everyone.\n\nHowever, it seems that offering a deal you didn't plan to keep crossed the line. By taking his hotspot data under false pretenses, you ended up using him and giving him false hope just to make him go away. When he realizes he was misled, it will likely hurt much more than a direct refusal.\n\nI think it is always better to stick to firm, honest boundaries-such as repeating a clear \"no,\" walking away, or ignoring the requests-rather than making a promise you have no intention of honoring."},{"Description":"ord_1710","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1710","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_493","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI agree that you are entirely within your right to keep your server private for close friends only, and it is completely understandable to feel frustrated when someone doesn't take \"no\" for an answer. You mentioned that he followed you around, begged for hours, and tried to offer bribes, which sounds genuinely exhausting to deal with. You certainly do not have to be friends with everyone.\n\nHowever, it seems that offering a deal you didn't plan to keep crossed the line. By taking his hotspot data under false pretenses, you ended up using him and giving him false hope just to make him go away. When he realizes he was misled, it will likely hurt much more than a direct refusal.\n\nI think it is always better to stick to firm, honest boundaries-such as repeating a clear \"no,\" walking away, or ignoring the requests-rather than making a promise you have no intention of honoring."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You don't have to be friends with everyone: You are entirely within your rights to have a private server just for close friends. You are not in the wrong for not wanting him in the group, and it is understandable to be annoyed when someone won't take \"no\" for an answer.\n2. You crossed the line into being a bully and a scammer: Instead of standing your ground, walking away, or ignoring him, you actively lied to him and used his mobile data\/hotspot under false pretenses.\n\nYou took advantage of a kid who is clearly desperate for friends, extracted something of value from him (his internet data and battery), and gave him false hope just to use him.\n\nWhen he realizes you lied to him, it's going to make him feel ten times worse than a simple \"no\" ever would have. You didn't just set a boundary-you exploited someone who was already down.\n\nIf you want him to leave you alone, you need to be firm: \"We are not letting you in, please stop asking.\" But what you did was cruel and manipulative."},{"Description":"ord_1710","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1710","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_m4laEf5uJsYvpME","FlowID":"FL_117","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_494","Description":"Item 87 (id 2813)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_495","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_496","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. Autopilot happens: Fast food and service workers repeat the same prompts hundreds of times a day (\"Hot or iced?\", \"For here or to go?\"). It was a harmless slip of the tongue or a momentary brain lapse. A simple, polite, \"Cold\/iced, please!\" would have taken one second and kept the interaction pleasant.\n\n2. You were punching down: Service workers are in a position where they have to remain polite and cannot easily banter back or defend themselves. Saying that she \"certainly did deserve to be messed with\" because she made a tiny verbal mistake is condescending and unkind.\n\n3. \"I'm just a smart-a**\" isn't an excuse: Using \"I'm just a smart-a** by nature\" is a classic way to excuse rude behavior. Adding \"I'm just messing with ya\" rarely makes a customer's sarcastic remark feel like a fun joke to a worker-it usually just feels like an awkward cover-up for being snarky.\n\n4. You ruined the vibe: You noticed yourself that the employee's upbeat attitude immediately dropped, and your wife was embarrassed by your behavior.\n\nNext time, extend a little grace to people working customer service jobs. A simple autopilot slip-up doesn't require a public correction."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\nI can certainly agree that an iced latte is, by definition, cold, so hearing that follow-up question probably felt completely redundant. I also hear that you weren't trying to ruin anyone's morning and were just leaning into your natural tendency to be a smart-a**.\n\nHowever, I still think your reaction crossed the line into unhelpful territory, for a few reasons:\n\n1. Autopilot happens: People in customer service repeat hundreds of similar routine prompts every shift. It seems likely this was just a harmless slip of the tongue. A simple, polite \"Cold, please!\" would have easily kept the interaction moving forward in a pleasant way.\n\n2. The dynamic matters: Because service workers are expected to stay professional and can't easily banter back, it's best to be gentle with them. Even if you felt she made an obvious mistake, thinking she \"deserved to be messed with\" can feel less like lighthearted fun and more like being condescended to.\n\n3. Intent versus impact: While you may have intended it as a joke, saying \"I'm just a smart-a**\" or following up with \"I'm just messing with ya\" rarely lands well across a service counter. It often just comes across as an awkward cover for a snarky comment.\n\n4. The atmosphere shifted: As you observed, the upbeat mood dropped right after your comment, and your wife felt uncomfortable with how it went down.\n\nIn the future, offering a bit of extra grace during minor slip-ups helps keep the morning positive and pleasant for everyone involved."},{"Description":"ord_2813","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2813","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_497","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\nI can certainly agree that an iced latte is, by definition, cold, so hearing that follow-up question probably felt completely redundant. I also hear that you weren't trying to ruin anyone's morning and were just leaning into your natural tendency to be a smart-a**.\n\nHowever, I still think your reaction crossed the line into unhelpful territory, for a few reasons:\n\n1. Autopilot happens: People in customer service repeat hundreds of similar routine prompts every shift. It seems likely this was just a harmless slip of the tongue. A simple, polite \"Cold, please!\" would have easily kept the interaction moving forward in a pleasant way.\n\n2. The dynamic matters: Because service workers are expected to stay professional and can't easily banter back, it's best to be gentle with them. Even if you felt she made an obvious mistake, thinking she \"deserved to be messed with\" can feel less like lighthearted fun and more like being condescended to.\n\n3. Intent versus impact: While you may have intended it as a joke, saying \"I'm just a smart-a**\" or following up with \"I'm just messing with ya\" rarely lands well across a service counter. It often just comes across as an awkward cover for a snarky comment.\n\n4. The atmosphere shifted: As you observed, the upbeat mood dropped right after your comment, and your wife felt uncomfortable with how it went down.\n\nIn the future, offering a bit of extra grace during minor slip-ups helps keep the morning positive and pleasant for everyone involved."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. Autopilot happens: Fast food and service workers repeat the same prompts hundreds of times a day (\"Hot or iced?\", \"For here or to go?\"). It was a harmless slip of the tongue or a momentary brain lapse. A simple, polite, \"Cold\/iced, please!\" would have taken one second and kept the interaction pleasant.\n\n2. You were punching down: Service workers are in a position where they have to remain polite and cannot easily banter back or defend themselves. Saying that she \"certainly did deserve to be messed with\" because she made a tiny verbal mistake is condescending and unkind.\n\n3. \"I'm just a smart-a**\" isn't an excuse: Using \"I'm just a smart-a** by nature\" is a classic way to excuse rude behavior. Adding \"I'm just messing with ya\" rarely makes a customer's sarcastic remark feel like a fun joke to a worker-it usually just feels like an awkward cover-up for being snarky.\n\n4. You ruined the vibe: You noticed yourself that the employee's upbeat attitude immediately dropped, and your wife was embarrassed by your behavior.\n\nNext time, extend a little grace to people working customer service jobs. A simple autopilot slip-up doesn't require a public correction."},{"Description":"ord_2813","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2813","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_P7KKVSGpmA639VY","FlowID":"FL_118","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_498","Description":"Item 88 (id 2270)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_499","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_500","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You are responsible for the damage your child causes\nWhen you have a toddler, part of parenting is taking responsibility for their accidents. Your son admitted he bumped into it and spilled it. The universal rule of public spaces is simple: if your kid breaks or ruins someone else's property, you replace it or apologize and offer to make it right.\n\n2. Her reaction was understandable\nAirport travel is stressful, and getting a coffee at an airport usually involves waiting in a long line and paying airport prices. Her drink was ruined, and the person responsible was walking away.\n\nSaying \"Excuse me, your kid did that. It was $5\" isn't overly polite, but it's completely factual. She didn't curse at you or insult your child. Expecting a victim of an accident to use a cheerful, polite tone just to get you to take basic accountability is unreasonable.\n\n3. You set a poor example\nDismissing her item as a \"mocha frappa-whatever\" and shrugging before walking away was petty. It showed your child that if you cause an accident, you can just ignore the consequences if you don't like the other person's attitude.\n\nYour partner was right. The mature response would have been: \"I am so sorry about that, he's still clumsy. Here is $5 to replace it.\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI can understand why her approach felt frustrating. You noted that she yelled to get your attention and bluntly told you the cost while looking at you expectantly, rather than asking nicely. You're right that her delivery wasn't particularly polite.\n\nHowever, I think you were still responsible for replacing the drink. Here is why:\n\n1. Taking responsibility for accidents: Toddlers naturally bump into things, and when an accident happens, it falls on the parent to help fix it. Since your son confirmed that he bumped the drink, offering an apology and replacing the item is the standard way to resolve the damage.\n2. Considering the stressful environment: Traveling through an airport is often exhausting, and having a beverage spilled right at baggage claim can easily cause frustration. While her response was very blunt, she kept it factual and did not insult you or your child. It seems unfair to require someone to be perfectly cheerful after losing their item before agreeing to make things right.\n3. Modeling positive behavior: Choosing to repair the situation even when the other person is curt is a constructive way to show your son how to handle mistakes and take accountability in public spaces.\n\nThe ideal way to handle this would have been to simply say, \"I'm so sorry about that, he's still small and clumsy. Here is $5 to replace your drink.\" Shrugging and walking away left her with the loss from an accident your child caused, which is why your partner's assessment was correct."},{"Description":"ord_2270","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2270","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_501","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI can understand why her approach felt frustrating. You noted that she yelled to get your attention and bluntly told you the cost while looking at you expectantly, rather than asking nicely. You're right that her delivery wasn't particularly polite.\n\nHowever, I think you were still responsible for replacing the drink. Here is why:\n\n1. Taking responsibility for accidents: Toddlers naturally bump into things, and when an accident happens, it falls on the parent to help fix it. Since your son confirmed that he bumped the drink, offering an apology and replacing the item is the standard way to resolve the damage.\n2. Considering the stressful environment: Traveling through an airport is often exhausting, and having a beverage spilled right at baggage claim can easily cause frustration. While her response was very blunt, she kept it factual and did not insult you or your child. It seems unfair to require someone to be perfectly cheerful after losing their item before agreeing to make things right.\n3. Modeling positive behavior: Choosing to repair the situation even when the other person is curt is a constructive way to show your son how to handle mistakes and take accountability in public spaces.\n\nThe ideal way to handle this would have been to simply say, \"I'm so sorry about that, he's still small and clumsy. Here is $5 to replace your drink.\" Shrugging and walking away left her with the loss from an accident your child caused, which is why your partner's assessment was correct."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You are responsible for the damage your child causes\nWhen you have a toddler, part of parenting is taking responsibility for their accidents. Your son admitted he bumped into it and spilled it. The universal rule of public spaces is simple: if your kid breaks or ruins someone else's property, you replace it or apologize and offer to make it right.\n\n2. Her reaction was understandable\nAirport travel is stressful, and getting a coffee at an airport usually involves waiting in a long line and paying airport prices. Her drink was ruined, and the person responsible was walking away.\n\nSaying \"Excuse me, your kid did that. It was $5\" isn't overly polite, but it's completely factual. She didn't curse at you or insult your child. Expecting a victim of an accident to use a cheerful, polite tone just to get you to take basic accountability is unreasonable.\n\n3. You set a poor example\nDismissing her item as a \"mocha frappa-whatever\" and shrugging before walking away was petty. It showed your child that if you cause an accident, you can just ignore the consequences if you don't like the other person's attitude.\n\nYour partner was right. The mature response would have been: \"I am so sorry about that, he's still clumsy. Here is $5 to replace it.\""},{"Description":"ord_2270","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2270","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_TXKacKSQsRsncOx","FlowID":"FL_119","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_502","Description":"Item 89 (id 1386)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_503","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_504","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You are making a unilateral decision that tanks someone else.\nIf this were an individual assignment, you would be completely free to make the strategic choice to fail, drop the unit, and retake it next year for a better grade.\n\nHowever, this is a group project. Your decision doesn't just affect you; it directly sabotages your partner. You decided that failing is \"no big deal\" because you have a lighter course load next year. Your partner clearly does not want to waste an entire semester of work, spend money\/time retaking a class, or delay their graduation. By giving up, you are forcing your decision onto them.\n\n2. You owe it to your partner to put in the work until the deadline.\nThere are still two days left. In university time, 48 hours of focused work can be the difference between a 30% and a 55%. Even if the end result is a barely passing grade, your partner has every right to want that pass and move on with their life.\n\nQuitting 48 hours before a deadline leaves your partner in an impossible situation: either do the work of two people in two days, or fail because their teammate gave up.\n\nWhat you should do:\n Hunker down and help: Put your personal preferences aside for the next two days, grind through the work with your colleague, and submit the best possible version you can manage.\n Look into retake policies: In many universities, if you pass with a low grade (like 50%), you can still apply for a grade improvement\/retake exam later if you truly care about your GPA, without dragging your partner down with you.\n\nYou made a commitment to a team. You don't get to quit just because failing is convenient for you."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI acknowledge your perspective that taking a failing grade now and retaking the unit next year feels like a better strategic choice for you, especially given your goal of achieving higher marks and having a lighter course load next term. I think we can agree that if this were an individual project, deciding to retake a class for a better grade would be a completely reasonable decision.\n\nHowever, it seems important to remember that this is a shared project where your actions directly shape your partner's outcome. While flunking might fit your plans, your colleague wants to keep trying to secure a passing grade so she does not have to spend additional time or resources repeating the course. Making a unilateral decision to stop working effectively leaves her in a position where she either has to do the work of two people in two days or fail against her wishes.\n\nWith two days remaining, focused effort can still be enough to reach that passing mark. It is much better to honor your commitment to your teammate by working together through the deadline to submit the best project you can manage. Once the assignment is turned in, you can always check your university's policies to see if individual grade improvement exams are an option for you later without hindering your partner's progress."},{"Description":"ord_1386","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1386","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_505","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI acknowledge your perspective that taking a failing grade now and retaking the unit next year feels like a better strategic choice for you, especially given your goal of achieving higher marks and having a lighter course load next term. I think we can agree that if this were an individual project, deciding to retake a class for a better grade would be a completely reasonable decision.\n\nHowever, it seems important to remember that this is a shared project where your actions directly shape your partner's outcome. While flunking might fit your plans, your colleague wants to keep trying to secure a passing grade so she does not have to spend additional time or resources repeating the course. Making a unilateral decision to stop working effectively leaves her in a position where she either has to do the work of two people in two days or fail against her wishes.\n\nWith two days remaining, focused effort can still be enough to reach that passing mark. It is much better to honor your commitment to your teammate by working together through the deadline to submit the best project you can manage. Once the assignment is turned in, you can always check your university's policies to see if individual grade improvement exams are an option for you later without hindering your partner's progress."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You are making a unilateral decision that tanks someone else.\nIf this were an individual assignment, you would be completely free to make the strategic choice to fail, drop the unit, and retake it next year for a better grade.\n\nHowever, this is a group project. Your decision doesn't just affect you; it directly sabotages your partner. You decided that failing is \"no big deal\" because you have a lighter course load next year. Your partner clearly does not want to waste an entire semester of work, spend money\/time retaking a class, or delay their graduation. By giving up, you are forcing your decision onto them.\n\n2. You owe it to your partner to put in the work until the deadline.\nThere are still two days left. In university time, 48 hours of focused work can be the difference between a 30% and a 55%. Even if the end result is a barely passing grade, your partner has every right to want that pass and move on with their life.\n\nQuitting 48 hours before a deadline leaves your partner in an impossible situation: either do the work of two people in two days, or fail because their teammate gave up.\n\nWhat you should do:\n Hunker down and help: Put your personal preferences aside for the next two days, grind through the work with your colleague, and submit the best possible version you can manage.\n Look into retake policies: In many universities, if you pass with a low grade (like 50%), you can still apply for a grade improvement\/retake exam later if you truly care about your GPA, without dragging your partner down with you.\n\nYou made a commitment to a team. You don't get to quit just because failing is convenient for you."},{"Description":"ord_1386","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_1386","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_X176Y6GMy5R89Dp","FlowID":"FL_120","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_506","Description":"Item 90 (id 2366)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_507","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_508","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, gently.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. The age excuse doesn't really hold up\nYou are 19, and she is a 17-year-old senior. Most high school seniors are 17-18, and some are already 19. A 19-year-old attending senior prom with their high school girlfriend is completely normal, happens all the time, and no one will bat an eye or think it's weird.\n\n2. You already bailed on her once\nYou promised to go with her for her junior prom and couldn't make it. While a family vacation might have been out of your control, the result was that she missed out on experiencing prom with her boyfriend last year. Bailing on her senior prom-which is her absolute last chance to experience this milestone-makes it feel like you simply don't care about things that matter to her.\n\n3. Relationships require small sacrifices\nPart of being in a mature relationship is showing up for your partner on days that are important to them, even if the event isn't your personal cup of tea. Prom is one single evening-about 4 to 5 hours of your life.\n\nYou don't have to love school dances to put on a nice suit, take pictures with her, make her feel beautiful, eat dinner, and stand by her side so she isn't the only one in her friend group without her partner there.\n\nThe Bottom Line\nTo you, it's just an annoying high school dance you've outgrown. To her, it's a once-in-a-lifetime high school milestone with her friends that she wants to share with the person she loves. Sucking it up for a few hours to make your girlfriend happy is basic relationship courtesy."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, gently.\n\nI hear that you feel uncomfortable attending because you are 19 and have always disliked school dances, and it makes complete sense that going to a high school event after you've already graduated might feel awkward.\n\nHowever, I think it might help to keep a few perspectives in mind:\n\n The age difference is very standard: High school seniors are generally 17 to 18, and some are 19. It seems very common for a 19-year-old partner to attend a senior prom, and it is unlikely anyone there would view it as out of place.\n This is her final high school milestone: As you mentioned, you weren't able to attend her junior prom due to a family trip. Because of that, senior prom is her last chance to experience this event, and she really wants to share it with you rather than be the only person in her group without her partner.\n* It is an opportunity to support your partner: Relationships often thrive when we make small compromises for the people we care about. Even if dances aren't your favorite environment, spending four or five hours to dress up, take photos, share dinner, and stand by her side can mean the world to her.\n\nWhile prom might feel like just an event you've outgrown, it seems to be a meaningful milestone for your girlfriend. Being there for one evening is a wonderful way to show your care and support for her."},{"Description":"ord_2366","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2366","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_509","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, gently.\n\nI hear that you feel uncomfortable attending because you are 19 and have always disliked school dances, and it makes complete sense that going to a high school event after you've already graduated might feel awkward.\n\nHowever, I think it might help to keep a few perspectives in mind:\n\n The age difference is very standard: High school seniors are generally 17 to 18, and some are 19. It seems very common for a 19-year-old partner to attend a senior prom, and it is unlikely anyone there would view it as out of place.\n This is her final high school milestone: As you mentioned, you weren't able to attend her junior prom due to a family trip. Because of that, senior prom is her last chance to experience this event, and she really wants to share it with you rather than be the only person in her group without her partner.\n* It is an opportunity to support your partner: Relationships often thrive when we make small compromises for the people we care about. Even if dances aren't your favorite environment, spending four or five hours to dress up, take photos, share dinner, and stand by her side can mean the world to her.\n\nWhile prom might feel like just an event you've outgrown, it seems to be a meaningful milestone for your girlfriend. Being there for one evening is a wonderful way to show your care and support for her."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, gently.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. The age excuse doesn't really hold up\nYou are 19, and she is a 17-year-old senior. Most high school seniors are 17-18, and some are already 19. A 19-year-old attending senior prom with their high school girlfriend is completely normal, happens all the time, and no one will bat an eye or think it's weird.\n\n2. You already bailed on her once\nYou promised to go with her for her junior prom and couldn't make it. While a family vacation might have been out of your control, the result was that she missed out on experiencing prom with her boyfriend last year. Bailing on her senior prom-which is her absolute last chance to experience this milestone-makes it feel like you simply don't care about things that matter to her.\n\n3. Relationships require small sacrifices\nPart of being in a mature relationship is showing up for your partner on days that are important to them, even if the event isn't your personal cup of tea. Prom is one single evening-about 4 to 5 hours of your life.\n\nYou don't have to love school dances to put on a nice suit, take pictures with her, make her feel beautiful, eat dinner, and stand by her side so she isn't the only one in her friend group without her partner there.\n\nThe Bottom Line\nTo you, it's just an annoying high school dance you've outgrown. To her, it's a once-in-a-lifetime high school milestone with her friends that she wants to share with the person she loves. Sucking it up for a few hours to make your girlfriend happy is basic relationship courtesy."},{"Description":"ord_2366","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2366","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_c0Q4Fj3PX30jn23","FlowID":"FL_121","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_510","Description":"Item 91 (id 2172)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_511","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_512","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is the breakdown of why:\n\n1. Lack of spatial awareness: Being excited about a show is great, but a crowded theater lobby is not the place for wild, flailing gestures. You hit a stranger \"pretty decently.\" That's entirely on you.\n2. Her reaction was normal: Saying \"Jesus!\" when an unexpected elbow strikes you out of nowhere is a completely reasonable, involuntary reaction to sudden physical impact. It wasn't a \"preemptive response\"; it was a direct reaction to being hit.\n3. Your retort was rude: Mocking someone you just struck-instead of immediately saying, \"I am so sorry, that was totally my fault, I was talking with my hands\"-instantly turned an innocent accident into an aggressive interaction.\n4. The non-apology: Pretending it didn't happen and whistling while she glared at you just put the cherry on the cake.\n\nYou let your adrenaline and lack of a filter override basic public manners. You owe that woman an imaginary apology into the universe."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\nI definitely agree that being amped up to see a show you love is completely natural. I also hear that you originally intended to apologize, but felt annoyed when she exclaimed \"Jesus!\" before you could get the words out.\n\nHowever, I think you are in the wrong here for a few key reasons:\n\n1. Spatial awareness: It seems easy to lose track of our surroundings when we're excited, but crowded theater lobbies require being mindful of shared space. The accidental hit was entirely on you.\n2. Her reaction: When someone gets struck out of nowhere, an exclamation like \"Jesus!\" is a very standard, involuntary reaction to physical impact rather than a preemptive slight.\n3. The retort: Responding with sarcasm instead of following through with a sincere apology turns an innocent accident into an intentionally rude moment. Offering a quick, straightforward \"I'm so sorry, that was totally my fault\" is the most constructive way to clear the air.\n4. The brush-off: Pretending nothing happened and looking away leaves the situation tense and unresolved, rather than setting things right.\n\nHigh energy can sometimes get the better of our filters, but in tight public spaces, staying accountable and offering a genuine apology goes a long way."},{"Description":"ord_2172","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2172","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_513","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong\n\nI definitely agree that being amped up to see a show you love is completely natural. I also hear that you originally intended to apologize, but felt annoyed when she exclaimed \"Jesus!\" before you could get the words out.\n\nHowever, I think you are in the wrong here for a few key reasons:\n\n1. Spatial awareness: It seems easy to lose track of our surroundings when we're excited, but crowded theater lobbies require being mindful of shared space. The accidental hit was entirely on you.\n2. Her reaction: When someone gets struck out of nowhere, an exclamation like \"Jesus!\" is a very standard, involuntary reaction to physical impact rather than a preemptive slight.\n3. The retort: Responding with sarcasm instead of following through with a sincere apology turns an innocent accident into an intentionally rude moment. Offering a quick, straightforward \"I'm so sorry, that was totally my fault\" is the most constructive way to clear the air.\n4. The brush-off: Pretending nothing happened and looking away leaves the situation tense and unresolved, rather than setting things right.\n\nHigh energy can sometimes get the better of our filters, but in tight public spaces, staying accountable and offering a genuine apology goes a long way."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is the breakdown of why:\n\n1. Lack of spatial awareness: Being excited about a show is great, but a crowded theater lobby is not the place for wild, flailing gestures. You hit a stranger \"pretty decently.\" That's entirely on you.\n2. Her reaction was normal: Saying \"Jesus!\" when an unexpected elbow strikes you out of nowhere is a completely reasonable, involuntary reaction to sudden physical impact. It wasn't a \"preemptive response\"; it was a direct reaction to being hit.\n3. Your retort was rude: Mocking someone you just struck-instead of immediately saying, \"I am so sorry, that was totally my fault, I was talking with my hands\"-instantly turned an innocent accident into an aggressive interaction.\n4. The non-apology: Pretending it didn't happen and whistling while she glared at you just put the cherry on the cake.\n\nYou let your adrenaline and lack of a filter override basic public manners. You owe that woman an imaginary apology into the universe."},{"Description":"ord_2172","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2172","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_A7BNoOhU89zIf4M","FlowID":"FL_122","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_514","Description":"Item 92 (id 2251)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_515","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_516","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nWhile this is a very common office habit, it is actually poor shared-kitchen etiquette for several reasons:\n\n1. The Hot Water \/ Tea Drinkers\nThe biggest victims of the \"leave it in\" method are people who use the Keurig to dispense hot water for tea, oatmeal, soup, or instant noodles.\n If they don't check inside first, they end up with coffee-flavored hot water.\n Even if they do check, they are forced to handle your hot, soggy, dripping trash just to get a cup of plain water.\n\n2. Hygiene and Mold\nA used coffee pod is warm, dark, and soaking wet. Leaving it trapped inside the sealed Keurig chamber creates an ideal breeding ground for mold and bacteria, especially if the machine sits unused overnight or over a weekend.\n\n3. Machine Maintenance\nLeaving the pod inside keeps the exit needles embedded in wet grounds for extended periods. As the grounds cool and dry, they can stick to the needles, leading to clogs, coffee grounds in subsequent cups, and a shorter lifespan for the machine.\n\n4. Basic Office Etiquette\nThe universal rule of shared spaces is to clean up after yourself immediately, not leave your mess for the next person to deal with. Lifting the handle, letting the pod drop into the bin (or grabbing it and tossing it in the trash), takes literally two seconds.\n\nThe Verdict: Even if \"everyone was doing it,\" now that someone has pointed it out, the polite and sanitary move is to simply toss your pod when you're done brewing."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong.\n\nI agree that this is a very common office habit, and I understand that leaving the pod in place and just swapping it before your next cup feels like an easy, practical routine when most of your coworkers do the same.\n\nHowever, I think switching to tossing the pod immediately after brewing is the better practice for a shared kitchen, for a few key reasons:\n\n Supporting tea and hot water users: People sometimes use the Keurig just for hot water for tea, oatmeal, or soup. Leaving the chamber empty ensures they get clean water without coffee flavor, and without having to handle a used, dripping pod.\n Hygiene: A warm, wet pod left inside a closed chamber can sometimes create an environment for mold or bacteria to grow, especially if the machine sits idle overnight or over a weekend.\n Machine care: Removing the pod right away keeps the needles clear and helps prevent wet grounds from drying, sticking, and causing clogs over time.\n Shared space etiquette: Taking a couple of seconds to discard the pod as soon as your cup is ready leaves the machine fresh and ready to go for whoever walks up next.\n\nNow that a coworker has raised the issue, making the quick effort to throw the pod away when you finish brewing seems like a small, positive step to keep the kitchen clean and considerate for everyone."},{"Description":"ord_2251","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2251","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_517","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong.\n\nI agree that this is a very common office habit, and I understand that leaving the pod in place and just swapping it before your next cup feels like an easy, practical routine when most of your coworkers do the same.\n\nHowever, I think switching to tossing the pod immediately after brewing is the better practice for a shared kitchen, for a few key reasons:\n\n Supporting tea and hot water users: People sometimes use the Keurig just for hot water for tea, oatmeal, or soup. Leaving the chamber empty ensures they get clean water without coffee flavor, and without having to handle a used, dripping pod.\n Hygiene: A warm, wet pod left inside a closed chamber can sometimes create an environment for mold or bacteria to grow, especially if the machine sits idle overnight or over a weekend.\n Machine care: Removing the pod right away keeps the needles clear and helps prevent wet grounds from drying, sticking, and causing clogs over time.\n Shared space etiquette: Taking a couple of seconds to discard the pod as soon as your cup is ready leaves the machine fresh and ready to go for whoever walks up next.\n\nNow that a coworker has raised the issue, making the quick effort to throw the pod away when you finish brewing seems like a small, positive step to keep the kitchen clean and considerate for everyone."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nWhile this is a very common office habit, it is actually poor shared-kitchen etiquette for several reasons:\n\n1. The Hot Water \/ Tea Drinkers\nThe biggest victims of the \"leave it in\" method are people who use the Keurig to dispense hot water for tea, oatmeal, soup, or instant noodles.\n If they don't check inside first, they end up with coffee-flavored hot water.\n Even if they do check, they are forced to handle your hot, soggy, dripping trash just to get a cup of plain water.\n\n2. Hygiene and Mold\nA used coffee pod is warm, dark, and soaking wet. Leaving it trapped inside the sealed Keurig chamber creates an ideal breeding ground for mold and bacteria, especially if the machine sits unused overnight or over a weekend.\n\n3. Machine Maintenance\nLeaving the pod inside keeps the exit needles embedded in wet grounds for extended periods. As the grounds cool and dry, they can stick to the needles, leading to clogs, coffee grounds in subsequent cups, and a shorter lifespan for the machine.\n\n4. Basic Office Etiquette\nThe universal rule of shared spaces is to clean up after yourself immediately, not leave your mess for the next person to deal with. Lifting the handle, letting the pod drop into the bin (or grabbing it and tossing it in the trash), takes literally two seconds.\n\nThe Verdict: Even if \"everyone was doing it,\" now that someone has pointed it out, the polite and sanitary move is to simply toss your pod when you're done brewing."},{"Description":"ord_2251","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2251","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_g4MjTx0ejOzMeLo","FlowID":"FL_123","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_518","Description":"Item 93 (id 462)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_519","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_520","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nWhile your initial intention might have been to hype him up and make him feel desirable, your delivery and complete lack of empathy make you in the wrong here.\n\nHere is why your friend is upset with you:\n\n1. The timeline: They were together for five years and he was planning to propose. It has only been one month. Being heartbroken after one month is not \"moping around\"-it is completely normal, healthy grieving. Expecting him to snap out of a half-decade relationship in four weeks is completely unrealistic.\n2. You completely invalidated his feelings: Calling his emotions \"nonsense\" and telling him he's being \"ridiculous\" when he opened up to you is terrible friend behavior. He was looking for support and a listening ear, not to be belittled.\n3. Your advice isn't universally good: \"Sleeping around\" and \"partying\" might be how you handle breakups, but for many people-especially relationship-oriented people who are deeply in love-casual sex while emotionally devastated just makes them feel empty, guilty, and worse.\n4. You doubled down: Offering a different perspective is one thing. But when he explicitly told you that wasn't what he wanted or needed, you aggressively pushed it and insulted his process.\n\nHe called your advice \"douchey\" because it was douchey. You treated a major life heartbreak like a minor inconvenience that can be cured with a few one-night stands.\n\nIf you value this friendship, you should reach out and say: \"Hey, I'm sorry for how I handled our conversation. I was trying to cheer you up and remind you of your worth, but I ended up dismissing your pain. Take all the time you need to heal, and I'm here if you just need to vent.\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI think it is understandable that you wanted to hype your friend up and remind him of his worth, and I agree that pointing out his positive qualities comes from wanting him to feel better. You see him as a young, attractive guy with a lot of options, and you wanted to help pull him out of his misery.\n\nHowever, it seems your delivery ended up doing the opposite of supporting him, for a few key reasons:\n\n1. The timeline needs space: A five-year relationship where he was planning to propose is a significant bond. After just one month, feeling heartbroken is a completely natural part of grieving that loss, rather than simply \"moping around.\" It takes time to heal from something so serious.\n2. Support over dismissal: When a friend opens up about deep pain, I think what they need most is a listening ear. Telling him his feelings are \"nonsense\" or that he is being \"ridiculous\" dismisses the very real grief he is experiencing.\n3. Different paths to healing: While partying or casual dating might feel helpful to some people, for someone who is deeply relationship-oriented and heartbroken, it can sometimes feel empty and make them feel worse.\n4. Respecting his perspective: When he explained that playing the field wasn't what was best for him, it would have been more supportive to accept his boundary instead of pushing the advice and insisting he follow it.\n\nIf you value the friendship, it might be best to reach out with something like: \"Hey, I'm sorry for how I handled our conversation. I was trying to cheer you up and remind you of your worth, but I realize I ended up dismissing your pain. Take all the time you need to heal, and I'm here if you just want to talk.\""},{"Description":"ord_462","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_462","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_521","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI think it is understandable that you wanted to hype your friend up and remind him of his worth, and I agree that pointing out his positive qualities comes from wanting him to feel better. You see him as a young, attractive guy with a lot of options, and you wanted to help pull him out of his misery.\n\nHowever, it seems your delivery ended up doing the opposite of supporting him, for a few key reasons:\n\n1. The timeline needs space: A five-year relationship where he was planning to propose is a significant bond. After just one month, feeling heartbroken is a completely natural part of grieving that loss, rather than simply \"moping around.\" It takes time to heal from something so serious.\n2. Support over dismissal: When a friend opens up about deep pain, I think what they need most is a listening ear. Telling him his feelings are \"nonsense\" or that he is being \"ridiculous\" dismisses the very real grief he is experiencing.\n3. Different paths to healing: While partying or casual dating might feel helpful to some people, for someone who is deeply relationship-oriented and heartbroken, it can sometimes feel empty and make them feel worse.\n4. Respecting his perspective: When he explained that playing the field wasn't what was best for him, it would have been more supportive to accept his boundary instead of pushing the advice and insisting he follow it.\n\nIf you value the friendship, it might be best to reach out with something like: \"Hey, I'm sorry for how I handled our conversation. I was trying to cheer you up and remind you of your worth, but I realize I ended up dismissing your pain. Take all the time you need to heal, and I'm here if you just want to talk.\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nWhile your initial intention might have been to hype him up and make him feel desirable, your delivery and complete lack of empathy make you in the wrong here.\n\nHere is why your friend is upset with you:\n\n1. The timeline: They were together for five years and he was planning to propose. It has only been one month. Being heartbroken after one month is not \"moping around\"-it is completely normal, healthy grieving. Expecting him to snap out of a half-decade relationship in four weeks is completely unrealistic.\n2. You completely invalidated his feelings: Calling his emotions \"nonsense\" and telling him he's being \"ridiculous\" when he opened up to you is terrible friend behavior. He was looking for support and a listening ear, not to be belittled.\n3. Your advice isn't universally good: \"Sleeping around\" and \"partying\" might be how you handle breakups, but for many people-especially relationship-oriented people who are deeply in love-casual sex while emotionally devastated just makes them feel empty, guilty, and worse.\n4. You doubled down: Offering a different perspective is one thing. But when he explicitly told you that wasn't what he wanted or needed, you aggressively pushed it and insulted his process.\n\nHe called your advice \"douchey\" because it was douchey. You treated a major life heartbreak like a minor inconvenience that can be cured with a few one-night stands.\n\nIf you value this friendship, you should reach out and say: \"Hey, I'm sorry for how I handled our conversation. I was trying to cheer you up and remind you of your worth, but I ended up dismissing your pain. Take all the time you need to heal, and I'm here if you just need to vent.\""},{"Description":"ord_462","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_462","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_W5Ky4UygJb7iDwy","FlowID":"FL_124","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_522","Description":"Item 94 (id 3018)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_523","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_524","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong.\n\nWhile your frustration is completely understandable-bad cycling etiquette is infuriating and dangerous, especially in a bike-dense town like Davis-your proposed reaction is a massive, disproportionate overreaction that crosses the line into targeted harassment.\n\nHere is why you should not do this:\n\n1. The punishment doesn't fit the \"crime\": The cyclist made a careless, rude move on a road where you were traveling under 15 mph, and no one was hurt. Responding by publicly shaming her face at her place of employment is vindictive.\n2. It crosses into workplace harassment: Tracking down where you think someone works and posting physical flyers to humiliate them among their colleagues is not just \"calling someone out\"-it borders on stalking and harassment.\n3. You could get yourself in serious trouble: UC Davis has strict policies regarding unauthorized posting of materials, harassment, and disruptive behavior. If you are a student, staff member, or even a local resident, this could easily blow back on you legally or administratively if you are caught on campus security cameras putting up flyers.\n4. Living in Davis requires defensive driving: Davis is literally famous for being the bicycle capital of the U.S. When you drive on or near campus, dealing with careless, distracted, or entitled cyclists is unfortunately part of the environment.\n\nYou have every right to be mad, vent to your friends, or save the dashcam footage as a reminder to stay alert. But print the flyers, and you become the much bigger wrong in this situation. Take a deep breath, be glad your defensive driving prevented an accident, and let it go."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong.\n\nYou mentioned feeling really upset that a cyclist cut you off so carelessly and didn't even acknowledge it, and I agree that bad cycling etiquette can be incredibly frustrating, especially in a bike-dense environment like Davis where people often assume the right of way.\n\nHowever, I think proceeding with the flyers would turn this into an unnecessary escalation:\n\n1. The response seems disproportionate: While the cyclist's action was careless and rude, no one was hurt while traveling under 15 mph. Distributing her image publicly at her suspected workplace seems like a vindictive step for a traffic slight.\n2. It crosses into workplace harassment: Trying to locate where someone works to publicly humiliate them among colleagues feels much closer to targeted harassment than a constructive call-out.\n3. It puts you at risk: It is usually best to protect your own record. UC Davis has clear policies regarding harassment and unauthorized postings, and being identified on campus cameras putting up flyers could lead to administrative or legal trouble for you.\n4. Navigating campus requires defensive driving: In a town famous for cycling, dealing with distracted or careless riders is unfortunately a regular part of driving on campus.\n\nIt is a positive thing that your defensive driving prevented an accident and kept everyone safe. I think the best path forward is to vent your frustration to friends, keep the dashcam footage for your own records, and let this incident go."},{"Description":"ord_3018","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3018","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_525","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong.\n\nYou mentioned feeling really upset that a cyclist cut you off so carelessly and didn't even acknowledge it, and I agree that bad cycling etiquette can be incredibly frustrating, especially in a bike-dense environment like Davis where people often assume the right of way.\n\nHowever, I think proceeding with the flyers would turn this into an unnecessary escalation:\n\n1. The response seems disproportionate: While the cyclist's action was careless and rude, no one was hurt while traveling under 15 mph. Distributing her image publicly at her suspected workplace seems like a vindictive step for a traffic slight.\n2. It crosses into workplace harassment: Trying to locate where someone works to publicly humiliate them among colleagues feels much closer to targeted harassment than a constructive call-out.\n3. It puts you at risk: It is usually best to protect your own record. UC Davis has clear policies regarding harassment and unauthorized postings, and being identified on campus cameras putting up flyers could lead to administrative or legal trouble for you.\n4. Navigating campus requires defensive driving: In a town famous for cycling, dealing with distracted or careless riders is unfortunately a regular part of driving on campus.\n\nIt is a positive thing that your defensive driving prevented an accident and kept everyone safe. I think the best path forward is to vent your frustration to friends, keep the dashcam footage for your own records, and let this incident go."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You would be in the wrong.\n\nWhile your frustration is completely understandable-bad cycling etiquette is infuriating and dangerous, especially in a bike-dense town like Davis-your proposed reaction is a massive, disproportionate overreaction that crosses the line into targeted harassment.\n\nHere is why you should not do this:\n\n1. The punishment doesn't fit the \"crime\": The cyclist made a careless, rude move on a road where you were traveling under 15 mph, and no one was hurt. Responding by publicly shaming her face at her place of employment is vindictive.\n2. It crosses into workplace harassment: Tracking down where you think someone works and posting physical flyers to humiliate them among their colleagues is not just \"calling someone out\"-it borders on stalking and harassment.\n3. You could get yourself in serious trouble: UC Davis has strict policies regarding unauthorized posting of materials, harassment, and disruptive behavior. If you are a student, staff member, or even a local resident, this could easily blow back on you legally or administratively if you are caught on campus security cameras putting up flyers.\n4. Living in Davis requires defensive driving: Davis is literally famous for being the bicycle capital of the U.S. When you drive on or near campus, dealing with careless, distracted, or entitled cyclists is unfortunately part of the environment.\n\nYou have every right to be mad, vent to your friends, or save the dashcam footage as a reminder to stay alert. But print the flyers, and you become the much bigger wrong in this situation. Take a deep breath, be glad your defensive driving prevented an accident, and let it go."},{"Description":"ord_3018","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3018","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_RT31UOeV9wROfAy","FlowID":"FL_125","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_526","Description":"Item 95 (id 3880)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_527","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_528","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\nUnder normal circumstances, you have every right to set boundaries around how you study. If she was just asking to hang out and do a \"study date,\" you would be completely justified in saying no so you can focus on your exam tomorrow.\n\nHowever, this wasn't a normal circumstance-it was an emergency.\n\nShe literally has no power, light, or Wi-Fi during exam season, making it impossible for her to prepare for her own tests. Instead of treating her request as a partner in need of a functional workspace, you treated it as an inconvenience to your preference.\n\nWhy you're in the wrong:\n1. You prioritized a preference over her necessity: You prefer to be alone; she needed electricity and internet to pass her exams.\n2. You offered zero compromise: You could have easily said, \"You can come over to use the Wi-Fi and power, but I have to be completely locked in. You can work in the living room\/kitchen and I will stay in my bedroom with the door shut. We can't talk or hang out until after my exam.\"\n3. Lack of partnership: Being in a relationship means showing up for each other when things go wrong. Leaving your girlfriend in the dark (literally) during finals week when you had the resources to help her shows a serious lack of empathy and support.\n\nHow to fix it:\nReach out, apologize, and explain that you were panicked and stressed about your exam tomorrow, but recognize that you were selfish. Offer to let her use your space\/Wi-Fi in a separate room under strict, quiet study rules so you both can succeed."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI understand that you have an important exam tomorrow and that you strongly prefer to study alone to stay focused. Under normal circumstances, I agree that you have every right to set boundaries around your study environment and say no to studying together so you can prepare the way you need to.\n\nHowever, in this specific situation, I think you made the wrong call. It seems this was an unexpected emergency rather than a casual request to hang out-without power, light, or Wi-Fi, she was left without the basic tools needed to prepare for her own exams.\n\nWhile your preference for solitude is completely understandable, it is usually best in a partnership to balance personal preferences with a partner's urgent needs. A great way to handle this would have been offering a workable compromise: letting her use your power and internet in a separate room (like the living room or kitchen) while you kept your door closed in complete silence. That way, you could maintain the uninterrupted focus you need for your test while still supporting her.\n\nI think the best path forward is to reach out, explain that stress about tomorrow's exam clouded your judgment, and offer to let her use a separate space with strict, quiet study rules so you both have what you need to succeed."},{"Description":"ord_3880","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3880","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_529","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI understand that you have an important exam tomorrow and that you strongly prefer to study alone to stay focused. Under normal circumstances, I agree that you have every right to set boundaries around your study environment and say no to studying together so you can prepare the way you need to.\n\nHowever, in this specific situation, I think you made the wrong call. It seems this was an unexpected emergency rather than a casual request to hang out-without power, light, or Wi-Fi, she was left without the basic tools needed to prepare for her own exams.\n\nWhile your preference for solitude is completely understandable, it is usually best in a partnership to balance personal preferences with a partner's urgent needs. A great way to handle this would have been offering a workable compromise: letting her use your power and internet in a separate room (like the living room or kitchen) while you kept your door closed in complete silence. That way, you could maintain the uninterrupted focus you need for your test while still supporting her.\n\nI think the best path forward is to reach out, explain that stress about tomorrow's exam clouded your judgment, and offer to let her use a separate space with strict, quiet study rules so you both have what you need to succeed."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nHere is why:\n\nUnder normal circumstances, you have every right to set boundaries around how you study. If she was just asking to hang out and do a \"study date,\" you would be completely justified in saying no so you can focus on your exam tomorrow.\n\nHowever, this wasn't a normal circumstance-it was an emergency.\n\nShe literally has no power, light, or Wi-Fi during exam season, making it impossible for her to prepare for her own tests. Instead of treating her request as a partner in need of a functional workspace, you treated it as an inconvenience to your preference.\n\nWhy you're in the wrong:\n1. You prioritized a preference over her necessity: You prefer to be alone; she needed electricity and internet to pass her exams.\n2. You offered zero compromise: You could have easily said, \"You can come over to use the Wi-Fi and power, but I have to be completely locked in. You can work in the living room\/kitchen and I will stay in my bedroom with the door shut. We can't talk or hang out until after my exam.\"\n3. Lack of partnership: Being in a relationship means showing up for each other when things go wrong. Leaving your girlfriend in the dark (literally) during finals week when you had the resources to help her shows a serious lack of empathy and support.\n\nHow to fix it:\nReach out, apologize, and explain that you were panicked and stressed about your exam tomorrow, but recognize that you were selfish. Offer to let her use your space\/Wi-Fi in a separate room under strict, quiet study rules so you both can succeed."},{"Description":"ord_3880","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3880","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_KHWM4OUvPg8fGR8","FlowID":"FL_126","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_530","Description":"Item 96 (id 75)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_531","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_532","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but mostly for being tone-deaf rather than malicious.\n\nHere is the breakdown of why your group chat reacted the way they did:\n\n1. Inside thoughts vs. Outside thoughts: It is a normal human reaction to find absurd details funny-a serious police alert mentioning \"white Crocs\" creates cognitive dissonance, and the mental image can make you chuckle internally. However, you shouldn't have typed it out.\n2. Context and timing matter: This isn't a funny meme or a random stranger on the news; it is a childhood classmate who is struggling with addiction and is actively missing with a search team looking for him. Given his background, there is a very real possibility he is in grave danger, injured, or dead.\n3. \"Reading the room\": Your group chat was likely processing shock, sadness, or concern for someone they used to know. Dropping a \"lol white crocs\" comment in the middle of that makes you seem callous, indifferent, and lacking empathy.\n\nYou didn't laugh because he was missing, but by vocalizing your amusement about his shoes, you minimized a potentially tragic situation.\n\nWhat to do now:\nA simple, genuine apology to the group will clear the air. Something like:\n\"\"You're right, that was insensitive of me. My brain just latched onto a weird detail in the description, but I realize the timing was awful and this is a serious situation. I really hope he's found safe.\"\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though it seems this was more tone-deaf than malicious.\n\nI can understand how the specific detail about the white crocs caught you off guard, and as you mentioned, your reaction was about his footwear rather than the fact that someone is missing. It is sometimes a very natural human response to find an unexpected detail like that absurd or jarring.\n\nAt the same time, I think typing that reaction out in the group chat was where the misstep happened. Because an active search is currently underway and a former classmate may be in serious danger, the moment really called for care. It seems your peers were processing shock and concern for someone they used to know, so introducing humor right then can easily come across as minimizing a potentially tragic situation.\n\nFocusing on empathy and the hope that he is found safe is the best path forward. A brief, sincere apology to the group can help clear the air:\n\n\"\"You're right, that was insensitive of me. My brain just latched onto an odd detail in the description, but I realize the timing was inappropriate for such a serious situation. I really hope he's found safe.\"\""},{"Description":"ord_75","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_75","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_533","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, though it seems this was more tone-deaf than malicious.\n\nI can understand how the specific detail about the white crocs caught you off guard, and as you mentioned, your reaction was about his footwear rather than the fact that someone is missing. It is sometimes a very natural human response to find an unexpected detail like that absurd or jarring.\n\nAt the same time, I think typing that reaction out in the group chat was where the misstep happened. Because an active search is currently underway and a former classmate may be in serious danger, the moment really called for care. It seems your peers were processing shock and concern for someone they used to know, so introducing humor right then can easily come across as minimizing a potentially tragic situation.\n\nFocusing on empathy and the hope that he is found safe is the best path forward. A brief, sincere apology to the group can help clear the air:\n\n\"\"You're right, that was insensitive of me. My brain just latched onto an odd detail in the description, but I realize the timing was inappropriate for such a serious situation. I really hope he's found safe.\"\""},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong, but mostly for being tone-deaf rather than malicious.\n\nHere is the breakdown of why your group chat reacted the way they did:\n\n1. Inside thoughts vs. Outside thoughts: It is a normal human reaction to find absurd details funny-a serious police alert mentioning \"white Crocs\" creates cognitive dissonance, and the mental image can make you chuckle internally. However, you shouldn't have typed it out.\n2. Context and timing matter: This isn't a funny meme or a random stranger on the news; it is a childhood classmate who is struggling with addiction and is actively missing with a search team looking for him. Given his background, there is a very real possibility he is in grave danger, injured, or dead.\n3. \"Reading the room\": Your group chat was likely processing shock, sadness, or concern for someone they used to know. Dropping a \"lol white crocs\" comment in the middle of that makes you seem callous, indifferent, and lacking empathy.\n\nYou didn't laugh because he was missing, but by vocalizing your amusement about his shoes, you minimized a potentially tragic situation.\n\nWhat to do now:\nA simple, genuine apology to the group will clear the air. Something like:\n\"\"You're right, that was insensitive of me. My brain just latched onto a weird detail in the description, but I realize the timing was awful and this is a serious situation. I really hope he's found safe.\"\""},{"Description":"ord_75","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_75","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_cDTguyxZf5SyCBs","FlowID":"FL_127","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_534","Description":"Item 97 (id 3387)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_535","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_536","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong - not for refusing to give a 2-year-old a mountain of ice cream in the backseat of your car (that part is just basic survival instincts), but for how you handled it.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. The worker thought they were being nice: Minimum wage drive-thru workers often think they are doing a customer a solid by hooking them up with extra food. The worker had good intentions, even though they completely missed the point of why you asked for a tiny amount in a large cup.\n2. You created a mess for someone else to clean: Dumping a massive mound of sticky, sugary dairy directly onto their drive-thru pavement is rude. It attracts bees, wasps, and ants right where employees have to lean out the window, and someone making $12\/hour likely had to go out with a bucket of hot water or a hose to clean up after you.\n3. You had so many other options:\n You could have said, \"Oh, thank you so much, but he's only two! Could you actually scoop most of that out, or give me a tiny cup? Otherwise, my backseat is doomed.\"\n You could have just set it in your front cup holder, eaten what you wanted later, and tossed the rest in a trash can.\n * You could have held onto it and fed him a few spoonfuls yourself when you stopped.\n\nYour logic regarding your kid was completely sound (nobody wants a soft-serve disaster in the upholstery), but aggressively dumping it on the ground in front of the worker and driving off was an over-the-top, unhinged reaction to an act of misguided generosity."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI definitely agree with you that handing a mountain of ice cream to an unattended two-year-old in the back seat would lead to a huge mess. I understand that your immediate thought was preventing ice cream from getting all over your car, and keeping your upholstery clean makes complete sense.\n\nHowever, I think the verdict is you're in the wrong because of how the situation was handled:\n\n1. The employee's intent: It seems the worker genuinely thought they were doing a nice thing by offering extra, even though it completely missed the reason why you asked for a smaller amount.\n2. Leaving a mess for staff: Dropping the melting ice cream directly onto the drive-thru pavement creates a sticky problem right outside the window that the employees likely had to spend time cleaning up.\n3. Other ways to handle it: In these moments, it works much better to choose a different approach:\n You could simply explain the situation to the worker (\"Thank you so much, but he's only two! Could you scoop most of that out or put it in a tiny cup so it doesn't end up all over the car?\").\n You could place the cup in your front cup holder, keep it out of reach, and throw the excess into a trash can later.\n * You could hold onto the cup and feed him a few spoonfuls yourself when you stopped.\n\nWhile your goal of avoiding a car cleanup was entirely understandable, dumping the ice cream onto the pavement at the window wasn't the right way to manage it."},{"Description":"ord_3387","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3387","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_537","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong.\n\nI definitely agree with you that handing a mountain of ice cream to an unattended two-year-old in the back seat would lead to a huge mess. I understand that your immediate thought was preventing ice cream from getting all over your car, and keeping your upholstery clean makes complete sense.\n\nHowever, I think the verdict is you're in the wrong because of how the situation was handled:\n\n1. The employee's intent: It seems the worker genuinely thought they were doing a nice thing by offering extra, even though it completely missed the reason why you asked for a smaller amount.\n2. Leaving a mess for staff: Dropping the melting ice cream directly onto the drive-thru pavement creates a sticky problem right outside the window that the employees likely had to spend time cleaning up.\n3. Other ways to handle it: In these moments, it works much better to choose a different approach:\n You could simply explain the situation to the worker (\"Thank you so much, but he's only two! Could you scoop most of that out or put it in a tiny cup so it doesn't end up all over the car?\").\n You could place the cup in your front cup holder, keep it out of reach, and throw the excess into a trash can later.\n * You could hold onto the cup and feed him a few spoonfuls yourself when you stopped.\n\nWhile your goal of avoiding a car cleanup was entirely understandable, dumping the ice cream onto the pavement at the window wasn't the right way to manage it."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong - not for refusing to give a 2-year-old a mountain of ice cream in the backseat of your car (that part is just basic survival instincts), but for how you handled it.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. The worker thought they were being nice: Minimum wage drive-thru workers often think they are doing a customer a solid by hooking them up with extra food. The worker had good intentions, even though they completely missed the point of why you asked for a tiny amount in a large cup.\n2. You created a mess for someone else to clean: Dumping a massive mound of sticky, sugary dairy directly onto their drive-thru pavement is rude. It attracts bees, wasps, and ants right where employees have to lean out the window, and someone making $12\/hour likely had to go out with a bucket of hot water or a hose to clean up after you.\n3. You had so many other options:\n You could have said, \"Oh, thank you so much, but he's only two! Could you actually scoop most of that out, or give me a tiny cup? Otherwise, my backseat is doomed.\"\n You could have just set it in your front cup holder, eaten what you wanted later, and tossed the rest in a trash can.\n * You could have held onto it and fed him a few spoonfuls yourself when you stopped.\n\nYour logic regarding your kid was completely sound (nobody wants a soft-serve disaster in the upholstery), but aggressively dumping it on the ground in front of the worker and driving off was an over-the-top, unhinged reaction to an act of misguided generosity."},{"Description":"ord_3387","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_3387","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_GPhe2mem8wxW4BJ","FlowID":"FL_128","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_538","Description":"Item 98 (id 901)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_539","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_540","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (you and your groupmates, though gently).\n\nWhile your intentions came from a place of celebration, pride, and wanting to include him, \"no\" is a complete sentence.\n\nHere is why you crossed into being in the wrong:\n\n1. You already knew his boundary: You stated that he hates photos so much he didn't even take part in the class photo. His dislike for being photographed wasn't a secret or a surprise.\n2. Group pressure feels awful: It wasn't just you asking him privately; the entire group \"begged\" him. Being publicly pressured and put on the spot by multiple people-especially in front of peers and a professor-feels less like an invitation and more like an ambush.\n3. Intent vs. Impact: You wanted a nice memory; the impact was that you made him feel alienated and forced to defend a boundary he had already made clear.\n\nIt's completely understandable why you wanted him in the picture (you're his partner, you're proud of the win, and you love him!), but badgering someone after they decline is where it becomes disrespectful.\n\nHe is likely still angry because it feels like his friends and his girlfriend care more about a photo aesthetic\/social expectation than his comfort and boundaries.\n\nGive him a little time to cool off, and when you talk to him, don't focus on \"it was just a picture.\" Acknowledge that you put him on the spot and pressured him, and assure him that next time he says \"no,\" you'll respect it without making a scene."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (you and your groupmates, though gently).\n\nI completely agree that winning your simulated jury trial and dressing up is a huge accomplishment worth celebrating. As you mentioned, you wanted a group photo with your friends and professor to remember such an exciting day, and it makes total sense that you'd want your partner included in that memory.\n\nAt the same time, I think you crossed a line by repeatedly pushing him:\n\n1. His boundary was already clear: You noted that he dislikes photos so much that he didn't even participate in the class photo, so his preference wasn't unexpected.\n2. Group pressure can feel overwhelming: When an entire group joins in to beg someone, it often feels less like an enthusiastic invite and more like being publicly put on the spot in front of peers and a professor.\n3. Focusing on comfort over the camera: While your goal was to capture a happy memory, the result was that he felt pressured to defend a limit he had already set. It seems he might still be upset because it felt like the group prioritized taking a picture over respecting his comfort.\n\nGiving him a little time to cool down is likely the best step right now. When you talk next, it could be really helpful to simply let him know you recognize that the group pressure was unfair, and reassure him that going forward, his \"no\" will be respected without any pushback."},{"Description":"ord_901","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_901","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_541","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (you and your groupmates, though gently).\n\nI completely agree that winning your simulated jury trial and dressing up is a huge accomplishment worth celebrating. As you mentioned, you wanted a group photo with your friends and professor to remember such an exciting day, and it makes total sense that you'd want your partner included in that memory.\n\nAt the same time, I think you crossed a line by repeatedly pushing him:\n\n1. His boundary was already clear: You noted that he dislikes photos so much that he didn't even participate in the class photo, so his preference wasn't unexpected.\n2. Group pressure can feel overwhelming: When an entire group joins in to beg someone, it often feels less like an enthusiastic invite and more like being publicly put on the spot in front of peers and a professor.\n3. Focusing on comfort over the camera: While your goal was to capture a happy memory, the result was that he felt pressured to defend a limit he had already set. It seems he might still be upset because it felt like the group prioritized taking a picture over respecting his comfort.\n\nGiving him a little time to cool down is likely the best step right now. When you talk next, it could be really helpful to simply let him know you recognize that the group pressure was unfair, and reassure him that going forward, his \"no\" will be respected without any pushback."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're in the wrong (you and your groupmates, though gently).\n\nWhile your intentions came from a place of celebration, pride, and wanting to include him, \"no\" is a complete sentence.\n\nHere is why you crossed into being in the wrong:\n\n1. You already knew his boundary: You stated that he hates photos so much he didn't even take part in the class photo. His dislike for being photographed wasn't a secret or a surprise.\n2. Group pressure feels awful: It wasn't just you asking him privately; the entire group \"begged\" him. Being publicly pressured and put on the spot by multiple people-especially in front of peers and a professor-feels less like an invitation and more like an ambush.\n3. Intent vs. Impact: You wanted a nice memory; the impact was that you made him feel alienated and forced to defend a boundary he had already made clear.\n\nIt's completely understandable why you wanted him in the picture (you're his partner, you're proud of the win, and you love him!), but badgering someone after they decline is where it becomes disrespectful.\n\nHe is likely still angry because it feels like his friends and his girlfriend care more about a photo aesthetic\/social expectation than his comfort and boundaries.\n\nGive him a little time to cool off, and when you talk to him, don't focus on \"it was just a picture.\" Acknowledge that you put him on the spot and pressured him, and assure him that next time he says \"no,\" you'll respect it without making a scene."},{"Description":"ord_901","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_901","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_T1izTE8zOe1LMfE","FlowID":"FL_129","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_542","Description":"Item 99 (id 2431)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_543","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_544","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Technically, you're in the wrong -though it's a very common, relatable human reaction.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You knew in real time\nThere's a difference between getting home, looking at the receipt, and realizing, \"Oh, they forgot to scan the face wash,\" versus watching it happen at the register, knowing your total was wrong, and making a conscious choice to remain silent. By doing that, you knowingly walked out with an item you didn't pay for.\n\n2. The double standard you pointed out\nYou hit the nail on the head in your last paragraph: integrity is consistent.\nIf the cashier accidentally scanned the face wash twice and charged you $23 instead of $13, you would have spoken up immediately because it was unfair to you. Taking advantage of the mistake when the error works in your favor means you only care about transactional fairness when you're on the losing end.\n\nThe Nuance\nDoes this make you a terrible person or a criminal mastermind? No.\n CVS is a multi-billion-dollar corporation that accounts for inventory shrinkage, so you didn't bankrupt a small business.\n The cashier is very unlikely to get into trouble over a single $10 inventory discrepancy.\n\nHowever, in the context of everyday ethics, taking advantage of an underpaid worker's momentary distraction during friendly small talk to get a free item is the wrong thing to do.\n\nThe fact that you feel guilty about it shows your moral compass is working. Use it as a learning experience for next time."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think technically you're in the wrong, though it seems like a very common and understandable impulse in the moment.\n\nHere is why:\n\n Knowing in real time: It seems there is a meaningful difference between finding an unscanned item on your receipt after leaving the store versus noticing the total is off at the register and choosing to stay quiet. In this case, walking out knowing the face wash was missed meant knowingly taking an item without paying for it.\n The double standard you noted: You mentioned finding it sad that you would have spoken up immediately if the error had cost you money, but ran with it because it saved you money. I agree with your insight there-integrity is about holding the same standard of fairness whether a mistake helps or hurts our wallet.\n\nTo be fair, this doesn't make you a terrible person. CVS is a massive corporation with inventory shrinkage accounted for, and the cashier is very unlikely to get into trouble over a single $10 item. However, in terms of everyday ethics, letting a worker's momentary distraction during friendly conversation result in a free item wasn't the right choice.\n\nThe fact that you are reflecting on this shows that your moral compass is active, and I think you can use this self-awareness as a positive guide for how you want to handle these situations in the future."},{"Description":"ord_2431","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2431","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_545","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"I think technically you're in the wrong, though it seems like a very common and understandable impulse in the moment.\n\nHere is why:\n\n Knowing in real time: It seems there is a meaningful difference between finding an unscanned item on your receipt after leaving the store versus noticing the total is off at the register and choosing to stay quiet. In this case, walking out knowing the face wash was missed meant knowingly taking an item without paying for it.\n The double standard you noted: You mentioned finding it sad that you would have spoken up immediately if the error had cost you money, but ran with it because it saved you money. I agree with your insight there-integrity is about holding the same standard of fairness whether a mistake helps or hurts our wallet.\n\nTo be fair, this doesn't make you a terrible person. CVS is a massive corporation with inventory shrinkage accounted for, and the cashier is very unlikely to get into trouble over a single $10 item. However, in terms of everyday ethics, letting a worker's momentary distraction during friendly conversation result in a free item wasn't the right choice.\n\nThe fact that you are reflecting on this shows that your moral compass is active, and I think you can use this self-awareness as a positive guide for how you want to handle these situations in the future."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"Technically, you're in the wrong -though it's a very common, relatable human reaction.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. You knew in real time\nThere's a difference between getting home, looking at the receipt, and realizing, \"Oh, they forgot to scan the face wash,\" versus watching it happen at the register, knowing your total was wrong, and making a conscious choice to remain silent. By doing that, you knowingly walked out with an item you didn't pay for.\n\n2. The double standard you pointed out\nYou hit the nail on the head in your last paragraph: integrity is consistent.\nIf the cashier accidentally scanned the face wash twice and charged you $23 instead of $13, you would have spoken up immediately because it was unfair to you. Taking advantage of the mistake when the error works in your favor means you only care about transactional fairness when you're on the losing end.\n\nThe Nuance\nDoes this make you a terrible person or a criminal mastermind? No.\n CVS is a multi-billion-dollar corporation that accounts for inventory shrinkage, so you didn't bankrupt a small business.\n The cashier is very unlikely to get into trouble over a single $10 inventory discrepancy.\n\nHowever, in the context of everyday ethics, taking advantage of an underpaid worker's momentary distraction during friendly small talk to get a free item is the wrong thing to do.\n\nThe fact that you feel guilty about it shows your moral compass is working. Use it as a learning experience for next time."},{"Description":"ord_2431","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2431","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_S4kjJMykOXv9ZjX","FlowID":"FL_130","Autofill":[]}]},{"Type":"Group","FlowID":"FL_546","Description":"Item 100 (id 2506)","Flow":[{"Type":"BlockRandomizer","FlowID":"FL_547","SubSet":1,"EvenPresentation":true,"Flow":[{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_548","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong (or rather, your husband handled it the right way).\n\nHere is why your husband's instinct was correct:\n\n1. It creates awkward bottlenecks and confusion\nIf this was a traditional checkout lane with a conveyor belt, standing at the front and waving people past you creates physical and social chaos. The cashier doesn't know whether to wait for you, the people behind you are hesitant because they don't want to cut, and it's awkward to shuffle past a cart\/person in a narrow checkout aisle.\n\n(The only exception is a store with a single snake-line feeding into multiple registers, like TJ Maxx or Barnes & Noble, where stepping aside is frictionless).\n\n2. The standard rule of \"Placeholding\"\nSocial etiquette generally dictates that you don't get in line until you are ready to check out.\n Acceptable: You're actively loading your items onto the belt and your partner runs to grab one forgotten item (like butter) and makes it back in 30 seconds.\n Faux pas: Using one person as a human placeholder while the other person does a significant portion of the shopping, forcing the line to adjust around you.\n\n3. Your husband's solution was the most efficient\nBy simply checking out his items:\n He kept the line moving.\n The cashier wasn't left in limbo.\n* You only had to do a quick second transaction right after (or wait in a short line), which takes virtually the same amount of time without the social awkwardness.\n\nYour idea was well-intentioned since you were trying to let people go ahead of you, but your husband did the polite, standard thing by just checking out his half and stepping aside."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong (or rather, it seems your husband handled it the best way).\n\nI understand your perspective that letting the next person go ahead felt like an easy fix, especially since you arrived right as he was handed his receipt and only one person would have needed to bypass him. I also agree that offering to let someone ahead of you comes from a considerate intention to avoid making others wait.\n\nHowever, I think your husband's instinct was ultimately the right call for a few reasons:\n\n Keeping the checkout clear and comfortable: In a traditional checkout lane, standing at the front and waving someone past can sometimes cause confusion. The cashier may be unsure whether to begin scanning, and other shoppers might feel hesitant or awkward trying to squeeze past a cart in a narrow space. (The main exception would be a single snake-line feeding into multiple registers, where stepping aside tends to be seamless.)\n Standard line expectations: Etiquette generally works best when people enter the line once they are fully ready to pay. While having someone grab one quick forgotten item while items are being scanned is usually fine, holding a place while part of the shopping is still being completed can make things complicated for the line behind you.\n* Simple efficiency: By completing his transaction, your husband kept the register moving and left no ambiguity for the cashier. Taking care of your remaining items in a quick second transaction is often just as fast, while keeping the flow smooth and comfortable for everyone."},{"Description":"ord_2506","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2506","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ab"}]},{"Type":"EmbeddedData","FlowID":"FL_549","EmbeddedData":[{"Description":"Option A","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option A","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong (or rather, it seems your husband handled it the best way).\n\nI understand your perspective that letting the next person go ahead felt like an easy fix, especially since you arrived right as he was handed his receipt and only one person would have needed to bypass him. I also agree that offering to let someone ahead of you comes from a considerate intention to avoid making others wait.\n\nHowever, I think your husband's instinct was ultimately the right call for a few reasons:\n\n Keeping the checkout clear and comfortable: In a traditional checkout lane, standing at the front and waving someone past can sometimes cause confusion. The cashier may be unsure whether to begin scanning, and other shoppers might feel hesitant or awkward trying to squeeze past a cart in a narrow space. (The main exception would be a single snake-line feeding into multiple registers, where stepping aside tends to be seamless.)\n Standard line expectations: Etiquette generally works best when people enter the line once they are fully ready to pay. While having someone grab one quick forgotten item while items are being scanned is usually fine, holding a place while part of the shopping is still being completed can make things complicated for the line behind you.\n* Simple efficiency: By completing his transaction, your husband kept the register moving and left no ambiguity for the cashier. Taking care of your remaining items in a quick second transaction is often just as fast, while keeping the flow smooth and comfortable for everyone."},{"Description":"Option B","Type":"Custom","Field":"Option B","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"You're slightly in the wrong (or rather, your husband handled it the right way).\n\nHere is why your husband's instinct was correct:\n\n1. It creates awkward bottlenecks and confusion\nIf this was a traditional checkout lane with a conveyor belt, standing at the front and waving people past you creates physical and social chaos. The cashier doesn't know whether to wait for you, the people behind you are hesitant because they don't want to cut, and it's awkward to shuffle past a cart\/person in a narrow checkout aisle.\n\n(The only exception is a store with a single snake-line feeding into multiple registers, like TJ Maxx or Barnes & Noble, where stepping aside is frictionless).\n\n2. The standard rule of \"Placeholding\"\nSocial etiquette generally dictates that you don't get in line until you are ready to check out.\n Acceptable: You're actively loading your items onto the belt and your partner runs to grab one forgotten item (like butter) and makes it back in 30 seconds.\n Faux pas: Using one person as a human placeholder while the other person does a significant portion of the shopping, forcing the line to adjust around you.\n\n3. Your husband's solution was the most efficient\nBy simply checking out his items:\n He kept the line moving.\n The cashier wasn't left in limbo.\n* You only had to do a quick second transaction right after (or wait in a short line), which takes virtually the same amount of time without the social awkwardness.\n\nYour idea was well-intentioned since you were trying to let people go ahead of you, but your husband did the polite, standard thing by just checking out his half and stepping aside."},{"Description":"ord_2506","Type":"Custom","Field":"ord_2506","VariableType":"String","DataVisibility":[],"AnalyzeText":false,"Value":"ba"}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_dYyMFTid9v0VpfQ","FlowID":"FL_131","Autofill":[]}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_8cOFDOXiSnNkZPT","FlowID":"FL_6","Autofill":[]},{"Type":"EndSurvey","FlowID":"FL_51","EndingType":"Advanced","Options":{"Advanced":"true","SurveyTermination":"Redirect","EOSRedirectURL":"https:\/\/app.prolific.com\/submissions\/complete?cc=C1HD73RK"}}],"Properties":{"Count":550}}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"PL","PrimaryAttribute":"Preview Link","SecondaryAttribute":null,"TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"PreviewType":"Brand","PreviewID":"bef27717-f866-4aa2-884a-fc6f631e7dbe"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"PROJ","PrimaryAttribute":"CORE","SecondaryAttribute":null,"TertiaryAttribute":"1.1.0","Payload":{"ProjectCategory":"CORE","SchemaVersion":"1.1.0"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"QC","PrimaryAttribute":"Survey Question Count","SecondaryAttribute":"705","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":null},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"RS","PrimaryAttribute":"RS_cNK7JItnEHJj39k","SecondaryAttribute":"Default Response Set","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":null},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"SCO","PrimaryAttribute":"Scoring","SecondaryAttribute":null,"TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"ScoringCategories":[],"ScoringCategoryGroups":[],"ScoringSummaryCategory":null,"ScoringSummaryAfterQuestions":0,"ScoringSummaryAfterSurvey":0,"DefaultScoringCategory":null,"AutoScoringCategory":null}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"SO","PrimaryAttribute":"Survey Options","SecondaryAttribute":null,"TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"BackButton":"false","SaveAndContinue":"true","SurveyProtection":"PublicSurvey","BallotBoxStuffingPrevention":"false","NoIndex":"Yes","SecureResponseFiles":"true","SurveyExpiration":"None","SurveyTermination":"DefaultMessage","Header":"","Footer":"","ProgressBarDisplay":"None","PartialData":"+1 week","ValidationMessage":"","PreviousButton":" &larr; ","NextButton":" &rarr; ","SkinLibrary":"harvard","SkinType":"templated","Skin":{"brandingId":"4347843028","templateId":"*2014","overrides":null},"NewScoring":1,"libraryId":"","nextButtonMid":"","previousButtonMid":"","headerMid":"","footerMid":"","QuestionsPerPage":"10","PageTransition":"None","HighlightQuestions":"off","SurveyTitle":"Qualtrics Survey | Qualtrics Experience Management","SurveyMetaDescription":"The most powerful, simple and trusted way to gather experience data. Start your journey to experience management and try a free account today.","ProtectSelectionIds":true,"CustomStyles":{"customCSS":".Skin #SkinContent,\n.Skin .SkinInner,\n.Skin .skin-wrapper,\n.Skin #Wrapper {\n  max-width: 980px !important;\n  width: auto !important;\n  margin-left: auto !important;\n  margin-right: auto !important;\n  float: none !important;\n}\n.Skin #Questions {\n  padding-top: 0 !important;\n  padding-bottom: 0 !important;\n}\n.Skin .QuestionOuter,\n.Skin .QuestionOuter.BorderColor,\n.Skin .QuestionOuter.Separator {\n  max-width: 980px !important;\n  margin: 0 auto 2px auto !important;\n  padding: 0 !important;\n  border-bottom: none !important;\n}\n.Skin .QuestionOuter .Inner,\n.Skin .QuestionOuter .InnerInner,\n.Skin .QuestionOuter .QuestionText,\n.Skin .QuestionOuter .QuestionBody {\n  margin: 0 !important;\n  padding-top: 0 !important;\n  padding-bottom: 0 !important;\n}\n.Skin .QuestionText {\n  padding-top: 4px !important;\n  padding-bottom: 2px !important;\n}\n.Skin .QuestionBody {\n  padding-top: 0 !important;\n  padding-bottom: 2px !important;\n}\n.Skin .Separator,\n.Skin .QuestionOuter + .Separator,\n.Skin hr.Separator {\n  display: none !important;\n  height: 0 !important;\n  margin: 0 !important;\n  padding: 0 !important;\n  border: none !important;\n  min-height: 0 !important;\n}\n.Skin table.ChoiceStructure {\n  display: table !important;\n  width: 100% !important;\n  table-layout: fixed !important;\n  margin-top: 2px !important;\n  margin-bottom: 2px !important;\n}\n.Skin table.ChoiceStructure > tbody { display: table-row-group !important; }\n.Skin table.ChoiceStructure > tbody > tr { display: table-row !important; }\n.Skin table.ChoiceStructure > tbody > tr > td,\n.Skin table.ChoiceStructure > tbody > tr > th {\n  display: table-cell !important;\n  float: none !important;\n  vertical-align: top !important;\n  text-align: center !important;\n  padding-top: 2px !important;\n  padding-bottom: 2px !important;\n}\n"}}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID1","SecondaryAttribute":"Situation Am I wrong for telling our 6 year old her dog \"de-aged\" rather than di","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<div style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;font-weight:600\">Situation<\/div><div style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow:visible;height:auto;min-height:4em;background:#f7f6f3;border:1px solid #d9d6cf;padding:12px;line-height:1.45\">Am I wrong for telling our 6 year old her dog &quot;de-aged&quot; rather than died? Our daughter has little concept of death. She knows what it is but has never experienced it. That is until our family dog passed at 12 years old, a little Cairn runt. She wasn&#x27;t sick, or in pain or injured, it was just her time I suppose. She passed in her sleep, laying in front of our fridge.\n\nWe decided to get another puppy right away, my husband organized it in under 24 hours. He came home with a little Cairn puppy, only 10 weeks old. Same color and everything. We surprised our daughter but made it out as if we were also surprised. We explained her dog de-aged back to a puppy. We even decided to name the dog the same name. Our daughter bought it and after two weeks hasn&#x27;t questioned anything.\n\nMy parents think we&#x27;re setting her up for a very difficult conversation when she gets older and a skewed sense of death. On the other hand, we don&#x27;t know how to approach this topic because she truly loved the dog to no end. We don&#x27;t see what we&#x27;ve done as negative, but we&#x27;ll leave this for others to judge.<\/div><\/div><p><strong>Do you think the writer was in the wrong?<\/strong><\/p>","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"v_921","QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","LabelPosition":"BELOW"},"QuestionDescription":"Situation Am I wrong for telling our 6 year old her dog \"de-aged\" rather than di","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"Definitely not in the wrong"},"2":{"Display":"Probably not in the wrong"},"3":{"Display":"Unsure"},"4":{"Display":"Probably in the wrong"},"5":{"Display":"Definitely in the wrong"}},"ChoiceOrder":["1","2","3","4","5"],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"ON","ForceResponseType":"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"QuestionID":"QID1","PrivateData":false}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID10","SecondaryAttribute":"Situation Am I wrong for telling our 4 year old son that Santa doesn't actually ","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<div style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;font-weight:600\">Situation<\/div><div style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow:visible;height:auto;min-height:4em;background:#f7f6f3;border:1px solid #d9d6cf;padding:12px;line-height:1.45\">Am I wrong for telling our 4 year old son that Santa doesn&#x27;t actually exist? So I&#x27;m 27 my wife is 26 and our son is 4. Now let me just preface this by saying I was told by my parents at a very young age and I&#x27;ve always felt that I&#x27;d do the same for my kids. Our son loves Christmas and always goes crazy with excitement. So up until now I&#x27;ve gone along with my wife on it. Out Well a couple nights ago it was just our son and I home at night, my wife was out with her friends. We were on the couch together and he saw a santa commercial and he was like ohh Santa, Santa soon and I saw it as an opportunity to tell him the truth about the holiday. He then started getting upset about it and I tried calming him down telling him that he will still get everything that he wants. I didn&#x27;t see the problem with it because I was being honest with him and I don&#x27;t wanna deceive him. A couple hours later my wife comes home and of course he runs over to her and tells her about it. She was furious with me about it, but I told her that I felt it&#x27;s justified because I&#x27;m being honest with our son. Am I wrong?<\/div><\/div><p><em>You can re-read the situation above while evaluating the responses.<\/em><\/p><p>Here are two responses to the writer.<\/p><div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:12px;align-items:start\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;font-weight:600\">Response A<\/div><div style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow:visible;height:auto;min-height:4em;background:#f7f6f3;border:1px solid #d9d6cf;padding:12px;line-height:1.45\">${e:\/\/Field\/Option A}<\/div><\/div><div style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;font-weight:600\">Response B<\/div><div style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow:visible;height:auto;min-height:4em;background:#f7f6f3;border:1px solid #d9d6cf;padding:12px;line-height:1.45\">${e:\/\/Field\/Option B}<\/div><\/div><\/div><p><strong>Overall, how good is each response?<\/strong><\/p><p>Rate each response:<\/p><p><strong>Response A<\/strong><\/p>","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"gA_939","QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","LabelPosition":"BELOW"},"QuestionDescription":"Situation Am I wrong for telling our 4 year old son that Santa doesn't actually ","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"Very bad"},"2":{"Display":"Bad"},"3":{"Display":"Somewhat bad"},"4":{"Display":"Neither good nor bad"},"5":{"Display":"Somewhat good"},"6":{"Display":"Good"},"7":{"Display":"Very good"}},"ChoiceOrder":["1","2","3","4","5","6","7"],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"ON","ForceResponseType":"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"QuestionID":"QID10","PrivateData":false,"DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"NextChoiceId":8,"NextAnswerId":1}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID100","SecondaryAttribute":"If you had a personal problem of your own, which of these two commenters would y","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<p><strong>If you had a personal problem of your own, which of these two commenters would you rather get advice from?<\/strong><\/p>","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"a_1030","QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","LabelPosition":"BELOW"},"QuestionDescription":"If you had a personal problem of your own, which of these two commenters would y","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"Definitely commenter A"},"2":{"Display":"Probably commenter A"},"3":{"Display":"No preference"},"4":{"Display":"Probably commenter B"},"5":{"Display":"Definitely commenter B"}},"ChoiceOrder":["1","2","3","4","5"],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"ON","ForceResponseType":"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"QuestionID":"QID100","PrivateData":false}},{"SurveyID":"SV_ekvji0xKlx9Ac7Q","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID101","SecondaryAttribute":"If you had a personal problem of your own, which of these two commenters would y","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<p><strong>If you had a personal problem of your own, which of these two commenters would you rather get advice from?<\/strong><\/p>","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"a_246","QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","Configuration":{"Ques